// ============================================================================
// CE-INDEX — WHERE THINGS ARE.
// Not a changelog, not a design document, not a second copy of the comments.
// Every decision is written at the code that implements it and tagged [TOKEN];
// this block maps subject -> token -> section, and states a rule directly only
// when that rule belongs to no single site. If you want to know WHY, grep the
// token. If you are about to add history here, add it to the code instead.
// ============================================================================
//
// ── SECTION MAP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   1    Types                       2    Constants, seed catalogs, EVT_* registry
//   3    Subsystems: 3a logging  3b diagnostics/slash  3c budget + engine locks
//        3d classic wizard AND selftest  3e WIZLIVE  3f CharChat  3g gen diags
//        3h generation execution (direct + yoink)  3i script state  3j ctx census
//        3k helper mesh -- generation delegation to companion scripts [HELP-MESH]
//   3k SITS BETWEEN 3h AND 3i IN THE FILE, beside the router it hooks into rather than after
//   3j. This list is a subject map, not a reading order -- 3d already appears in two places.
//   4    Utilities                   4b   Native modal helpers
//   5    Characters   6 Entities   7 Config   8 Party/NPC web   9 Images
//   9b   Per-character image slots [IMG-SLOTS]
//   7b   State core [SEC:STATE] — includes the 'event' scope and band terminals [EVENT]
//   7c   Clock [SEC:CLOCK]
//   7d   Schedules [SEC:SCHED] — characters AND location atmosphere, one shape [ATMO]
//   10   Lorebook helpers   10b Story journal   10c Character journal
//   10d  Cast, World and the two Species lorebooks (engine-composed; ordered last via
//        lorebookEntryOrder, whose only writer in this file is onBeforeContextBuild)
//        [D-07] [SPEC-LORE]
//   12   Snapshots                   13   Context injection [SEC:INJECTION]
//   15   Dynamic engine              16   Image prompts   16b Story image widgets
//   18   Bridge messaging            18b  Lorebook world-pack
//   19   Scene presence, location, equip [SEC:TRAVEL] — pricing is pure and
//        lives here; the Map (32) is its only surface
//   20   Shared entity/config/import ops
//   26   Hook registrations          27   UI registration & startup
//   28   UI foundation (jsx kit)
//   29 Scene  30 Profile  31 Story (Journal + Threads)  31b Icons
//   32   Map -- four tabs: Map, Entities, Species [SPEC-LIST], Time
//   33 Chat  34 Settings
//   35 Images  35b Image edit  36 Entity  37 Wizard
//   38   Actions panel — a scriptPanel, NOT a shell route [SEC:ACTIONS]
//   39   World pipeline: charter store, world question set, prompt substrate
//        [CHARTER] [P-SUBSTRATE] [WORLD-PICK] [WORLD-HELPER]
//   40   Build surface (jsx) — the pipeline's one route [BUILD-ONE-ROUTE]
//   41   Chat dock (kit) [CHAT-DOCK] — the in-menu chat surface every chat moves onto
//   42   Build chats [BUILD-CHAT] — one transcript per scope, routed, never WIZLIVE's path;
//        the world, name and seed passes that read them live here too
//   14 is a retired marker. 11, 17 and 21-25 ARE RETIRED NUMBERS and are never
//   reused, so every "SECTION N" reference in this file keeps its meaning.
//
// ── FILE-WIDE RULES (each belongs to no single site) ────────────────────────
// ONE PICKER. CE_PICKER_DEFS + _cePickerSurface (28). Anything that chooses one
//   thing out of a list is a def in that table. Do not write a second chooser.
//   TWO HOSTS, ONE SURFACE [PANEL-PICK], because ceRoot is not mounted when 38 is.
//   A third host adds a slot, never a second surface.
// ONE COLLAPSIBLE PAIR. ceAccordion and ceExpandRow are kit, not profile-local;
//   they are declared in 30 for historical reasons only.
// A CONTAINER MOUNTED OUTSIDE THE SHELL CALLS ceStoresInit FIRST
//   [PANEL-SUBSTRATE]. ceUiStore holds the busy latch, so ceAction — and with it
//   every ceBtn, ceToggle, ceSelect, ceField and ceStepperRow — silently no-ops
//   without it. The symptom is a fully drawn surface where nothing works.
// EVERY STORE JOINS ceOpenScene'S INIT LIST [STORE-BEFORE-HOOK] or the surface
//   renders blank with nothing on the console.
// A LIST WHOSE LENGTH MOVES IS ALL-KEYED OR DOES NOT MOVE [H-01]. Push null for
//   the empty case. Preact matches mixed-key lists partly by position, and a
//   rebuilt div loses bridge-painted children with no error and no repaint.
//   Tab bodies carry their own key [TABBODY-RECYCLE] or the subtree is recycled
//   across tabs and identical shapes carry each other's values.
// THE PROTOCOL IS CLOSED. Every name in the SECTION 2 EVT_* registry has a
//   partner case in the bridge, both directions, verified rather than assumed.
//   ADD BOTH HALVES IN THE SAME PATCH OR ADD NEITHER — a broadcast with no case
//   is not inert; it builds its payload, ships it, and lands on the bridge's
//   default:. [CHARCHAT-NATIVE-SEND] Add new verbs as new 'op' values.
// THE BRIDGE HAS NO UI. It does image generation, image storage and display, the
//   generation indicator, and transport. Every author-facing surface is jsx in
//   28-38. A feature that wants a widget builds it here, not there.
// A SURFACE IS NOT ALWAYS A ROUTE. 29-37 are screens inside one full-size modal
//   from CE_SHELL_ROUTES; 38 is a scriptPanel below the editor and is the only one,
//   because it is the only surface whose job needs NAI's story field and Send button
//   visible beside it. Same primitives, different frame.
// SURFACES COMPOSE, THEY DO NOT STYLE [SS-BOX]. The twelve primitives in 28 replace
//   hand-rolled h('div',{style}): children as an ARRAY, opacity by ROLE, `key` read in
//   _ceBoxProps and nowhere else. What remains hand-rolled mixes a genuinely local
//   decision into layout and is not a backlog.
// A FLAG THAT GRANTS A CAPABILITY IS THE MEMBERSHIP TEST [F-29]. Build the
//   enumerator FROM the flag. A hand-kept id list beside it is a second source
//   of truth that silently wins.
// ONE DERIVATION PER ADDRESS. ceStakeForMove, ceDefaultResolutionId,
//   ceStandingLadderFor, ceEntityCatalog and _ceStakeRef are read through, never
//   around. Two derivations is how a surface comes to promise one thing and
//   charge another.
// THE MODEL NEVER MOVES DISCRETE STATE [D-21, D-22]. Every transition is
//   user-declared or rule-fired, classifications default to no-change, and a
//   value reaches context only through its band.
// A DECLARED ATTACHMENT NOTHING READS IS A PARKED FIELD; A SEEDED ONE IS A
//   PROMISE [F-20]. Do not seed an unread attachment, and do not add a knob
//   with no door — that is why the day's length (7c) and the spend rate are
//   constants rather than record fields.
// A DOC COMMENT WITH NO CODE UNDER IT ANSWERS GREP AND READS AS A CONTRACT
//   [I-05]. ceMeter's specification sat above ceCard for versions with no
//   function under it. Check for the function, not the paragraph.
// GREP BEFORE MINTING a message name, storage key, constant or function, and before
//   concluding a feature was never built [CE-STRIP].
// WHEN A RECORD'S TYPE MIGRATES, THE SURFACE THAT EXISTED FOR THE OLD TYPE IS
//   PART OF THE MIGRATION [F-29]. F-28 changed the record and left the screen.
// TABS ARE CONTENT THAT STAYS, LAUNCHERS ARE DOORS THAT LEAVE. A tab whose body
//   is a single button to somewhere else is a duplicate screen.
//
// ── GREP DIRECTORY: subject -> token -> what the rule is ───────────────────
// ONE LINE PER TOKEN, two where a rule genuinely needs it. The reasoning is at the code
// under that token; a line here that grows into an explanation is a second copy of one, and
// the copy goes stale on its own schedule.
// THERE ARE NO SECTION NUMBERS HERE. GREP IS THE LOCATOR. They were carried until an audit
// compared each entry's trailing digits against where its tag actually sits and found 76 of
// 174 pointing at a section the token was not in -- ATT-TIER claimed three it had never been
// in, every HEX-* entry claimed 32 for tags living in 32c. A number that has to be right and
// is not is worse than no number, and `grep '\[TOKEN\]'` cannot drift.
// EVERY TOKEN IS GREPPABLE AT A SITE except those marked ~, which record a decision that was
// taken and NOT built and so have no code to sit at. A token that is neither greppable nor
// marked is rot: tag it or delete the line.
// STATE, ROLLS, STAKES
//   D-19 D-21 D-23  one primitive (bounded integer), its projection, its verbs
//   F-17   scales are band-sized; pressure is the only non-state target
//   F-14   a stake is a default first, a choice second; the ledger keeps the ref
//          and the REAL delta
//   F-14u  reversal is one-use and reverses only stored deltas
//   STAKE-ADDR  a stake ref stores its owner and resolves nothing later, because the ledger
//          outlives the roll
//   MISS-NAMED  a cell that cannot be written is reported with its own reason, never skipped
//   F-15c  zero dice is a no-roll resolution, not a second subsystem
//   F-15d  the arm survives a roll; the spend does not
//   F-20   a bonus is bought, not typed; ceSpendForBonus, CE_SPEND_MAX
//   I-04   which table judges an attempt is world vocabulary, not a per-turn
//          choice; schema 7 deletes the seeded no-roll def
//   I-05   a bounded number has a shape: pips vs bar, chosen by span
//   G-02   hideAtStart hides a row and changes nothing a reader sees
//   G-03   authored category order; an order list, not a category record
//   F-09b  a condition is a SHAPE, not a kind; the seeds exist to show it
//   EVT-BAND  a terminal is addressed by band INDEX or by bound, never both
//   STORAGE_POS_TERMINUS  either terminus raises a Standing rewrite, prefilled
// ENCOUNTERS
//   EVENT  a MILESTONE WITH A TRACK: foes are milestone.text, progress is one
//          bounded number of scope 'event' owned by the milestone id
//   EVENT-ONE  one live encounter, DERIVED by ceLiveEvent from the two places one
//          can be, so there is no flag to drift
//   EVENT-LOOSE  the threadless store has no entity, so its archive is capped at
//          the conclude instead of reaped
//   EVENT-STAKE  the fight's wager rides every roll and joins the ONE ledger
//   EVENT-TURN  ceEventTurnLine leads the spliced user turn; the AN carries it too
//          and the duplication is the point
//   EVENT-DOOR  step 0 of the Actions panel, a SECTION and not a tab -- a tab would hide
//          the fight at the moment the author is rolling against it
//   EVT-SHAPE  ten seeded shapes; the span counts successes and they count UP
//   EVT-MIRROR  two doors onto one encounter, symmetrical; neither owns a
//          verb -- both press ceMilestoneOp
//   EVT-POLARITY  the meter's tint is derived from the wager, never declared
// ACTIONS AND DELIVERY
//   F-15a  the armed move is primed in the panel and fired by NAI's Send
//   F-15b  Settings > Rolls owns resolutions and stakes
//   ARM-KEEP  the attempt is consumed by a roll; approach, resolution, target and wager
//          are not
//   CC-PREFIX  CharChat writes the TAIL of context and nothing else; one builder,
//          no swap
//   CC-TAIL  exactly one trailing empty paragraph before any generation
//   CC-REPAIR  the same normaliser runs BEFORE the generation as well as after
//   CC-REGEN  prepare, identify, refuse or clear, shape, fire
//   F-18   the four delivery mechanisms and why the read channel was the fault
//   F-18b  bracket shape rules -- live for the stepper-terminal writer
//   IMP-TRACE  /ce imp reports both ends of the queue
//   IMP-PIVOT  the onContextBuilt message ARRAY is ours to edit; storyText is not
//          reachable below the framing
//   F-19b  a roll arms a silent directive spliced as a bare user message before the
//          generation point. SUPERSEDES F-19, which made it two sentences of story
//   H-02   the panel is four steps in the order they happen
// GENERATION TRANSPORT
//   GEN-THINK  a reasoning model leaks its scratchpad into the answer and enable_thinking is
//          a REQUEST, not a guarantee. ceGenText is the one read of a result; ceStripThink
//          handles the unpaired tag by taking what FOLLOWS it and falling back to what
//          preceded it only when nothing does -- a cleaner that can empty a non-empty reply
//          is worse than the leak
//   GEN-ROUTER  one door per transport and no bypasses: aeDiagGenerate wraps
//          api.v1.generate, aeDiagGenerateWithStory wraps api.v1.generateWithStory, and
//          _aeGenRun holds the accounting both share. The /ce cache probe is the ONE
//          documented bypass because it measures a raw call
//   GEN-FAIL-KIND  a transport failure is not an answer. 'transport' halts, 'model'
//          may be retried, and a ladder that cannot tell them apart turns an exhausted
//          budget into a full set of silent defaults
// GENERATION DELEGATION
//   HELP-MESH  a companion script carries its OWN output allowance, and delegation lands at
//          the router so no caller in this file knows helpers exist. Six message names on
//          one channel, closed on the bridge protocol's rule -- partner is the helper
//          script, not the bridge
//   HELP-BUDGET-REPORTED  a helper REPORTS getAllowedOutput/getAllowedInput from inside its
//          own scope. Inferring exhaustion from the error text of a call that already failed
//          cannot answer which helper can serve 400 tokens, only that one just could not
//   HELP-ON-WALL  THE MECHANISM. A local call that is REFUSED for budget is handed to a
//          helper rather than thrown. Safe precisely because a refusal means the call never
//          happened; abort, network and timeout are 'transport' too and are NOT retried
//          elsewhere, because those may have completed with the answer lost
//   HELP-BOTH-TRANSPORTS  BOTH ROUTERS DELEGATE. Supersedes HELP-DIRECT-ONLY, which claimed
//          a story call could not be handed over because NAI builds its context from the
//          CALLING script's hooks. _aeGenRun sets _diagProbeActive around BOTH transports,
//          so our own story calls already suppress our own hooks and a helper's build is the
//          same. The wrong rule excluded the entire Build pipeline, which is all withStory
//   HELP-NEED-INPUT  THE WALL IS THE INPUT ALLOWANCE. These passes ask 24-400 output tokens
//          against a charter-plus-transcript prompt, so output is still comfortable when
//          input refuses -- an output-only pre-flight never fired once. Judged with
//          countUncachedInputTokens, LOCAL SIDE ONLY: it counts against our cache and a
//          helper's is cold, so the number does not transfer
//   HELP-LOCAL-DRY  once the allowance refuses, stop asking it for a cooldown. Otherwise
//          every call in a batch pays a failed local round trip first. Expires rather than
//          latching, because the allowance replenishes on its own
//   HELP-CAPS  a helper DECLARES story support and absent means no. A helper that ignored
//          the method would answer a story call with no story in it, which reads as working
//   HELP-LOCAL-FIRST  the local allowance is spent first; a mesh that round-robins keeps
//          every allowance half full and empties them together
//   HELP-NO-FALLBACK  a failed delegation THROWS and is never re-run locally: the helper may
//          still be generating, and the local allowance was already too small. The REVERSE
//          direction is HELP-ON-WALL and is safe; these two are not symmetrical
//   HELP-DEAD-ONCE  one timeout marks a helper dead for the session, so a deleted helper
//          costs one timeout and not one per call. Refresh clears the mark
//   HELP-NO-SIGNAL  a call carrying a CancellationSignal stays local -- the signal is an
//          object in this worker and there is no verb to relay an abort
//   HELP-REGISTRY-EPHEMERAL  module state, NO storage key. Helpers announce on their load and
//          this engine pings on its own, so it rebuilds from either end
//   HELP-HOOKS  A HELPER'S GENERATION FIRES THIS SCRIPT'S HOOKS. api.v1.generate builds story
//          context and NAI runs every installed script's hooks for it, so a delegated call
//          arrives at our five guards unmarked. _diagProbeActive is set and RESTORED around it:
//          it is our nested call, executing in another worker. No yoink-run reset -- that frees
//          a run because a METERED call was made, and this one is metered elsewhere
//   HELP-SEND-VOID  api.v1.messaging.send DOES NOT RELIABLY RETURN A PROMISE on this runtime.
//          `.catch()` on its result threw TypeError inside the pending-request executor, so the
//          delegated call rejected while the helper served it and answered into nothing. Send
//          through _ceHelpSend, which reports failure by RETURN VALUE, never by rejection
//   HELP-GUARD-FINALLY  _diagProbeActive and the busy flag are restored in a finally. Restoring
//          after the await leaked both on any throw between them, and a leaked probe flag
//          silently suppresses this file's own context hooks for the rest of the session
//   HELP-CHANNEL  its own messaging channel; handleBridgeMessage is registered UNFILTERED and
//          carries the matching early return
//   HELP-DEFAULT-ON  absent means on, summonOnMention's shape: installing a helper is the
//          opt-in, and a switch found afterwards makes the install look broken
// WIZARD GENERATION
//   YOINK-PREFILL  THE YOINK DOES NOT REPLACE THE CONTEXT. It appends one bracketed
//          block to storyText's tail and the directive rides the PREFILL, never a
//          spliced message
//   YOINK-PREFILL-FAIL  an override that throws leaves the context UNTOUCHED, so the
//          failure is visible as a story continuation rather than silent truncation
// ~ CTX-SWAP-NONE  audited: nothing in this file replaces a built context
//   IMP-PIVOT-GENPOINT  _ceGenPointIdx is the one answer to where the model starts
//          writing -- found, not assumed
// WIZARD CHAT (WIZLIVE)
//   WIZ-TAIL   CC-TAIL, ported
//   WIZ-REPAIR  CC-REPAIR, ported
// WIZARD SURFACE
//   SHELL-TAB  ceOpenShell forwards a tab to ceNav; ceNav clears the one it is not
//          given
//   WIZ-ONE-DOOR  the Setup tab is deleted into Start
//   WIZ-SEED   two fields, and the state is chosen by content
//   WIZ-ROWTEXT  committed rows show what the generators wrote, not entIndex
//   LORE-ONE-FIELD  one lorebook editor; the drill is gone
//   WIZ-GENSRC  the shared generation strip carries the wizard's selected chats
//   CTX-SOURCE  one source-of-truth frame on the context itself, not per prompt
//   CTX-CLEAN  saved chats are PARSED, not pasted
//   WIZ-SRCFILTER  the context checklist filters; chats and entries sit under their
//          own headers
//   WEB-GONE   the Drama tab's web block is deleted
//   REL-PAIR-RETIRED  ce_fx_pos_history and ce_fx_pos_unsaid gone from
//          BUILTIN_FACETS, v12
//   WIZ-ENTKIND  species, factions and items are one body driven by
//          CE_WIZ_ENT_KINDS
//   WIZ-WALK   the instructions are BUILT FROM CE_WIZ_TABS
//   WELL-ONE   ceWell is the kit's read-only text payload
//   CC-THOUGHT-EDIT  char.ephemeral is authorable from Chat; one writer
//   ACT-MODE   the panel has three shapes and the mode is a VALUE
//   ACT-MODE-DOORS  two doors onto one field, against SPEC-CTX: 'none' unregisters
//          the panel, so it cannot be its own way back
//   ACT-ODDS   exact, not sampled -- a convolution bucketed through ceRollBand
//   ATTEMPT-OPTIONAL  F-19's "the attempt is required" is DELETED with its composer 15, 38
// WORLD PIPELINE
//   CHARTER  the pipeline's INPUT, and the only thing that reaches a prompt as an
//          instruction. Records are its output and reach a prompt only as a digest. Two
//          prose tiers plus the rung ladder
//   LADDER  scale is AUTHORED, never derived from depth -- mapKind's own argument one layer
//          up. A rung maps onto an EXISTING LOCATION_FACETS scope and widens nothing, so the
//          facet table's rule survives: scope is a display-filter default that touches no
//          record. The rung decides what is ASKED by default, never what a record can HOLD,
//          and there is zero migration
//   LADDER-TOP-ONLY  only the top rung is authored on the scope form. The rest arrive when a
//          location is first stepped into, because there is no point naming a scale before
//          there is something at it. GAPS ARE LEGAL -- a house directly inside a region skips
//          the settlement rung because there is no settlement
//   SCOPE-FORM  three fields and a scale, no chat: the one stage where the author already
//          knows the answer and a conversation is a longer way to type it. The button authors
//          the rung and tops up the queue in one press because they are one decision, and it
//          is ADDITIVE ON RE-PRESS -- a destructive re-seed would throw away exactly the rows
//          the author had already named
//   NO-MANIFEST  WORLDPIPE 2 -- THE RECORDS ARE THE QUEUE. A manifest row and a stub record
//          hold the same facts, and the one that is not the record loses. Stubs are
//          WizardDrafts; status is DERIVED by ceScopeRowState from which fields are blank, so
//          nothing stores a status and nothing can drift out of one
//   SCOPE-COUNT-HONEST  the count SEEDS ROWS rather than being remembered as a number, which
//          is what stops it being a parked field [F-20] and is what lets an author name the
//          three places they already had in mind before any generator runs
//   NAME-CALL  PROMPTS 4 -- the name is its OWN call, on its own MACHINE-family head. A name
//          that arrives alone can be compared against a pool and re-asked for; a name inside
//          a paragraph cannot be fixed without discarding good prose. 24 tokens, stop on the
//          newline, three retries plus a five-candidate final rung
//   NAME-POOL-WORLD  the pool is WORLD-WIDE, not per rung. PROMPTS 4.3 argues per KIND, and
//          the half that decides it here is that two places sharing a name anywhere in one
//          world is the collision a reader actually hits. The ~120 cap is what pays for it
//   NAME-APPEND-ONLY  the pool grows by APPENDING, oldest first, so call n's prompt is call
//          n-1's plus text. WE orders most-recent-first with a cap, which rewrites the block
//          every call and throws the warm prefix away
//   NAME-CLEAN  PROMPTS 4.7 in order, with two corrections found by running it: FENCES COME
//          OFF BEFORE THE LINE IS PICKED (a fenced reply's first line is the fence, so
//          picking first returned nothing), and the leading-key strip is CASE-INSENSITIVE
//          (the spec's two spellings are `NAME:` and `Name -`, and a literal alternation of
//          those two cases matches neither)
//   NAME-ROOT  the spec's rule and the spec's example DISAGREE and both are implemented.
//          PROMPTS 4.8 says Levenshtein <=2 "catches Duskmere/Duskmoor"; measured, that pair
//          is distance 3 and the stated rule clears it. A long shared prefix on two long
//          names is the shared ROOT the example is about, and it returns `near`, never
//          `exact`, so it costs one re-ask and is still acceptable on the final rung
//   NAME-RETRY  the rejected candidate goes back as a REAL assistant turn, so the model sees
//          its own answer rather than being told about it in the third person. AN EXACT
//          DUPLICATE IS NEVER ACCEPTED at any rung, which makes a duplicate record
//          structurally impossible without ever stalling the batch
//   LADDER-BLOCK  PROMPTS 8, locations only. "Stay at this level" is the single most valuable
//          line in the location pipeline: without it a model handed a ward describes the
//          hospital, the city and the country it is in
//   EXTRACT-HANDOFF  extraction rides the CHAT family's prefix (that is what makes it
//          affordable) while PROMPTS 3.2 puts it in the MACHINE family, and both cannot sit
//          in the system block without discarding the cache. So the family switch rides the
//          ASK. Observed: an extraction returned a conversational turn and it was written
//          into a Memory field verbatim
//   EXTRACT-RELAPSE  a reply that is a question is not an answer, and it is detectable --
//          a question mark PLUS an address or hortative, both required, because canon prose
//          legitimately contains either alone. One retry with the rejected turn above it,
//          then REFUSE: a conversational turn in Memory is read before every scene for the
//          life of the story, which is worse than a blank the author can see
//   EXTRACT-REROLL  one field, on demand, overwriting. This is the answer to a bad
//          extraction instead of a review step between the chat and the fields -- a manifest
//          of proposed answers is a second copy of the question list the author is already
//          looking at, which is the structure WORLDPIPE 2 deleted
//   SCOPE-ONE-STAGE  Scope and Locations are ONE stage, two phases, at every depth: no rung
//          renders the scale form, a rung renders the workspace. WIZUI 5 gap 1 already puts
//          the rung question inline in the workspace's empty state at depth, so a separate
//          form stage answered one question in two places. 'scope' is a RETIRED STAGE ID and
//          normalises to 'places'
//   SCOPE-DEPTH  where the author is standing, in tempStorage per WORLDPIPE 7.5 -- session
//          state, because a return path that survives a story switch points at a record the
//          story does not have. '' is the top rung
//   SCOPE-DOOR  ONE DOOR WITH A SCOPE ARGUMENT, BOTH WAYS. A committed row has two visible
//          destinations and they are not the same door: Open goes to the record's profile to
//          develop its seeds into fields, Step into goes to this workspace one scale down.
//          Both return through NAV-BACK's existing address -- a surface inventing its own
//          back button is how three of them appear
//   LADDER-PER-PLACE  the rung below a record is OFFERED, not imposed. Different things sit
//          directly under a region -- a city here, a single house there -- so the ladder is
//          the default and the place decides. A skipped rung is a true fact about the world
//   REC-CHAT  ONE CHAT PER RECORD, beside the one per scope, because the two EXTRACTIONS
//          want different scopes: the roster asks what the author raised across a whole
//          conversation, and a record's seeds want that record's conversation with nothing
//          else in it. A ten-character transcript is paid on all ten seed calls and is noise
//          on nine. Addressed 'rec:<id>' on the SAME store, no new shape
//   REC-CHAT-DERIVED  the chat list is built FROM THE ROWS and is not authored. A chat exists
//          because a record does, so there is no add verb -- two chats about one thing would
//          be two sources for one extraction with no rule for which wins [F-29]
//   REC-CHAT-REKEY  a draft's id dies at commit, so the transcript is re-keyed onto the new
//          record id in the commit path itself. The alternative is a chat that orphans at
//          exactly the moment the thing it is about becomes real
//   REC-CHAT-ONE-EXIT  a record chat names ONE exit where the scope chat has to name two, so
//          its question list stops being context and becomes the agenda
//   CHAT-KEEP  a committed record KEEPS its chat, and the transcript store grows with the
//          cast for the life of the story. That is affordable only because Copy and Clear are
//          on the strip: the valve is what pays for keeping, and reaping at commit was the
//          alternative that was declined
//   CHAT-EXPORT  a JSON file through api.v1.file.save, the door exportData already uses. It
//          carries the RESOLVED SYSTEM BLOCK beside the turns, which closes WIZUI 5's
//          gap 4 at the record level for free: the export's second audience is a bug report
//   OPEN-SOLO  an accordion is a PREFIX ARGUMENT on ceExpandRow, not a second component.
//          Thirty seed rows open at once is 270 live fields; one at a time is nine
//   SCOPE-CHAT  PROMPTS 15 -- the scoped chat has its OWN block and was running on the world
//          profile's. It names both exits and decides neither, because the author picks by
//          pressing a button afterwards. Its scope instruction is SOFT: a hard refusal reads as
//          obstructive and authors route around it by not mentioning things
//   SCOPE-CHAT-STABLE  no digest of what exists at this scope, against PROMPTS 15's last line.
//          A committed row would rewrite the chat's system block and throw away a prefix the
//          whole conversation is warm on. The ladder and the question list do not move
//   SEED-CALL  PROMPTS 10 -- one call per RECORD, not per question, because the author judges
//          the lines as a set. The opposite reasoning holds one layer down, where the full
//          write of each question is its own call on the profile
//   SEED-ASK-SET  LADDER-SCOPE applied, not a new rule: the `any` lenses, the facets at this
//          rung's scope, and every field the author defined. A Region is not asked how the room
//          is lit. THE FULL SET ALREADY HAS A CONTROL -- a rung whose scope is `any` asks
//          everything, which is what the scale picker says it does
//   SEED-BASELINE  a seedless field is not a degraded one. A HAND-BUILT RECORD HAS NO SEEDS AT
//          ALL, so a narrower ask is measured against a baseline that does not exist
//   SEED-ROUTE  two sources, one format. A row whose NAME APPEARS IN THIS SCOPE'S TRANSCRIPT is
//          extracted from the conversation; every other row is generated from the charter. The
//          test is a substring and costs nothing, and `none` from the extraction falls through
//          to the generate path, so a miss degrades to the safe half
//   SEED-KEYS  labels are the keys. POSITION IS THE FALLBACK ONLY WHEN NO KEY RESOLVED AT ALL,
//          which is the one failure it is for -- a model that renamed the labels but kept the
//          order. A line with no colon is never placed, because that is a format failure and
//          guessing at it writes a silently wrong record
//   SEED-PARTIAL  A MISSING LINE IS A RESULT, correcting PROMPTS 10.5. That section retries for
//          every missing label while 10.3 tells the model to omit what it has nothing for, so
//          the retry as written forces exactly the padding 10.2 forbids. The retry fires only
//          when NOTHING parsed
//   SEED-REROLL  one row, on demand, and it CLEARS the asked set before writing -- unlike the
//          batch, which only adds. A re-roll that left a stale line behind would show the
//          author a record half from each pass
//   DIGEST-ANCESTOR DIGEST-SIBLING  PROMPTS 9. Built ONCE PER BATCH and carried in the base, so
//          they are prefix rather than tail. An ungenerated ancestor contributes NO LINE, not a
//          placeholder: a line saying a thing exists and nothing else is an invitation to
//          invent it at the level the pipeline has decided not to invent at yet
//   KIND-SHAPE  a LOCATION's shape is the RUNG's, because a rung is a scale and a scale is a
//          question set. NO OTHER KIND HAS SCALES, so theirs is one choice per story on
//          charter.shapes[kind]. A one-rung ladder would have unified them and would be a
//          ladder pretending to be a scale
//   SEED-DEFS-CHAR  A CHARACTER IS NOT A CATALOG ENTRY AND ITS THREE SHAPES ARE THREE REAL
//          QUESTION SETS -- classic is STATIC_FIELD_DEFS (the sheet), studio is the
//          `kind: 'slot'` rows of customStaticFields (the active profile template), custom
//          is the author's own facets. AN ENTITY'S CLASSIC IS ONE PROSE BOX AND ASKS
//          NOTHING; A CHARACTER'S CLASSIC ASKS FOUR THINGS. Same word, opposite meaning,
//          which is why the old early return had to carve the kind out and then had nothing
//          to give it. ceShapeOk MUST NOT RUN OVER A CHARACTER SHAPE: it knows
//          builder/classic/custom, so 'studio' normalises to 'builder' and the slots are
//          never asked -- ceKindShape, ceScopeShapeWrite and ceSeedDefs each branch before
//          reaching it. The classic defs reuse the sheet's own `placeholder` as the hint
//          rather than paraphrasing it, because a second copy of a question drifts
//   CHAR-SEED-DIRECT  A BUILD-ROUTE CHARACTER COMMITS ITS SEEDS AND THE DUMP PASS IS NOT
//          RUN. The old path derived the sheet from a concept dump with its own extraction
//          call -- one more generation, over prose the author never sees, to reach fields
//          the Seed pass has already written from the conversation itself. Running both
//          spends a call to overwrite better answers with worse. A SHEET FIELD IS A RECORD
//          COLUMN AND A SLOT IS A VALUE IN A BAG, and ceSeedDefs hands the pass one flat
//          list, so the id decides: the four STATIC_FIELD_DEFS ids ARE their column keys and
//          everything else lands in customFieldValues. THE LEGACY PATH IS UNTOUCHED for an
//          unseeded row [F-32] -- and it now STAMPS, which it never did: `character` has
//          always been in CE_BUILD_KINDS so _ceKindHasStage said true, the row went to
//          stampIds with no stampTo entry, was skipped by the stamp loop, and was committed
//          again on the next press
//   NAME-KIND  THE NAME PASS WAS ASKING FOR PLACES FOR EVERY KIND, in two independent ways.
//          ceNameMessages read `c.ladder` -- the LOCATION ladder -- while ceNameBatch had
//          already resolved the right one and never passed it; and the noun fell back to the
//          literal string 'place' whenever there was no rung, which is every ladderless kind,
//          so items, species, threads and characters were named by the sentence "Name one
//          place." A LADDERLESS KIND GETS ceKindNameBlock, NOT A LADDER WITH NOTHING IN IT:
//          every sentence of ceLadderBlock is about scale, and a kind with no scales was
//          handed it with an empty level list and the rung reading "this scale"
//   NAME-FROM-CHAT  the conversation is part of the name pass's BASE. It had none at all --
//          an author could spend ten turns saying what these are and get names drawn from a
//          noun and a collision pool. It goes in the base rather than the per-row ask, which
//          preserves NAME-CALL's stable prefix: the transcript is identical for every row, so
//          call n is still call n-1 plus text
//   DESC-ASKED  THE DESCRIPTION IS THE FIRST QUESTION, not a slot in the formatting rules.
//          It was named only in the format block, three paragraphs after a doctrine ending
//          "Never summarise." -- so a model that honoured the doctrine and skipped a
//          formatting slot dropped it, which is what an item's worn appearance did while its
//          facets filled. ALL FOUR CALLERS PASS THE KIND, both chat blocks included, because
//          SCOPE-CHAT-STABLE needs publish and the extraction pass to emit identical bytes
//   KIND-SHAPE-CAST  ceEntityHasShape asks the CATALOG, and a character has none -- so the
//          Cast stage hid the one picker it most needs, while ceKindShape and ceSeedDefs both
//          already knew about its three shapes. ceBuildKindHasShape is the Build route's own
//          question and the catalog is not its authority
//   OPEN-CHAT-FIRST  the conversation is ABOVE the button that consumes it and is not gated
//          on a draft existing. It was below and hidden until after the first write, so the
//          first press always happened before the author had said anything
//   OPEN-DEFAULT-NOT-RULE  in medias res is the DEFAULT AND NOT A RULE. The prompt commanded
//          it, so an author asking for a prologue, a frame or a cold open was overruled by
//          the paragraph above their own instruction. Length is the author's too [OPEN-BUDGET]
//   OPEN-WRITE  THE LAST STAGE AND THE ONLY ONE THAT IS NOT A QUEUE. Every stage above it
//          produces records; this produces ONE ARTIFACT from all of them. It is also the one
//          pass in this pipeline that writes PROSE FOR A READER rather than filling fields,
//          which is why it sits at 0.9 rather than the extraction row's 0.7: a colder call
//          writes competent generic prose, and that is the specific thing an opening cannot
//          be. THE RECORDS ARE CONTEXT, NOT SUBJECT MATTER -- the failure mode a pass
//          holding a charter and a cast list has is to introduce them, and most of what it
//          is given should not appear. Names are never invented; a needed person who is not
//          in the records is left unnamed, which is usually a sign of somebody worth
//          building. The upstream blocks are built from ceWorldQuestions and the entity
//          index, both of which already exist -- an opening with its own view of the world
//          would be a second derivation of it
//   OPEN-PLACE  WRITING AND PLACING ARE TWO ACTS. The text lands in an editable box on the
//          charter and reaches the document only on the second press --
//          DESTRUCTIVE-NEVER-IN-PLACE applied to the one control in this pipeline that
//          touches the story. PLACING IS NOT CONSUMING: the draft stays, so an author who
//          places, dislikes it and undoes in NAI still has the text. appendParagraphs, not
//          append, or NAI gets one enormous section the editor cannot split
//   OPEN-DRAFT  the opening lives on the CHARTER: it is the artifact written from the
//          charter, and a charter with an opening in it is a story that has been started
//   BUILD-CHAT-ONE  THE CHAT ADDRESS IS RESOLVED ONCE, IN cePublishBuild, AND STORED. It was
//          derived twice, the two were made to agree, AND IT BROKE AGAIN one stage later:
//          the Opening is not a kind, so it has no kdef, so the renderer's fallback
//          addressed its dock to 'loc:top' while publish read 'opn:top' -- the identical bug
//          from the identical cause. A SECOND DERIVATION IS NOT FIXED BY MAKING THE TWO
//          AGREE; IT IS FIXED BY DELETING ONE. The fallback survives for the single frame
//          before the first publish lands, where a blank address would post into nowhere
//   DESC-MEANS  THE ALWAYS-ON DESCRIPTION LINE IS NOT A SUMMARY FOR EVERY KIND. Every
//          staged row has a `description`: written by the seed pass's SUMMARY line, rendered
//          as its own box above the questions WHATEVER THE SHAPE IS, and committed to the
//          record's own description column. It never passes through ceSeedDefs, so it is
//          already always part of every kind's shape including `classic` and `custom` --
//          nothing needed adding. AN ITEM'S DESCRIPTION IS MECHANICAL: what the thing looks
//          like on whoever wears or carries it, which is the line ceEntityLoreText emits
//          when it is equipped. What was wrong was only what the box and the ask SAID, in
//          three places that must agree -- label, placeholder, and the SUMMARY line -- so
//          they now come off ONE TABLE with three readers. CE_P_SEED_FORMAT is split into a
//          head and tail around the varying clause and is gone as a constant; ceSeedFormat
//          is the only builder, and `kind` is threaded to both prompt assemblers rather than
//          re-derived from the noun
//   NAV-TAB-REMEMBERED  ONE SLOT PER SURFACE, NOT ONE SLOT QUALIFIED BY SURFACE. The
//          qualified single slot stopped one surface reading another's tab -- the bug
//          NAV-TAB-QUALIFIED was written for -- but made remembering IMPOSSIBLE: there is
//          one value and the next surface to set one overwrites it, and ceNav CLEARED it on
//          every nav that carried no tab. So any arrival that was not a Back landed on the
//          fallback, and Build opened on Charter every time whatever stage the author left
//          from. A map keyed by surface gives isolation (the key IS the check) and memory
//          (that is what a map does). A NAV THAT NAMES NO TAB LEAVES THE MAP ALONE: it is
//          not a statement that the destination has no tab. Still ceUiStore and still not
//          persisted, on SCOPE-DEPTH's argument -- which stage you are on is where you are
//          standing, not a fact about the story. An already-qualified value is tolerated and
//          stripped once, which fixes the two stored back addresses that pass
//          'entity:resources' and 'entity:roster' and were being re-qualified into a tab id
//          no tab bar has ever had
//   FIELD-MINT  WRITING THE QUESTIONS, not answering them -- WORLD-PICK-ADD one level down.
//          IT EXISTS BECAUSE A SEED PASS WITH NO QUESTIONS CANNOT RUN: an item's catalog
//          base is EMPTY and `custom` shape empties any kind's, so those stages had a Seed
//          button with nothing to ask and no way to say why. SEED-BASELINE covers a narrow
//          ask against a full set and does not cover an empty one. It rides the SCOPE
//          CHAT's exact prefix per EXTRACT-HANDOFF, is told what is already asked so it does
//          not repeat it, and uses WORLD-PICK-ADD's `+ Name | one line` format because there
//          is no reason for two parse shapes for one act
//   FIELD-MINT-DOOR  the questions group is NOT the second schema editor SHAPE-ASKS forbids.
//          That rule is about a screen duplicating the shape picker's decision -- WHICH set
//          is asked. This decides what is IN the author's own set, which is ENT-CF-DOOR's
//          job and has always lived beside the record; the Build route is the other place an
//          author looks at a kind, and at Build time there is no record to hang it on.
//          ceCustomFieldDefine is the ONE writer and the profile's define button is its
//          other caller
//   ROW-RETAIN-COUNT  ceScopeWrite's top-up counted STAMPED drafts. Every other draft scan
//          in the file skips them, so after committing two the count read 2, `want` could
//          never exceed it, and Add reported "2 already queued" over a visibly empty list.
//          The queue and the count have to mean the same thing or the number in the box is
//          answering a different question from the list under it
//   SHAPE-ASKS  THE SHAPE PICKS THE QUESTIONS AND THERE IS NO SECOND EDITOR. The profile's
//          own three-way -- Lorebook Builder / Classic / Custom fields only -- already means
//          what a schema screen would have to mean, so it moves onto the SCALE, where a rung
//          is one question set by construction. custom asks only the author's own; classic
//          asks nothing FOR AN ENTITY and still writes the summary -- but a character's
//          Classic is a real question set, so the kind must reach this filter before Cast. Carried to EntityRecord.uiShape at
//          commit, so a Classic place does not scan as `builder` and grow a set the author
//          chose not to have
//   BUILD-COMMIT-KIND  the faction commit branch now carries parent, rung, seeds and shape
//          exactly as the location branch does. It predates the pipeline and dropped all
//          four, which would have committed seeded factions with nothing in them.
//          _ceDraftSeeds and _ceRungShapeIn are shared rather than copied, because a second
//          kind reaching commit is exactly when the copy would have been made
//   PASS-KIND  every pass takes a `kind` and DEFAULTS IT to 'location'. The default is what
//          let the threading land in one patch without a flag day, and it is also the thing
//          to delete once every stage is on -- a default that is right for one caller is a
//          silent wrong answer for the next one added
//   BUILD-BANDS  FIVE BANDS, ONE ORDER, EVERY STAGE: where you are, talk, set up, the work,
//          what exists. THE ORDER IS THE ORDER OF OPERATIONS and downward is forward. What
//          this replaces was nine consecutive ceGroups -- CE-STYLE's FIELD density -- so
//          "configure this kind once", "work these rows repeatedly" and "here is what
//          exists" rendered identically. ONE DENSITY WAS DOING FOUR JOBS, which is not a
//          spacing problem and no gap fixes it. ceGroup still exists and is still correct
//          INSIDE a band. A BAND IS NOT A BOX: Object density draws the box and is reserved
//          for the records themselves
//   BUILD-BANDS-SETUP  SET UP IS ABOVE THE WORK, which is the reported fault stated
//          structurally -- the shape picker and the question list both GOVERN the rows and
//          both rendered BELOW them. A thing that decides what a row is asked cannot sit
//          under the row. It COLLAPSES once answered, because setup is decided once and
//          re-read rarely and leaving it open is most of the stack on a return visit
//   BUILD-BANDS-TALK  TALK IS SECOND, above set-up, on all ten stages. The conversation is
//          the thing a new user cannot discover and every generator on the page reads it;
//          it was 2nd on World, 4th on the kind stages, absent on Charter and last on
//          Opening, so the order a user learned on one stage was wrong on the next
//   BUILD-STEPS  ceStepTrack DRAWS THE PROGRESSION. add -> name -> seed -> commit is four
//          acts and the only signal was a button label changing IN PLACE, so an author
//          looked below the fold for a next step that was never there. Every value it shows
//          is already derived by ceScopeRowState -- this is state finally being drawn, not
//          new logic. THE CELLS ARE NOT DOORS; the controls beneath them are
//   CHAT-PANEL  the dock HAD NO EDGES -- it paints its own ground and grows to 46vh inline
//          with no rule above or below, and closed, its only affordance was a TRUNCATED COPY
//          OF THE LAST MESSAGE, which reads as a status line rather than a door. The panel
//          is a wrapper, not a rewrite: rules, air, a head that says "Conversation" with the
//          turn count, and the last message demoted to a preview beneath it
//   BUILD-COPY  every stage states WHAT IT PRODUCES and where that goes, via
//          ceStagePreamble's three REQUIRED parts -- which is the only durable answer to a
//          surface whose whole vocabulary (charter, rung, seed, shape, commit) was
//          load-bearing and none of it introduced. "Charter" is now "How it reads", its
//          tiers are "What is always true" and "How the prose sounds", and the stored keys
//          stay `style` and `play` per CHARTER-TIER-NAMES
//   BUILD-CHROME  _ceBuildScope is ONE renderer for every kind. Everything below the trail
//          is identical; the trail and Step into ride `recursive`, the scale form rides
//          `ladder`, the scale picker rides KIND-SCOPE, and no branch names a kind
//   BUILD-CHROME-READS  the renderer was kind-clean and THE DATA READS WERE NOT. The draft
//          queue, the committed index, the commit call and the shape picker all named
//          'location', so every kind stage listed locations, offered to name them, and
//          committed them -- while the rows ceScopeWrite had correctly staged for that kind
//          were invisible. A structure with no branch on a kind is not the same thing as a
//          structure that reads the right one. THE LAST SITE WAS INSIDE ceScopeWrite: it
//          wrote the rung onto the right ladder, counted against the right queue, and then
//          staged the rows themselves as LOCATIONS -- so Add factions reported success, put
//          nothing on the faction stage, and quietly grew the location queue
//   BUILD-SCOPE-HOIST  `kdef` was DECLARED BELOW the two loops that read it. `var` hoists
//          the declaration and not the value, so String(kdef.kind) ran against `undefined`
//          on any stage with a staged row -- Locations included, and invisible on an empty
//          one, which is why it survived a session's testing
//   KIND-SCOPE  THE SCALE VOCABULARY BELONGS TO THE KIND, and which kinds have one is
//          MEASURED off the facet tables rather than declared: LOCATION_FACETS carries
//          region/settlement/structure/interior, SPECIES_FACETS carries people/beast, and
//          faction and narrative carry `any` alone. CE_KIND_SCOPES adds only the label and
//          hint prose a picker needs, so A KIND WITH NO ENTRY RENDERS NO PICKER and resolves
//          to 'any' -- which is what ceSeedDefs already did for it by falling through. The
//          picker is ABSENT rather than disabled, because there is no scale here to deny.
//          ceLadderScopeOk and _ceScopeScopeHint are DELETED: both read the location table
//          for every kind, which is how a species' 'people' became 'any'
//   KIND-SCOPE-LATER  species' two lists are a real scope pair and are NOT declared here
//          yet. Declaring a vocabulary before the stage that reads it is [F-20]'s parked
//          field one layer up
//   KIND-NOUN  THE ROUTE'S WORD FOR A RECORD IS READ FROM CE_ENTITY_CATALOGS, which has
//          carried `noun` per type since F-32 -- so the Build route mints no second table
//          and cannot disagree with the profile about what a thing is called. A CHARACTER
//          HAS NO CATALOG and the kind string is its own correct noun, which is why the
//          fallback is the kind rather than a literal. CE_KIND_RUNG_HINTS is the one
//          exception and is prose nothing reads back: it is the string an author sees
//          BEFORE they know what the field wants, and 'Region, Ward, Deck' on the faction
//          stage was the whole reported confusion
//   NAV-TAB-QUALIFIED  ceNav wrote a BARE tab into the slot ceTabSet writes 'surface:id'
//          into, so ceTabGet found no colon, read it as another surface's, and returned the
//          fallback. Every nav carrying a tab has silently landed on the destination's
//          default stage since F-29 -- silent because that default is a real screen
//   STAGE-COUNT  the stage picker numbered by INDEX into CE_BUILD_STAGES and sized by its
//          length, both of which count `built: false` entries. A stage that is not offered
//          does not have a number
//   PROFILE-RETURN  _ceEntityRoot returned ceScreen(null, body) at all three tails, so a
//          profile opened FROM the Build route rendered no way back to it. LOC-PROFILE3's
//          rule is kept and narrowed: the shell's nav row reaches a TAB, never a stage and
//          scope inside one, so a nav carrying a return address is the exception. ceBack()
//          is the condition -- null without an address, so direct visits are unchanged
//   RUNG-PLURAL  a rung's label is the author's and is usually ALREADY PLURAL (Universes,
//          Galaxies), so appending 's' printed "universess" in four places. _cePlural
//   ROW-VALUE-SHORT  ceRow's middle slot is a narrow column. A value that is a sentence goes
//          on its own ceSub line beneath, or it renders one character per line
//   ROW-RETAIN  a committed LOCATION row is STAMPED (CE_DRAFT_DONE_KEY) rather than deleted,
//          so the lines the record was born with stay legible after generation overwrites
//          them on the record. Every draft scan skips a stamped row -- commitStagedDrafts'
//          own loop first, because a duplicate record is the failure that costs something.
//          Other kinds are still removed: the Build route is the only surface that renders
//          a committed row, and a retained draft with nowhere to appear is staging that
//          never empties
//   SEED-IS-VALUE  A SEED IS THE FIELD'S FIRST VALUE. Commit writes customFieldValues, not a
//          parallel map -- so a committed record READS immediately instead of showing an
//          empty form over twenty real words per question. Generation overwrites its own
//          steer and that is accepted; the staged row keeps the lines. No marker
//   ~SEED-DURABLE  EntityRecord.fieldSeeds now has NO WRITER. ceEntityOrderedDefs still reads
//          it and old records still carry it, so the read stays until it can be removed
//          against the live surface. Delete both together or not at all
//   SEED-DURABLE  EntityRecord.fieldSeeds, keyed by the SAME facet id as customFieldValues.
//          PROMPTS 19.1: the seed goes ABOVE the instruction and the instruction is
//          UNTOUCHED, because ceCustomFieldSeedInstr's rule is that the author's own text
//          wins and a seed written into `instructions` would delete a facet instruction.
//          _ceGenSpec is the one resolver, so it cannot reach one generation path and miss
//          the other, and the facet field SHOWS it (G-04's rule)
//   SEED-NOT-WIZHINT  a seed is NOT _ceWizHints. That is a module var, explicitly
//          session-scoped -- its own header says persisting it "gave it a lifetime nothing
//          wanted" -- because it describes THIS press. A seed is a property of the record,
//          survives a reload, and steers every later generation of its field
//   SCOPE-SEL-STORE  the scale picker is store-backed, not ceFieldLive-backed, and that is a
//          property of ceSelect: it does not register in CE_FIELD_ELS, so a live read returns
//          null and a press would silently take the previous value. A select commits on
//          change, so there is nothing in flight to lose
//   CHARTER-AN  the Voice tier IS the Author's Note, per WORLDPIPE 3.1, and ceCommitAuthorNote
//          is the ONE site that writes the managed region. It is a resolver with a
//          precedence, not two writers: the charter wins, and the 'an'-targeted wizard
//          sections stay underneath as the fallback so a story written before the charter
//          does not have its Note emptied on the first save. Publishing is triggered inside
//          ceCharterWrite, so the field commit and the compression pass share one trigger
//   WIZUI-GUIDE  WIZUI 3.2 -- no permanent paragraph of instruction on a pipeline surface.
//          The empty-state lede stays (3.1); everything else is a GUIDE-BODY fold on the
//          group's heading row
//   WIZUI-PRIMARY  WIZUI 3.3 -- one accented control per surface. On World it is the
//          projection into Memory, which is safe to offer forever because Memory is a
//          projection and re-pressing is idempotent
//   CHARTER-TIER-NAMES  the tiers READ as Rules and Voice and are STORED as `style` and
//          `play`. Rules is stamped on every generation; Voice reaches the Author's Note and
//          no generator. The labels moved because Style sat one synonym from Voice and
//          described neither; the keys did not, on TAB-WORLD's rule
//   CHARTER-COMPRESS  two calls, one per tier, appended to the premise chat's EXACT prefix.
//          One call for both tiers lets the first eat the output budget; a fresh context
//          blob would be fully uncached and cost more than the conversation did
//   CHARTER-CANON-NONE  there is no third tier. canon IS the world profile's answers,
//          projected; a compression call for it would be a second writer of one artifact
//   P-SUBSTRATE  the cached head, ordered by VOLATILITY and never by topic. Caching
//          inverts the usual economy: the head is paid once per session and the ask is
//          paid every call, so the head may be thorough and the ask may not
//   P-GATE  PROMPTS 21's runtime gate, at /ce prompt. Three checks, no budget spent: every
//          call in a batch is one head plus one differing tail, the largest ask is under a
//          third of the head, and no substrate block names a setting
//   P-GATE-NO-TOKENS  it proves PREFIX IDENTITY rather than a billed delta, correcting 21.
//          countUncachedInputTokens reports against the cache AS IT STANDS, so a gate that
//          generates nothing never warms it and "call 2 bills less" would fail on a healthy
//          pipeline. Byte-identity is what that assertion was a proxy for and is free
//   P-GATE-NOUN-SCOPE  the noun scan covers the STATIC BLOCKS BY NAME, not a built array. An
//          array carries the author's charter and the author's transcript, which is exactly
//          where a setting noun is correct
//   P-STYLE-ABOVE-NAMING  PROMPTS 22.1 closed: style tier ABOVE. The volatility case for the
//          other order pays nothing because a session is one story, so the choice is semantic
//          and 4.2 already made it. Recency does not apply -- style says how names SOUND,
//          NAMING says what a name must not ANNOUNCE, and they do not compete
//   P-NO-SETTING  no block in the substrate contains a noun belonging to any setting. The
//          kind noun arrives in the ask. This is what makes one NAMING block serve places,
//          people, groups and objects where WE needed two
// CHAT IN A MENU
//   CHAT-DOCK  one component, two heights, composer present at BOTH. Closed is a lead line
//          plus the box; open is the thread. The height change is a resize, never a
//          navigation, which is what stops a mobile chat feeling like a second screen
//   CHAT-REVERSE  NOTHING CAN SCROLL A CONTAINER HERE (see SCROLL-ANCHOR), so the scroller
//          is column-reverse and turns are newest-FIRST in the DOM. Reverse the array, never
//          the reading order
//   CHAT-HEAD-SLOT  the dock owns no header. The wizard passes a spine or a trail, a
//          character passes a person, and a dock that owned one would force one to be wrong
//   CHAT-SEND-LIVE  Send is a CLICK and arrives before the blur that would commit the box,
//          so it READS through ceFieldLive and CLEARS through ceFieldSet -- a `value` prop
//          cannot clear a composer, because the seed is '' on both sides of a send and
//          ceField only writes when that prop CHANGES
//   BUILD-CHAT  one transcript per scope, addressed by scope id, one storyStorage record
//          for all of them. The wizard chat's SHAPE is reused; its generation path is not.
//          ceBuildChatAddr IS THE ONE RESOLVER: the strip and the dock spelled 'loc:' while
//          cePublishBuild built the same address through kdef.chatPfx, so on the faction
//          stage the dock WROTE loc:top and the publish READ fac:top -- every turn typed
//          there landed in the location conversation, silently, and never came back
//   BUILD-CHAT-NEW-PATH  WIZLIVE's prompt builder is retired and must not be recreated. It
//          pastes whole transcripts into a blob whose order varies per call, so every call
//          was fully uncached. This builds ONE stable [system, ...turns] prefix and appends
//   BUILD-CHAT-FAILKIND  a failed turn is written INTO the transcript with its reason. A
//          chat that drops a turn on a dead budget looks exactly like a model with nothing
//          to say
//   CHAT-DOTS  the waiting bubble. No @keyframes exist in this tree scope and there is no
//          setInterval, so it is a self-rescheduling api.v1.timers chain guarded by a
//          generation counter, driving a transition on an inline style. ITS OWN STORE, read
//          by nothing but the dots -- an indeterminate indicator a whole surface subscribes
//          to is a performance bug, which is what killed the old generation rail
//   BUILD-WORLD  the world profile's questions ARE cfg.wizardSections -- already the
//          question set, already editable, already what assembleWizardMemory projects into
//          Memory. The 'an'-targeted sections are not part of the pipeline's set because the
//          charter's Voice tier owns the Author's Note outright
//   WORLD-PICK  WHICH QUESTIONS A STORY ANSWERS IS `enabled`, a field that has gated
//          assembleWizardMemory since WIZARD-2. There is no second flag. The pass reads the
//          PREMISE chat, applies directly because every switch it moves is on the same
//          screen, and never switches off a question that already has text in it
//   WORLD-PICK-ADD  it also MINTS questions -- up to CE_WORLD_PICK_ADD_MAX the defaults have
//          no name for -- and the surface says so, because an author who is not told a
//          question can be written reads a new one on the list as a bug in the defaults. The
//          ceiling is one constant, read by the ask and by the copy
//   WORLD-EXTRACT  the answers, from the WORLD chat: blanks only, switched-on only, one call
//          per question. Not one call for all of them -- CHARTER-COMPRESS already measured
//          that and the first field ate the budget
//   WORLD-HELPER  two audiences, two fields. `instructions` is the model's copy and one seed
//          opens "Output ONLY a single ATTG line"; `helper` is the author's and is the BOX'S
//          PLACEHOLDER, not a line above it -- a placeholder clears itself when text arrives
//          and a prose line has to be hidden. A switched-off question has no box, so there it
//          is prose. Optional on the record, resolved through ceWorldHelper against the
//          defaults by id, so no story needs migrating
//   BUILD-CHAT-EDIT  a bubble is tappable and both roles are editable, because the
//          transcript IS the prompt -- a bad reply left in it steers every later call.
//          Editing does NOT re-run the reply
//   CHAT-THEME  the dock's own token group. Separate from Bubble/cb-* because that family
//          is the EDITOR-painted stage driven by CE_CB_LOOKS and has no name for a
//          composer, a dock bar or a scroller ground
//   EMPTY-LEDE  where a surface is the ONLY way to create the thing it is empty of, its
//          empty state is a LEDE ABOVE the controls, never ceEmpty() in their place --
//          otherwise the first run of every story has nothing to type into
//   BUILD-KINDS  a stage is a KIND plus three facts: recursive, ladder, lists. Species
//          divides into two lists, which is not depth
//   KIND-LIST  A FLAT KIND THAT STILL DIVIDES, and `lists` is now the TABLE rather than the
//          string 'species' that nothing read. THE LIST PLAYS THE RUNG'S PART: it is the
//          standing pointer, it supplies the facet scope to ceSeedDefs, it addresses the
//          scope chat, and it lands on the record as speciesList. What it does NOT carry is
//          a shape -- the scope already separates the question sets. EACH ENTRY DECLARES ITS
//          SCOPE because the map is not identity: the lists are people/bestiary and
//          SPECIES_FACETS is scoped people/beast, so an identity map would have asked a
//          creature the people questions. Its pointer is its OWN tempStorage key, not the
//          scope pointer -- `scopeAt` means "which record am I inside" and a list is not a
//          record, so fusing them would make a species' parentId read as 'people'
//   KIND-LIST-FALLBACK  a draft or record carrying no list lands on the FIRST list, which is
//          ceSpeciesList's own additive rule read at the queue end: a row staged by the old
//          wizard appears under Peoples rather than under neither
//   BUILD-COMMIT-KIND  the four facts a staged row carries into a record -- seeds as field
//          values, shape, rung, parent -- resolved ONCE by _ceCommitFacts. Species and items
//          dropped all four and threads HAD NO BRANCH AT ALL, so turning that stage on
//          without one would have logged every row and committed none. THE SHAPE FOLLOWS THE
//          SEEDS on a ladderless kind: commitStagedDrafts is reachable from the old wizard
//          too, and an unseeded row is one block of legacy prose that must still land in
//          Classic [F-32]
//   KIND-SHAPE-WRITE  ceScopeShapeWrite BRANCHES ON THE RUNG, NOT ON THE KIND, because that
//          is what ceKindShape's read does. It branched on `kind !== 'location'`, so a
//          faction -- which has rungs since KIND-LADDER -- had its shape written to
//          charter.shapes.faction and read back off the rung, which carries none and answers
//          'builder'. The picker committed, the store republished, and the value snapped
//          back with nothing on the console. The picker's own `pk === 'location' && !rid`
//          guard goes with it: an empty rung is now the flat-kind case, not "nowhere to
//          write"
//   KIND-LADDER  A LADDER PER RECURSIVE KIND, and FACTIONS HAVE ONE. Recursion and a ladder
//          are still separate facts -- parent context comes from the ancestor digest, which
//          walks parentId and needs no scales -- but the ladder also carries magnitude for
//          the passes, a default rung for children, and THE COUNT CONTROL that
//          SCOPE-ONE-STAGE folded into the scale form. A ladderless nesting kind needed a
//          second empty state to ask "how many", which is where one renderer starts to
//          fray. LOCATIONS KEEP charter.ladder; every other kind is charter.ladders[kind],
//          because moving locations would rewrite every existing story's charter for a
//          symmetry no author would see
//   SCOPE-PER-KIND  one scope pointer per recursive kind, keyed off STORAGE_BUILD_SCOPE with
//          a suffix. A single pointer carried a location id into the faction stage, where it
//          names nothing and the list is empty with no way to tell why
//   BUILD-ONE-ROUTE  the pipeline is ONE route with its own internal stages, not one route
//          per stage. It reaches out (to a profile, for field generation) and keeps a way
//          back to itself
//   BUILD-UI-SUB  the Build root subscribes to ceUiStore like every other route root. It was
//          the one that did not, so on this surface alone no '?' body opened, no accordion
//          moved and no button ever rendered its busy label -- ceAction set the latch and
//          nothing repainted to read it
// LOREBOOK PROJECTION
//   LORE-PROJECT-LEAK  projection-only fields must not reach a saved record
// RELATIONSHIPS
//   REL-PAIR   ONE RECORD PER PAIR, addressed by the unordered pair
//   REL-INJECT  the pair speaks in three places, each carrying a different thing
//   REL-WANT   what each side wants, keyed by character id; the pressure tier aims
//          at it
//   REL-DOOR   Relationships is a drill, not an accordion
//   REL-TAB    Profile and Relationships are tabs, not a door
//   SHAPE-CUSTOM  a third uiShape: only fields the author defined
//   SHAPE-PICK  three values is where two-buttons-beat-a-dropdown turns over
// CUSTOM FIELDS
//   ENT-CF-EVERY  a field the author defined is not a shape's property
//   ENT-CF-SEED  an empty instruction is a field with no Generate button
//   ENT-CF-EDIT  the manage drill is an editor; one write verb
//   ENT-CF-DOOR  define and remove are one door, on the record
//   CARD-HUE   the left border is PRESSURE, not pattern
//   REL-CARD   the card interior is a read view, not a form; REL-CARD-OLD is the
//          superseded form-shaped one
//   REL-MIRROR  only the open profile republishes; the store is single-slot
//   REL-AVATAR  the image anchor takes the RESOLVED protagonist id
//   REL-GEN    all four pair fields generate on a fifth slotOpts scope
//   ATT-TIER   _ceDramaAttention reads every pairing, want or no want
//   CARD-MEDIA  the picture fills the left end and has no frame
//   CARD-BODY  ceRecordCard's body renders INSIDE the card
//   SEG-BAR    N short doors in one enclosure; height explicit, cells do not shrink
//   LAUNCH-BTN  the profile's launcher cells are one segmented banner
//   CARD-EXPAND  ceExpandCard, the fourth collapsible, against KIT-ELSEWHERE
//   REL-WANT-RETIRED  ce_fx_pos_want's def is deleted; the prose moves to a custom
//          field, v10
//   REL-TIER   the non-protagonist side pays
//   REL-WRITE  ceRelWrite is the ONLY site that writes a pair record;
//          applyCharFieldValue refuses 'rel.*' out loud
//   HOOK-ONE   one hook per character, never per pair, and it renders ONCE at the
//          top of the Relationships tab; both halves enforced -- the picker filters
//          to existing pairings and a scan naming anyone else is dropped 1, 8, 12, 30
// FACTIONS, STANDING, DIPLOMACY
//   F-28   a faction is an EntityRecord; the id is preserved
//   F-29   the faction screen is the entity surface; CE_FACTION_TABS
//   F-31   a faction is an address on BOTH sides of a move
//   F-24   the standing ladder is the world's unless a faction overrides
//   F-25   ceSyncStandingDefs; managed defs are engine-owned and read-only
//   F-26   Diplomacy is a shell route; Settings has no Factions tab
//   F-27   the roster is derived from standing and never stored
// ~ F-22   [superseded by F-25] standing is a state, not a ladder
// ENTITIES
//   ITEM-CAT   a record type is a catalog row, not a surface
//   F-32   a species is an EntityRecord, F-28 one type over
//   SPEC-LIST  which list, as a VALUE on the record, not a second type
//   SPEC-LORE  two more composed entries, not one with two headings
//   SPEC-BODY  prose in lorebookText, the one-line read in description
//                                                                1, 6, 6b, 10, 21, 36, 41
//   CARD-ONE   one card for a record, ceRecordCard
//   SPEC-CTX   the two species entries are configured where the species are 1, 10d, 32, 41
//   SUMMON-EFF  the chip renders the EFFECTIVE answer, not the record's field
//   IMG-TAG-SHARED  a slot's tag is story vocabulary, one copy, merged at load
//   SPEC-SHAPE  the species image drill has a shape select; imgWidth/Height are read
//   SPEC-IMG   a species uses the character image architecture, not the location's 9b, 36
//   SPEC-REAP  the one image reap that asks the type first (ENT-ID1)
//   ITEM-HOLD  an item's live state is parentId + isEquipped; aeSetEquip is the verb 28, 36
//   G-01   delete lives at the bottom of the profile and takes the entry
//   NARR-LENS  a thread is an EntityRecord with a catalog; labels are entry prefixes 2, 36
//   NARR-KIND  the kind owns one steer line per lens and stores no answer
//   NARR-COLUMN  the profile-column gate is the CATALOG, not `shape`
//   MS-GEN   milestone generation is a third scope on the one slot-options strip 17, 29, 36
//   MS-TAB   a thread's milestones are a tab; no concept dump
//   TAB-ENTS  every entity in one indented list under World; search FLATTENS
//   MILESTONE  the current milestone is the thread's live state, its own key
//   JRNL-STAMP  bubbles carry storyT, stamped at the write and formatted in the publish;
//          an absent stamp self-suppresses, which is also how cfg.journalStamps turns them
//          off -- ONE value for every journal, applied at the projection, storyT untouched
//                                                                          10b, 12, 31
//   JRNL-SCAN  the journal writes its own entry off the yoink: one event, not a
//          period, and the trailing bracket is trimmed at this caller only
//   YOINK-VOICE  voice:'prose' swaps the prefill's refusal block for one that
//          refuses continuation without refusing prose
//   TAB-STORY  one surface, two subtabs; the route id stays 'journal'
//   LORE-EVERY  a field commit creates the entry it cannot find
//   LOC-PROFILE3  the anchored image frame
//   EQUIP-DESC-NOT-NAME  a holder's entry folds the item's description
// CLOCK AND SCHEDULES
//   T-01   the clock is not a StateDef -- a time band wraps midnight
//   T-03   it lives in historyStorage because it moves every turn and rewinds
//   T-06   the model sees the band and nothing else
//   SCENEBLOCK  the AN scene header is ONE part (time line + place line) and the
//          present block is a PARAGRAPH; no "Present:" label
// ~ T-02   [decided, not built] a clock stake cell is a third kind, not a defId
//   T-19   the pin: one plan forced on, checked before the rules
//   ATMO   a location's schedule IS its atmosphere
//   CE_SCHED_MODES  two owners, one editor; the table owns every string that differs 30
// CHARCHAT AND WIZLIVE
//   CC_MODES  four kinds of conversation; NOTHING ELSE MAY FORK ON MODE
//   CC-NO-DOUBLE  every CE-managed conversation entry is off for the duration
//   CC-CTX  saved threads have a context door: two entries, never one
//   CHARCHAT-USERTOKEN WIZLIVE-USERTOKEN  context ending on a closed bracket block,
//          measured rather than inferred
//   WIZLIVE-BRACE-DOUBLE  no literal braces -- NAI draws the brace itself
//   CHARCHAT-DOM-INJECT  work started in onGenerationRequested may not have landed
//          by context assembly
//   CC-EMO-NOEXT  THERE IS NO EXTERNAL EMOTION WIDGET. Three comments claimed one, one of them
//          calling it a hard external contract and a second calling the prefix's two leading
//          spaces load-bearing because of it. Audited false: the only matchers are
//          _charchatEmotionRx and the widget pass's own scan, both whitespace-tolerant
//   CC-EMO-SLOT  the parser answers WAS A TAG FOUND, _ccEmotionSlot answers WHAT DO I PAINT.
//          A mode that asks for an emotion always yields one and defaults to 'neutral', which
//          is a real canonical slot; a mode that does not ask still yields ''
//   PERSONA-PROBE  three blocked facts, one button, eight calls: is model: honoured on
//          api.v1.generate, do the two models share a prefix cache, does a trailing assistant
//          turn prefill. The model test is a tokenizer round-trip because nothing echoes which
//          model served a call
//   PCP-CACHE-SPLIT  MEASURED: glm-4-6 and xialong-v1 have INDEPENDENT prefix caches that do
//          not evict each other. 1179 cold, 27 warm, and the other model pays cold on the same
//          prefix. The two-channel split costs nothing
//   PCP-MODEL-HONOURED  MEASURED: api.v1.generate serves model:'xialong-v1'. Proved by the
//          CACHE MISS, not by a decode -- a miss cannot be faked, so a cache arm is the
//          strongest available answer to "which model served that call"
//   PCP-PREFILL-REACHES  MEASURED: a trailing assistant turn is billed, so it reaches the
//          prompt. Whether it is CONTINUED or answered as history is still open
//   PCP-SINK  a probe whose answer is generated TEXT needs a sink of its own; the ring buffer
//          records billing and timing and never a completion. runBudgetProbe's button discards
//          its report, and copying that pattern lost this probe's arm 2 on its first run
//   PCP-PREFILL  MEASURED: a trailing assistant turn IS continued, on BOTH models -- "Kesserel:
//          yeah, I" returned "'ll be there". The reply's ask can be empty and the name prefix
//          becomes a prefill plus `stop`, with the regex net demoted to a second pass
//   PCP-TOKENIZER-SAME  MEASURED: xialong-v1 and glm-4-6 return IDENTICAL token ids for one
//          sample, so they share a vocabulary. Every tokenizer measurement in this file is
//          already right for either, and budgeting a xialong prompt with the glm tokenizer is
//          not a defect. It also means a decode round-trip cannot name which model ran --
//          the CACHE MISS is what proves that [PCP-MODEL-HONOURED]
//   UTIL-PIN  THE UTILITY CHANNEL STATES ITS MODEL. ceUtilityModel() is the one read and
//          CE_UTILITY_MODEL the one string; wizActiveModel is the STORY's model and is for
//          measuring or riding editor.generate, never for building params.model
//   P-PERSONA  the third head family, and the only one that REPLACES CE_P_CORE instead of
//          sitting under it. Still one assembler: a `family` value, never a second builder
//   CHAT-PARTICIPANT  who the contact is, assembled from five EXISTING builders and placed
//          BELOW the shared prefix, so a chat switch invalidates it and nothing above it.
//          Thick on purpose: the direct transport activates no lorebook entry for us
//   CHAT-HEADERS  CAPITALS FOR INSTRUCTIONS, SENTENCE CASE FOR MATERIAL. A shouted heading is
//          a style instruction whether or not it was meant as one, and the model cannot tell a
//          label it should ignore from a register it should imitate. CE_P_* blocks keep their
//          caps (system voice); blocks 4/5/6 are a cast list, a journal and a dossier and do
//          not. The character's name left the headings too -- said once, at the top
//   REL-ONE-SOURCE  the participant block had TWO relationship projections firing and printed
//          the same standing twice. _ceRelPersonaColour wins on this channel because
//          _ceRelFoldBlock filters to scenePresent others, and a text conversation is precisely
//          the case where the other party is NOT in the room. buildCharDynamicBlock takes
//          opts.skipPosition, absent-means-off, and the chat is its only caller
//   CHAT-THREAD  a message is a RECORD and its rendering is a VIEW. storyStorage,
//          CE prefix STORAGE_CHAT_THREAD_PFX, keyed on charChatEntryId so a thread and its
//          lorebook archive answer to the same name and CC-CTX needs no change. Emotion and
//          attachment are FIELDS; the 8-hex uuid prefix is gone with the document sentinel
//   CHAT-TURNS  block 7, the transcript as REAL messages rather than prose in one user turn.
//          Contact turns keep their name header (matches the prefill) and their Emotion line
//          (the in-context example); POV turns carry no header
//   CHAT-SEND  one turn, TWO CALLS sharing blocks 1-7 byte for byte and differing only after
//          them, which is what makes the second nearly free. Thought first, discarded on
//          failure; nothing persists until both calls are done
//   CHAT-TAIL-NOTADAPTIVE  block 5b's budget is a CONSTANT on purpose. Sizing it from what the
//          other blocks left over would change it every turn, and it sits above the turns, so
//          every message would cold-start the whole prompt -- silently, at 1193 instead of 41
//   CE-BUBBLE  the bubble SHELL, shared by both docks so they cannot disagree about what a
//          message looks like. Geometry only; contents differ entirely
//   CC-ROUTE  the window is a SURFACE ('ccchat' + entityId), not a profile drill. A drill
//          inherits the profile's scrolling screen, and a chat needs a composer pinned under a
//          scroller that owns its height. Off CE_SHELL_ROUTES like 'entity' -- reached from the
//          chat menu, which is where the card's own tap now goes. 'Open in story' keeps the
//          legacy document path reachable beside it rather than deleting a working feature
//   CC-SURFACE  the character chat window. Its own dock, not ceChatDock bent to fit -- that one
//          has one verb and no face, photo or thought. Store created in ceOpenShell
//          [STORE-BEFORE-HOOK]; mounted as profileDrill 'chat' through h() because it hooks.
//          CE_CC_PAGE=32 is the RENDER window and grows on demand; it is unrelated to
//          CE_CHAT_TURN_MAX, and reading further back never sends more to a model
//   CC-PRESS-LABEL  the second verb names what it will actually do -- 'Let them text first',
//          'Nudge', 'Get a reply' -- because ceChatPress reads the transcript rather than the
//          button, and the label is the only place the author sees which meaning is live.
//          Regenerate shows ONLY on the last contact message, because that is the only one
//          _ceChatTurn can pop
//   CHAT-TURN-CORE  send / press / regenerate are one runner and three thin doors. They agree
//          on load, assemble, think, reply, parse, persist and differ in about six lines each
//   CHAT-PRESS  send with nothing to send. The nudge note is decided from the TRANSCRIPT, not
//          from the button: contact-last is a double-text, empty thread is the character
//          opening unprompted, POV-last is just a reply and gets no note. NEVER stored -- a
//          directive persisted as a turn fills the thread with POV messages nobody wrote.
//          Silence needed no new rubric; ceChatThoughtAsk already names it
//   CHAT-REGEN-THOUGHT  regenerate REUSES the discarded message's thought rather than thinking
//          again: the POV message it read has not changed, and the button means the same intent
//          said differently. The discard is in-memory only, so a failed regen keeps the original
//   CHAT-TURN-WINDOW  how many turns reach the PROMPT, which is not how many the view draws.
//          Trimmed in BATCHES of CE_CHAT_TURN_DROP rather than one at a time: a true sliding
//          window shifts every turn and kills the cache on every message past the cap. The trim
//          point is where dropped turns would feed ccSummarize into the journal, not yet built
//   CHAT-ATTACH-INLINE  the photo generates as part of the turn, not staged. A bad prompt
//          therefore costs a real generation, which is what the head's attachment clause earns
//   CHAT-PREFIX  blocks 1-5 in one assembler. Nothing in it singles out the SPEAKER; the cast
//          list naming everyone is fine because it is the same list whichever chat is open
//   CHAT-CAST  block 4, the Dramatis Personae WHOLE, through buildCastLoreTexts' cast half so
//          this channel cannot disagree with the story about who the cast is
//   CHAT-STORY-JRNL  block 5 is the JOURNAL: compressed, curated, and the artifact a chat
//          summary writes into -- so summarising a chat feeds the next one with no second
//          store. Trimmed from the oldest end, budget CE_CHAT_JRNL_TOKENS
//   CHAT-SCENE  block 5b is the LIVE STORY TAIL, and the journal does NOT replace it. The
//          journal is the long arc and is always behind the story by construction; the tail is
//          what is happening. Above the participant because it is shared across contacts AND
//          frozen while chatting. _charchatStoryTextAboveMarker is REUSED, not deleted -- its
//          marker cut is correct both before and after chats leave the document
//   CHAT-ASK-EMPTY  anything constant across a thread's turns belongs above them. The emotion
//          and attachment directives move into the head, and CHARCHAT-TAIL-TERMINAL retires on
//          this channel because nothing conditional sits last any more
//   CHAT-THINK  the thought moves BACK in front of the reply. CHARCHAT-THOUGHT-POST's
//          constraint was the HOOK -- no generate from onBeforeContextBuild -- not the ordering,
//          and off Send there is no hook
//   CHAT-THINK-NONAME  the rubric is second person and byte-identical for every character. The
//          old one interpolated a name into 13 of 15 lines, so nothing above it could cache
//   CHAT-REPLY-TAIL  the reply has NO ask: a prefill assistant turn plus `stop`. Stop is the
//          HEADER form ('\nBen:'), never the bare name, or the reply truncates the first time
//          the character says it aloud. charChatCleanReply is demoted, not deleted
//   CHAT-PARTICIPANT-NOLINE  there is no compact persona line and CE-SOCIAL 7.2 is DELETED.
//          The register clause was already compact (a compile was read as a cost), a cached
//          identity summary would be a second projection buildLorebookContent forbids, and the
//          compact reference already exists as the Dramatis Personae one block up
// HOOKS
//   HOOK-LIFE HOOK-BAND HOOK-TIER  one record on a 1-4 ladder, 0 means gone;
//          three injection bands, no gap
//   HOOK-GATE  present / absent / unknown, and unknown is asked anyway
//   HOOK-PROMPT HOOK-TAIL  built on the ephemeral prompt; the tail is ordered
// TRAVEL AND THE MAP
//   TRAVEL-TIER  which END prices a hop, returned as an id; an interior hop is
//          not travel
//   MAP-SELF   a parent is packed among its own children as one more circle
//   MAP-OUTLINE  depth 1 is always named
//   MAP-SCALE  sibling radii are compressed by a power under 1 BEFORE packing
//   HEX-COLORS  a cell's pt is an index into the shell's palette; the map owns
//          no table
//   HEX-LOCCOLOR  authored if ent.mapColor says so, derived otherwise
//   MAP-INLINE  the three drawing fields are ONE renderer with two hosts
//   HEX-CAMERA  the camera's cell is pinned on the map record (camHex)
//   TRAVEL-MODE  one config value decides which map prices; the other stops
//          quoting
//   HEX-VH   a vehicle is its own layer on the cell (`vh`), and ceHexOf is the ONE
//          resolver for both layers; arriving on its cell BOARDS it
//   MAP-VEHICLE  a mapKind that moves: the hull travels, the camera does not
//   MAP-VEHICLE-HEX  a hull occupies exactly one hex; painting MOVES it, the cell
//          decides its parent, and unpainted ground roots it
//   MAP-KIND  a mapKind that costs nothing: an interior hop is not travel
//   HEX-ICON-ANCHOR  a region's mark sits on a cell and fills one
//   HEX-GESTURE  the heavy half of an inline panel is dropped while a gesture
//          runs
//   HEX-NODECOUNT  SVG node count is the axis; consolidate into path subpaths
//   HEX-RUNAWAY  a per-event rebuild drains after the finger stops
//   HEX-AUTO  the generator PARTITIONS -- only the deepest claimant is written, every
//          cell is claimed, and the sizes come from CE_MAP_KINDS rather than a second list
//   PRIMER  the "?" is a nav SIBLING outside the scroller and opens a full swap, third of
//          that family; topic text is table data, never comments, and its links navigate
//   SPEC-PICK  CharacterRecord.species stays a STRING, not an id
// SURFACES AND LAYOUT
//   COLOR-TABLE  the palette is a frozen array and a colour can be an index into
//          it
//   COLOR-PICK  one colour picker, built rather than the platform's
//   FIELD-LIVE  what is in the box right now, asked of the ELEMENT
//   READ-FIELD  ceReadField -- label over prose, whole row taps to edit; `prompt`
//          is required, because a read view that omits what is missing gives no
//          way to add it
//   TRAIL-NUM  ceTrailNum -- nowrap signed number for a card trail
//   EDIT-SLOT  click-to-edit is the profile-wide standard
//   LIST-DRAFT  a list commits on blur and its buttons fire on click, which
//          arrives first (CLICK-BEFORE-BLUR)
//   LIST-SWEEP  the rule applies to every list editor, not the reported one
//   IMG-MODEL  the NAI model is a setting, not a bridge literal
//   GAL-WINDOW  the gallery renders at most CE_GAL_PAGE cells; the cell count IS
//          the memory cost
//   H-03   a fixed-height image in a flex column is crushed: flexShrink 0
//   H-04   the inline axis was never constrained: minmax(0,1fr), minWidth 0
//   ROW-STACK  a control that cannot shrink takes the row's SECOND line
//   NAV-ONELINE  the nav row's overflow does not zero its ancestors' min-content
//   SCENE-STAGE  the location frame is a wrapper and every image is a leaf
//          sibling inside it
//   SCENE-STAGE-TUNE  four numbers, one table, all the author's
//   SCENE-STAGE-ROW  reserved lanes are gone
//   CHAT-STAGE  the second stage, sharing NO geometry with the first: it draws
//          into NAI's own editor
//   CHAT-STAGE-LANE  the stage switch owns the layout
//   G-04   the Author's Note is shown as sent, sentinel included
//   GUIDE-BODY  a '?' glyph and its body are minted by the same function
//   FIELD-SEED  a field row without a seed writes and shows nothing
//   SELECT-DIRTY  a touched <select> ignores a new `selected`; remount on `cur`
//   STYLE2-SWEEP  token vocabulary; change fsKey/fsMono in the same patch
//   WEB-FLATTEN  the pair record is flat; salience is the per-side tier
//   ICON-LIB   paint is emitted as a style STRING -- the JSX property handler
//          drops SVG-specific properties like fill
//   ICON-FIT   an icon is fitted and centred on `cb` when it has one, one box per
//          SET and never per icon
//   ICON-WEIGHT  `sw` is a stroke in SOURCE units, for line art authored as fills 31b
// DIAGNOSTICS
//   DIAG-CONSOLE-ONLY  EVERY /ce, /log AND /diag COMMAND RUNS FROM THE BROWSER CONSOLE AND
//          NOWHERE ELSE. Verified: the only live caller of aeHandleDiagCommand is the
//          AE_DIAG_CMD bridge case. Typing one into NAI's story box writes it into the
//          story. The invocation, in full, is
//              window.aeBridge.sendToEngine('AE_DIAG_CMD', { cmd: '/ce gen' })
//          and the reply arrives as an AE_DIAG_REPLY console event, not a return value.
//          Any comment or surface text implying otherwise is wrong and is deleted on sight
//   CTX-CENSUS  /ce ctx, /ce cost, /ce cache
//   P-GATE  /ce prompt -- the one command that reads the pipeline's prompts rather than its
//          spending, and the only diagnostic here that costs nothing at all
//   CTX-TRACE  the SEQUENCE, not the pair; slim rows merged with _diagGenLog at
//          report time
//   SB-PREFIX  a prefix is matched from the START of the prompt, and the start is
//          the system turn
//   SB-REPORT  /ce sb -- whether the FRONT moved, the missing half of /ce cost 3b, 15
//   SB-SECTIONID  document.scan() rows are { sectionId, section, index }
//   CTX-FILL   /ce fill -- nonsense to a percentage of maxTokens
//   PANEL-PICK-BLANK  the selftest that would have caught the blank picker
//   GENDIAG  every generation call carries a tag; diagGenRecord is the one sink, and
//          /ce gen is its console door -- it had none until the wizard chat's /gendiag
//          branch was found to be its only live caller 3g
//   SCROLL-ANCHOR  NOTHING IN THIS SANDBOX CAN SCROLL A CONTAINER -- no scrollTop, no
//          scrollIntoView, and onScroll only reports. ANSWERED: a column-reverse flex
//          scroller anchors at its visual bottom natively, which is what CHAT-REVERSE is
//          built on. The Settings > System probe that measured it is DELETED, per its own
//          header -- a probe that outlives its question is a row nobody can explain
//
// ── CAPABILITY WITHOUT A DOOR — the complete list ───────────────────────────
// Anything else that is unreachable is a bug, not an entry here.
//   copyEntity                  wants a button on Entity.
//   runWizardChatTurn, buildWizardStatePayload, buildWizardSourcesPayload,
//   createWizardChat, deleteWizardChat   the Wizard chat generator layer; the
//                               Wizard port calls these directly, not by message.
//   executeAutoSnap             architect-pass auto-snapshot; Image Studio work.
//   ceStoryBlockReset           CLOSED by /ce sb reset [SB-REPORT]. It had none.
//
// ── KNOWN STALENESS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ceEntityWrite(<a character body>, 'parentId', ...) from the Entity surface
// moves a body without calling saveCharacter, so CharacterMeta.currentLocationId
// keeps pointing at the old room and the Entity surface's presentChars and the
// Map's occupants/findables go quietly stale. ceProfileSetLocation (30) is the
// correct verb and does save. Fixing the entity chain means teaching it that
// some entities are bodies.
// [CHARTER-AN] The two 'an'-targeted wizard sections (ATTG, Style) are now the FALLBACK
// behind the charter's Voice tier rather than the Author's Note's source. They are still
// editable on the legacy Memory tab and still assemble, but only for a story whose charter
// has no Voice written. They are not shown on the Build route and are not part of the
// pipeline's question set. The fallback is one line in ceAuthorNoteBody and goes with them.
// Resolutions do not rewind: stateValues live in storyStorage and Undo rewinds
// historyStorage, so an undone turn keeps the damage. onHistoryNavigated is
// registered and is where a continuityId-keyed reversal would go.
// [I-07] The panel's two-press write is gone: ceRollNow calls ceWriteMoveToStory itself.
// The Write button remains rendered for the case that call FAILS (a document error leaves
// sentToStory false), so a press that normally never happens is still reachable. If that
// second path ever proves dead, the button goes -- but a roll whose attempt silently did
// not land is worse than a button nobody uses.
// [EVT-BAND] A band-addressed terminal stores an INDEX, so reordering a def's bands or
// removing one from the middle re-points its directive at whatever now sits at that
// position -- restBand's long-standing hazard, one field over. It is not clamped, because
// clamping would fire the wrong directive rather than none; an index past the end simply
// never matches. The editor labels each field with the band it currently points at, which
// makes a loose directive visible but does not prevent it. The fix is remapping terminals
// inside ceIndexedBands' reorder and remove handlers, which needs that shared editor to
// know which def it is editing.
//
// ── COMMENT DISCIPLINE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Four kinds, and only two survive a pass:
//   KEEP      measured runtime facts — what a probe cost to learn and the
//             typings do not say.
//   KEEP      a decision plus the ONE constraint that forces it. Not the
//             alternatives, not who asked, not when.
//   ONE LINE  superseded theories: the class name, what was tried, that it failed.
//   DELETE    hunt narrative, changelog, status, and "this used to be X".
// WHEN YOU CHANGE CODE, REWRITE ITS COMMENT RATHER THAN APPENDING TO IT. A block
// describing two states has one wrong state in it. The same rule governs this
// index: it is rewritten, never grown.
// ============================================================================

// ============================================================================
// SECTION 1 — TYPE DEFINITIONS
// All interfaces and types used throughout the script.
// UIPart is a built-in NAI API type — no redefinition needed.
// ============================================================================

// ── Character Dynamics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Fields that update during play, representing moment-to-moment character state.


// [JOURNAL] Player Journal — a player-authored running record ("The story so far").
// Bubbles are the source of truth (storyStorage); the lorebook entries are a projection
// of them: non-archived bubbles feed one Always-On entry, archived bubbles a keyless,
// disabled (never-injected) entry that unarchiving restores from.
// [JRNL-STAMP] `storyT` is the IN-WORLD clock minute the entry was written at, beside
// createdAt's real-world ms. SELF-SUPPRESSING: every bubble written before this existed has
// none, and a story whose author never set a clock never gets one, so the line is absent
// rather than wrong. It is never used to sort -- the author's order is the order.
interface JournalBubble { id: string; text: string; createdAt: number; archived: boolean; storyT?: number; }
// [JRNL-SCAN] `scanPerson` IS A PROPERTY OF THE JOURNAL, not a setting. A character's own
// notebook is in the first person for as long as it exists and the story's record is in the
// third; a single global would make one of the two wrong every time the scope picker moved.
// '1' | '2' | '3', absent meaning "whatever this scope defaults to".
interface JournalState { bubbles: JournalBubble[]; activeLoreId?: string; archiveLoreId?: string; scanPerson?: string; }

// [CHARJOURNAL] Per-character journal — same bubble shape as the main
// journal, but no activeLoreId/archiveLoreId: this one has no dedicated lorebook
// entries of its own. Active bubbles fold straight into buildCharDynamicBlock
// (the character's OWN lorebook entry) as one more toggleable field, same
// treatment as equipment/conditions/relationship.
interface CharJournalBubble { id: string; text: string; createdAt: number; archived: boolean; storyT?: number; }
interface CharJournalState { bubbles: CharJournalBubble[]; scanPerson?: string; }

interface EphemeralSnapshot {
    // A snapshot of a character's immediate internal state at one moment in the story.
    // eventAnchor: the specific thing they just perceived or reacted to. Kept
    //   as generation-time scaffolding ONLY (grounds the other two fields in something
    //   real) — not referenced by the npc context template since earlier; the story
    //   text directly above already shows the beat it would restate.
    // emotionalResponse: how it landed, in their own voice. Injected as the
    //   '— thinking: ...' clause (npc template reactionLine macro).
    // immediateDesire: what they intend to do right now. A bare lowercase
    //   verb-phrase FRAGMENT (no capital, no trailing period) — it is embedded into a
    //   pressure-graded sentence by _ceFrameIntent (npc template intentLine macro),
    //   never injected raw. See generateEphemeral's prompt for the exact ask.
    // compressedLine: a fallback single-line summary (composed from all three fields
    //   above, unchanged shape) — still used by CharChat, the bridge HUD/dossier, the
    //   manual override textarea, and other generators' input context. Deliberately
    //   NOT touched by the earlier injection-format change, which is scoped to the AN
    //   npc template only.
    eventAnchor: string;
    emotionalResponse: string;
    immediateDesire: string;
    compressedLine: string;
    generatedAtGen: number; // [E1] was generatedAtTokens
    // [EVIDENCE-STAMP] The beat pass's own pursuit classification, recorded on
    // the snapshot that produced it. Costs zero extra output tokens (the value is already
    // in the parsed JSON) and is the ONLY way to tell a real 'untouched' apart from a
    // malformed/truncated reply, since generateEphemeral coerces anything unrecognized to
    // 'untouched' and 'untouched' writes nothing. A flat pressure tier is otherwise
    // ambiguous between "the model correctly said nothing moved" and "the model failed".
    // Also the evidence set an author prompt reads back at a parked tier. Optional:
    // generateChatEphemeral shares this persist path and never produces one.
    pursuitState?: string;
    // [POSITION] The beat pass's position classification, stamped by the same
    // rule and for the same reason as pursuitState: absent means "no usable answer came
    // back", NOT "the model said nothing moved". Without it a flat position tier is
    // ambiguous between a correct 'untouched' and a malformed reply, and those two have
    // completely different fixes.
    positionState?: string;
}

// [MIDRANGE-RETIRE] MidRangeSnapshot DELETED. The mid-range layer
// (currentDesire / socialMask / shiftedView + a frozen baselineView) was a
// free-writing generator that rewrote three prose fields every N generations. It is
// replaced by the pressure/threshold model: a tier moves on classified evidence and
// only the AUTHOR rewrites the prose. Its one surviving artefact is the salvage in
// migrateCharacter, which folds the last live values into the character journal.
// RelationshipSnapshot was the surviving half of the same earlier pair. [earlier
// POSITION] It retired with the layer it trailed. A change-gated prose trail only
// earns its storage when a GENERATOR rewrites the prose; Position's two fields are
// author-authored and change only when the author changes them, at which point the
// trail would just be an undo history for a textarea. The moving part is the tier,
// and that lives in historyStorage where Undo already reverts it for free.

// ── Character Relationships ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// [F-20] THE CATALOG IS PROSE NOW, BECAUSE THE FIELDS IT SEEDS ARE PROSE.
// ARCHETYPE IS THE SHAPE, STAGE IS THE STANDING — per author, and that mapping is the
// whole design. CharPosition.shape asks what the relationship IS and CharPosition.standing
// asks where it sits right now, so a catalog whose entries were bare LABELS ('Rivals',
// 'Hostile') could only ever seed a category into a field that wanted a sentence. `name`
// stays a label because a picker needs one; `shape` and each stage are the prose that
// actually lands in the record.
//
// NEITHER TOUCHES THE PRESSURE TIER. Per author. Seeding writes words into two textareas
// and nothing else — the tier is arithmetic in historyStorage that Undo reverts, and a
// catalog entry that also moved it would be authoring story state from a dropdown.
//
// STILL A ONE-SHOT SEED WITH NO REFERENCE KEPT. Choosing an archetype COPIES its prose in
// and the copy is immediately the author's, editable and never rewritten. There is no
// stored archetypeId, no stageIndex and no ladder to walk — all three retired with the
// old track (findArchetypeDef / resolveArchetypeStage / rel.stageIndex) and none come back.
// stages[] therefore stays ORDERED cold -> warm for the author's reading only; nothing in
// the engine indexes it.
interface RelationshipArchetypeDef {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    // [F-20] One or two sentences for CharPosition.shape. '' = label-only, which is still
    // a legitimate entry (see 'Custom') and seeds nothing.
    shape: string;
    // [F-20] Ordered cold -> warm. Each is a sentence for CharPosition.standing, NOT a
    // stage name. Empty array = the archetype seeds a shape and leaves standing alone.
    stages: string[];
}

// ── State  [SEC:STATE] ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// [D-19] ONE PRIMITIVE. A bounded integer, optional ordered bands, optional
// attachments. There is no Gauge type and no Track type: a Track is this with bands 1:1
// with its range, and that is a property of the DATA, not a second kind of thing.
//
// THREE INDEPENDENT PROPERTIES, which is why the earlier split was wrong. STORAGE is a
// bounded number. PROJECTION is bands (D-20/D-21). ATTACHMENT is which verbs may move it
// (D-24). Conditions, faction standing, currency and hook pressure differ on those axes
// and on nothing else.

// A LOWER BOUND PLUS A LABEL, ascending by `at`. THRESHOLDS, NOT AN EVEN SPLIT, and the
// choice is load-bearing: an even split makes 1:1 bands fall out for free but cannot
// express Health as dying 0-19 / hurt 20-79 / unhurt 80-100, and retrofitting thresholds
// later is a data migration over every def anybody ever wrote. The first band's `at` is
// treated as the def's min whatever it says, so a band list can never leave a hole at the
// bottom of the range.
//
// THE LABEL IS PROSE, not a word. cfg.conditions' stages already are ('Stumbling, loud,
// uninhibited.') and they migrate into this field unchanged.
interface StateBand {
    at: number;
    label: string;
}

// 'char'        — every character has one
// 'npc'         — a char-scoped def that applies to every character EXCEPT the protagonist.
//                 The exact complement of 'protagonist', and it stores IDENTICALLY to
//                 'char' (on the record, under stateValues) -- the scope is a VISIBILITY
//                 rule, not a storage tier, which is why the read/write verbs need no
//                 branch for it and only the per-character filters do.
// 'protagonist' — a char-scoped def that applies to EXACTLY ONE character, whoever
//                 cfg.protagonistCharId currently names. NOT a separate storage tier:
//                 the protagonist is a character (D-01/D-02) and the value lives on
//                 their record like anyone else's. Changing protagonist leaves the old
//                 holder's values ON THEIR RECORD rather than transferring them --
//                 stated, not fixed, because "whose currency is it" is an authoring
//                 question and inventing a transfer would answer it silently.
// 'world'       — one value, no owner
// 'faction'     — a FACTION owns the value (its unrest, its treasury). A character's
//                 STANDING WITH a faction is not this: it is a 'char' def whose bands are
//                 that faction's ladder, which is what keeps StateRef at one ownerId
//                 instead of a composite. [amends D-23]
// 'event'       — A LIVE ENCOUNTER OWNS THE VALUE, and ownerId is the MILESTONE id. The
//                 def is the encounter's SHAPE (its span, its bands, its per-band
//                 directives) and the value is that encounter's progress through it, so
//                 one authored 'Melee' shape serves every brawl the story ever has without
//                 minting a record per foe. It stores exactly like 'faction' -- a keyed map
//                 in storyStorage, not a field on anything -- and it appears on no
//                 character profile, because ceStateAppliesToChar answers false for it.
type StateScope = 'world' | 'char' | 'protagonist' | 'npc' | 'faction' | 'event';

// [T-07] THE WORLD'S CALENDAR. Vocabulary, not content -- what the days are called and how
// the year is divided, edited in Settings > Time. It sits beside stateDefs / resolutionDefs /
// stakeDefs / standingLadder for the reason those are there: a def is world vocabulary and
// inherits ceSettingsOp's write chain for free.
//   EVERY FIELD IS OPTIONAL AND EVERY ABSENCE IS A WORKING STATE. No dayNames means the world
//   has no week; no months means no date phrase at all. Read through ceCalendar() and never
//   directly -- absent, partial and malformed must resolve identically, which is
//   ceDefaultResolutionId's rule applied to a bigger shape.
//   NO minsPerHour / hoursPerDay. See the header: they are constants, deliberately, because
//   there is no control for them and a knob with no door is the thing F-20 removed.
interface CalendarDef {
    dayNames?: string[];        // LENGTH IS THE WEEK. [] = this world has no week
    months?: { name: string; days: number }[];   // [] = no months, and therefore no date
    bands?: TimeBandDef[];      // ABSENT = the shipped ladder. [] = deliberately silent
    startAt?: number;           // absolute minutes a fresh story begins at
    advancePerGen?: number;     // [T-08] minutes added per story turn. 0 = off = the default
}

// [T-01] A VIEW OVER THE DAY, AND THE REASON THE CLOCK CANNOT BE A StateDef. `to` is
// EXCLUSIVE, and from > to WRAPS past midnight (deep night, 22 -> 5). A StateBand is an
// ascending threshold list with no way to express a range that ends before it starts.
// from === to matches nothing rather than everything: a zero-width band is an authoring
// mistake, and reading it as "always" would silently replace every other band in the table.
interface TimeBandDef { label: string; from: number; to: number; }   // whole hours

// [T-03] The clock, in historyStorage so Undo rewinds it. ONE FIELD -- AE carried a
// gensSinceTick counter beside it, and advancePerGen is applied on the turn rather than
// accumulated, so there is no cadence state left to keep in step with anything.
interface ClockState { t: number; }   // absolute minutes since story zero

// [T-10] ONE NAMED DAY. `rows` is sorted ON READ and never on write, or an author dragging
// an hour selector past its neighbour would have the row jump out from under their thumb.
// A ROW IS A START, NOT A SPAN. The stop that follows it ends it, and the last stop of the
// day WRAPS BACK to cover the head of it (ceSchedSegments) -- so somebody asleep from 23:00
// is asleep at 01:00 without a duplicate midnight row, which is the one thing AE's schedule
// model got right and is kept unchanged.
// [ATMO] `locationId` IS UNREAD ON A LOCATION-OWNED SCHEDULE and is left empty there. A
// place does not travel to itself, so the field has no meaning for that owner; `doing`
// carries the atmosphere text instead. Left in the shape rather than split into a second
// row type, because every reader below treats a row as opaque and splitting it would fork
// ceSchedSegments, ceSchedSegAt and the editor to gain one absent key.
interface DayPlan {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    rows: { fromHour: number; locationId: string; doing: string }[];
}

// [T-10] WHEN A PLAN APPLIES. EVERY FIELD IS AND-ED AND ABSENT MEANS ANY -- the convention
// _ceStateOwner already uses for a missing owner: a working state, never a fault. That one
// predicate covers every case an author asked for, and none of them needs a second shape:
//   {}                           matches every day. NEVER WRITE ONE -- plans[0] is that.
//   { weekday: 3 }               every Wednesday
//   { monthDay: 0 }              every first of the month
//   { month: 2 }                 all of March
//   { month: 2, weekday: 3 }     Wednesdays in March
//   { fromDay: 412, toDay: 419 } an absolute span, for a world with no week at all
// weekday/month/monthDay are ZERO-BASED, matching what ceClockWeekday and ceClockMonth
// return. The editor prints names, so the numbering is never seen.
interface PlanRule {
    planId: string;
    when: { weekday?: number; month?: number; monthDay?: number;
            fromDay?: number; toDay?: number };
}

// [T-10] CharacterRecord.schedule AND EntityRecord.schedule. plans[0] IS THE STANDING DAY
// AND IS UNDELETABLE: resolution must terminate somewhere, and a nullable fallback would
// put a null check in every reader instead of a guarantee in one place.
// TWO LAYERS RATHER THAN MORE ROWS. AE tracked exactly one day per character, so a market
// day meant editing the schedule twice a week by hand. A character who is always in one
// place is still one plan, one stop and zero rules here -- nobody ever fills in a week.
//
// [ATMO] ONE TIMETABLE TYPE, TWO OWNERS. A location's schedule says what the place is LIKE
// at a given hour rather than where somebody is; its rows carry that text in `doing` and
// leave `locationId` empty, because a place cannot travel to itself. The name is a
// character's because that is who had one first, and renaming it would be forty sites for
// nothing: what matters is that one storage shape, one resolver and one editor serve both.
interface CharSchedule {
    enabled: boolean;
    plans: DayPlan[];
    rules: PlanRule[];
    // [T-19] THE PIN, AND THE ONE THING HERE THAT IS NOT A FUNCTION OF TIME. A plan named
    // for a CONDITION rather than a date -- a storm, a siege, a festival -- can never be
    // selected by a PlanRule, because every field of `when` is a calendar field. So it is
    // selected by hand instead: set this and that plan answers for every hour until it is
    // cleared, rules and standing day alike.
    // ON/OFF, NOT A SCHEDULE ENTRY, and deliberately at most one at a time: two pinned
    // conditions would need a precedence rule, which is the thing T-11 refused to build.
    // THIS IS THE HOOK AUTOMATION WILL PULL. When a trigger tier arrives it sets and clears
    // this field and nothing else, so an atmosphere fires off world state without the
    // resolver learning a second selection model.
    activePlanId?: string;
}

// [T-18] A DATED NOTE, AND ITS ONE BEHAVIOUR IS AN INJECTION. That is deliberately the
// whole of it: an entry that FIRED something would be F-16's authored effect tier arriving
// through a side door, and F-16 is deferred on purpose. `day` is absolute, so matching it
// costs no calendar arithmetic and cannot disagree with what the clock says.
interface DatedEntry { id: string; title: string; note: string; day: number; }

interface StateDef {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    scope: StateScope;
    min: number;
    max: number;
    start: number;        // value a fresh owner holds; clamped into [min,max] on read
    bands: StateBand[];   // [] = this value NEVER reaches context [D-21]
    // INDEX into bands, not a value. An index survives a re-threshold (moving Health's
    // "hurt" boundary from 20 to 30 must not change which band is resting); a value does
    // not. It does NOT survive a band reorder, which is why every reader clamps it --
    // the same arrangement CharacterConditionState.stageIndex has always had.
    restBand: number;
    // [F-10] The lorebook line this def writes, as a macro template. Blank uses
    // DEFAULT_STATE_LINE. PER-DEF RATHER THAN PER-SCOPE, which the F-07 design got wrong:
    // faction standing is char-scoped and reads "Marek is Hostile toward the Iron Circle",
    // while Health is char-scoped and reads "Marek is dying". Scope does not predict
    // sentence shape, so scope cannot select the template.
    // Macros: |name| |defName| |bandLabel| |value|
    // [F-11b] THE AUTHOR'S OWN GROUPING. Functionally these are all one thing (D-19) and
    // that is not going to change -- but a resource, a skill and a condition are DIFFERENT
    // THINGS IN THE AUTHOR'S HEAD, and a flat list of eleven rows makes them find that
    // difference again every time they look. Free text, not an enum, for locationType's
    // reason exactly: no closed list survives both a dungeon crawl and a courtroom drama.
    // PURELY PRESENTATIONAL. Nothing reads it but the surfaces, which derive their headers
    // from whatever categories happen to exist -- so an author inventing a category invents
    // a section, and neither the engine nor this file needs to know the word.
    category?: string;
    line?: string;
    // [D-24] 'cost' | 'modifier' | 'spendForBonus' | 'target'. Readers: modifier ->
    // ceStateIsModifier [F-11]; spendForBonus -> ceStateSpendables [F-20]. cost and target
    // are declared and unread -- see the seed library's note on why that is only safe while
    // nothing seeds them.
    attach?: string[];
    // [F-25] SET ON A DEF THE ENGINE OWNS, naming what owns it -- 'faction:<id>' for a
    // standing. A managed def is REGENERATED FROM ITS OWNER ON EVERY CONFIG LOAD, so hand
    // edits to it do not survive; the States tab therefore renders it read-only and points
    // at the door that does edit it. Absent on every def an author made, which is all of
    // them until a faction exists.
    managed?: string;
    // [F-11] What happens when this value reaches a bound. AE expressed this as a chained
    // "final resolution" action that every combat action had to remember to call, which is
    // why death was easy to author once and easy to forget forever after. DECLARED ON THE
    // DEF, so every move that can reduce Health inherits death without knowing it exists.
    // `note` REPLACES the roll band's directive rather than joining it -- at 0 health the
    // outcome is no longer a partial success that also hurt, it is a death, and telling the
    // model both would let it pick.
    terminal?: StateTerminal[];
    // [EVENT] THE WAGER AN ENCOUNTER OF THIS SHAPE CHARGES ITSELF, and the ONLY field here
    // read for one scope alone. It names a StakeDef whose rows move THIS def -- so a Melee
    // shape ships with the table that says what a strong hit takes off it, and no ordinary
    // stake table anywhere else in the story needs an encounter row.
    //   COPIED ONTO THE MILESTONE AT BEGIN, not read live: MilestoneEvent.stakeId is what
    //   the roll path resolves, so retitling or re-pointing a shape mid-fight cannot change
    //   what the encounter already committed to charging.
    //   IGNORED ON EVERY OTHER SCOPE. A character state has a stake table pointed AT it, not
    //   one it owns, and the editor only offers this control on an encounter shape.
    eventStakeId?: string;
    // [G-02] HIDDEN ON A PROFILE WHILE THE VALUE IS STILL ITS STARTING ONE, per author.
    // "Empty" IS `start`, NOT `min`, and the difference is the whole point: Stamina runs
    // 0-5 starting at 5, so an unhurt character is at start and has nothing to say, while
    // "at min" would show the row exactly when it is spent. Untouched is the question.
    //   THE FLAG ONLY EVER HIDES. It does not stop the value existing, injecting, feeding a
    //   roll or reaching a terminal -- ceStateRowsForChar still emits the row and marks it
    //   `hidden`, so anything reading the projection for meaning is unaffected and only the
    //   two surfaces that DRAW it filter.
    hideAtStart?: boolean;
}

// [F-11] [EVT-BAND] ONE OUTCOME, FIRED AT A POSITION IN THE RANGE RATHER THAN AT ITS END.
// A terminal is addressed one of two ways and never both:
//   `band` >= 0  — fires while the value sits in THAT BAND INDEX. `at` is ignored.
//   `band` absent — fires at a bound, and `at` says which end.
// Neither is a literal value, for the reason the bound form has always had: bounds and
// thresholds are author-edited number fields, and a terminal pinned to the literal 0 comes
// loose the moment somebody changes min.
//
// THE BAND FORM IS WHAT MAKES AN ENCOUNTER A STATE. An encounter needs a directive on every
// step of its track -- "they are blooded but still in the fight" -- not only at the end, and
// F-12a already fires terminals ON CROSSING at every write, once per arrival. So the band
// form is not a second mechanism running beside terminals; it is the same mechanism given a
// second way to say where. Nothing about the bound form changes and no stored def migrates.
//
// IT IS NOT EVENT-ONLY. Any def may carry one, and "announce it when Health first drops to
// wounded" is the same feature on a character sheet.
interface StateTerminal {
    at: 'min' | 'max' | 'band';
    // [EVT-BAND] Band INDEX, matching restBand's addressing exactly -- and clamped by no
    // one, because an index past the end of the band list simply never matches, which is
    // the quiet failure the author wants rather than a directive fired at the wrong step.
    // ADDRESSED BY POSITION, exactly as restBand is, and it does NOT survive a band reorder
    // or a mid-list removal -- see KNOWN STALENESS in the index. The editor labels each
    // field with the band it currently points at, so a directive that has come loose reads
    // as obviously wrong rather than firing silently at the wrong step.
    band?: number;
    note: string;          // the GM directive; replaces the band note entirely
    effects?: any[];       // [F-16] reserved: the authored tier is not built yet
}

// [F-12] A ROLL OUTCOME BAND. ITS OWN TYPE, NOT A StateBand, and the reason is not the two
// text fields -- it is that a resolution can touch several states and several things that
// are not states at all (a parentId, a queued directive). A def that describes one bounded
// number cannot describe that, so forcing the two together would have been a name collision
// dressed as a collapse. [supersedes D-36]
//
// TWO TEXTS, TWO JOBS, NEITHER OPTIONAL. `label` is the word the author reads on a surface
// ("Strong hit"); `note` is the directive the model reads ("The goal is achieved, but at a
// cost -- name the cost"). A StateBand's single label serves both jobs because a state's
// band is a DESCRIPTION OF A FACT and reads the same to either audience. An outcome is an
// INSTRUCTION, and an instruction phrased for a human is the wrong text to send a model.
interface RollBand {
    at: number;      // inclusive lower bound on the modified total
    label: string;
    note: string;
}

// [F-12] How a check is rolled and read. 2d6 IS A SEED, NOT A CONSTANT -- dice live in the
// data exactly like bands and bounds do, so d20-with-a-DC, 3d6 bell curves and fudge dice
// are all authored rather than coded.
interface ResolutionDef {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    count: number;   // number of dice
    sides: number;   // faces per die
    bands: RollBand[];
}

// [F-14] ONE SIDE'S CONSEQUENCE IN ONE OUTCOME. A SIGNED NUDGE, NOT A TRANSFER, and that
// distinction is what unblocked this ahead of F-13: AE's whole stake library is
// independent one-sided deltas -- a Miss costs the actor 1 Health and the target gains
// nothing -- so nothing is conserved and there is no two-sided atomic write to guard.
// Transfer remains a real verb for the cases that ARE conserved (paying a bribe); it is
// simply not what a stake is made of.
// A BLANK defId IS THE USUAL ANSWER. Most cells in a stake table do nothing, which is why
// there is no enable switch: an empty cell IS no consequence.
// [F-17] `kind` ADDED, AND IT EARNED ITS PLACE RATHER THAN ANTICIPATING A NEED. Flair had
// no target: Standing is authored prose with no track, so a charming roll could succeed and
// move nothing. Position PRESSURE is the track that already exists -- it is graded, it
// already reaches the model through the position macro, and it already has two termini.
// It is not a StateDef and never will be (it lives in historyStorage, keyed per PAIRING,
// and it is Undo-safe on purpose), so a cell that can only name a StateDef cannot reach it.
// TWO KINDS, NOT AN EFFECT SYSTEM. Absent or 'state' means the historical behaviour, so
// every stored stake keeps working untouched.
interface StakeCell {
    kind?: string;    // undefined | 'state' = StateDef; 'pressure' = the actor's Position with
                      // the target; 'time' = the story clock  [T-20]
    defId: string;    // StateDef id for 'state'; IGNORED for 'pressure' and 'time'. '' = this side pays nothing
    amount: number;   // signed delta. For 'pressure', NEGATIVE IS WARMTH -- see _ceApplyPressure.
                      // For 'time' the unit is MINUTES and negative is refused -- see ceApplyStake.
}

// [F-14] One outcome's row. INDEX-ALIGNED WITH THE RESOLUTION'S BANDS, exactly as
// StateDef.restBand is an index into its own band list -- and clamped by every reader for
// the same reason: an author who deletes a band must not silently re-point the rows under
// it at the wrong outcomes.
interface StakeRow {
    actor: StakeCell;
    target: StakeCell;
}

// [F-14p2] WHOSE KEY DOES THIS PAIRING'S TIER LIVE UNDER? A Position is between two people
// but its tier is stored against exactly ONE of them, and until now the pressure stake
// assumed it was the actor's.
//   THAT ASSUMPTION WAS THE BUG. Every reader in this file keys the tier by the character
//   who OWNS the Position record -- _ceReadPressure(char.id, 'position_' + char.position
//   .targetId), in the attention scan, the profile payload, the context frame and the
//   selftest alike. The stake wrote _ceReadPressure(actorId, 'position_' + target.id). Those
//   agree only when the PROTAGONIST holds the record, and the ordinary authoring pattern is
//   the opposite: Position lives on a character profile, so the NPC holds one pointing back
//   at the player. In that arrangement the roll moved a tier nothing ever read -- the
//   receipt reported a change, the injection never altered, and the terminal never fired.
//   Reported as "nothing happens when the target's pressure hits terminal."
//
// SO THE OWNER IS RESOLVED, NOT ASSUMED, and both directions are accepted: the actor's own
// Position aimed at the target, or the target's aimed back at the actor. Whichever record
// exists is the one charged.
//
// THE AXIS USES THE RAW STORED targetId AND THE MATCH USES THE RESOLVED ONE. This is the
// part that is easy to get wrong twice: an NPC's Position aimed at the protagonist stores
// CE_PROTAGONIST_REF, so every reader builds the axis 'position___player__'. Resolving that
// ref before building the axis would produce 'position_<realId>' -- a second key for the
// same pairing, which is the identical class of bug one layer down.
// [REL-PAIR] COLLAPSED. The two-way search above it existed because a Position was
// stored on ONE of the two characters and the stake could not know which -- so it asked the
// actor's record, then the target's, and returned whichever existed. With a shared pair
// record BOTH sides always exist, so there is nothing to search: find the pairing, then
// decide whose tier the roll moves.
//
// WHICH SIDE PAYS: THE NON-PROTAGONIST ONE. Not "the actor", which is the obvious answer and
// is [F-14p2] all over again. Nothing in this engine READS a tier banked against the player
// -- the protagonist has no lorebook entry to fold a want into and no Author's Note thoughts
// line -- so charging their side writes a number that changes no injection, fires no
// terminal, and reports a movement the story never feels. When neither side is the
// protagonist the actor pays, because they are the one who acted.
//
// THE AXIS IS BUILT FROM THE RAW STORED ID, via ceRelOther, and this is the part that is
// easy to get wrong twice: a pairing with the protagonist stores CE_PROTAGONIST_REF, every
// reader in this file builds 'position___player__' from it, and resolving the ref first
// would mint 'position_<realId>' -- a second key for one pairing, which is this same class
// of bug one layer down.
async function _cePositionAxisFor(actorId: string, target: CeTargetRef | null): Promise<any> {
    var tid = (target && target.id) ? String(target.id) : '';
    var aid = String(actorId || '');
    if (!tid || !aid) return null;
    if (String((target && target.kind) || 'char') !== 'char') return null;
    try {
        var rels = await loadRelationships();
        // The target ref may be a resolved id while the record stores CE_PROTAGONIST_REF (or
        // the reverse), so both encodings are asked for before giving up.
        var rel: any = ceRelFind(rels, aid, tid);
        if (!rel) {
            var aResolved = await ceResolveCharRef(aid);
            var tResolved = await ceResolveCharRef(tid);
            rel = ceRelFind(rels, String(aResolved), String(tid))
               || ceRelFind(rels, String(aid), String(tResolved))
               || ceRelFind(rels, String(aResolved), String(tResolved));
        }
        if (!rel) return null;
        var sideA = String(rel.charA_id || '');
        var sideB = String(rel.charB_id || '');
        var payer = '';
        if (ceIsProtagonistRef(sideA)) payer = sideB;
        else if (ceIsProtagonistRef(sideB)) payer = sideA;
        else payer = (sideA === aid || sideB === aid) ? aid : sideA;
        var other = ceRelOther(rel, payer);
        if (!payer || !other) return null;
        return { charId: payer, axis: ceRelAxis(other),
                 owner: (payer === aid) ? 'actor' : 'target' };
    } catch (e) { api.v1.error('[AE:position] axis resolve:', e); }
    return null;
}

// [F-14] A NAMED WAGER. What is on the line, per outcome, for each side.
//
// IT CARRIES ITS RESOLUTION so the editor can label rows with the outcome words the author
// actually wrote ("Miss", "Partial", "Strong") instead of "Row 1 of 3". Rows still apply by
// INDEX if a move is armed with a different resolution, clamped and reported rather than
// refused -- the same degrade-do-not-break habit ceResolutionDef's fall-to-first has.
//
// defaultForApproach IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE FEATURE, per author: a stake nobody has to
// choose is a stake that gets used. The approach already says what KIND of attempt this is,
// so it is the right thing to hang a default on, and the Actions panel's own control is an
// OVERRIDE of that default rather than a field to fill in from scratch every turn.
interface StakeDef {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    resolutionId: string;
    defaultForApproach: string[];   // approach StateDef ids this is the standing wager for
    rows: StakeRow[];
}

// [F-15a] WHO OR WHAT A MOVE IS AIMED AT. A REF, NOT A TWO-VALUE SIDE ENUM, settled before
// F-13 rather than after: a bribe pays a FACTION and a lockpick opposes a DOOR, so a Side
// that could only say 'player' or 'target' would have to be rewritten the first time
// Transfer met either. `kind` names which namespace `id` lives in.
// [F-31] 'faction' IS PRODUCED NOW. The Target select offers every faction below the cast,
// and a faction-scoped stake cell addresses it directly; ceCommitResolution's involvedIds is
// unaffected because it already tested `kind === 'char'` before loading anybody. 'entity'
// remains a legal value with no producer -- it arrives with the picker (F-15b), and the
// SHAPE not changing when it does is the whole reason this is a ref rather than a side enum.
// `name` rides alongside per CE-STYLE 13: store the id, carry the label, write both in the
// same commit.
interface CeTargetRef {
    kind: string;    // 'char' | 'entity' | 'faction'
    id: string;
    name: string;
}

// [F-15a] A MOVE THE PLAYER HAS PRIMED AND NOT YET SENT. It is not a queue and not a
// history: exactly one may be armed, and NAI's own Send is what fires it.
// tempStorage, session-only, by the same reasoning STORAGE_PENDING_INJECT uses -- an arm
// that outlived a reload would fire against a turn the author does not remember priming.
interface CeArmedAction {
    approachDefId: string;
    resolutionId: string;
    // [F-20] THE BONUS IS BOUGHT, AND THIS IS WHAT IT COSTS. `spendDefId` names a StateDef
    // declaring attach:'spendForBonus'; `spendAmount` is how many of its points this attempt
    // burns, 0..CE_SPEND_MAX, and the bonus to the roll is that same number.
    // THIS REPLACES `situational`, WHICH IS DELETED. That field was a free -20..+20 typed
    // into a control that charged nothing, on a 2d6 table whose bands sit three points
    // apart -- a range three times the dice, costing nothing, on the one surface whose
    // whole claim is that a roll is auditable. Nothing is lost by removing it: a bonus the
    // author simply decides on is a bonus they can also decide by editing the band, and a
    // bonus that matters in play is one somebody paid for.
    spendDefId: string;
    spendAmount: number;
    // [ACT-MODE] THE SIMPLE MODE DIAL, AND IT IS NOT spendAmount. Reusing that field would
    // make a mode switch charge a resource for a bonus nobody bought; a separate field means
    // a stranded value in the wrong mode is simply never read (see ceActionPlan, which is the
    // only reader of either).
    freeBonus?: number;
    target: CeTargetRef | null;
    // [F-14] THREE STATES, NOT TWO, which is why this is a string and not an id-or-null.
    // CE_STAKE_AUTO means "whatever the approach's default is" and is what a fresh arm
    // carries; '' means the author explicitly wants nothing on the line this turn; anything
    // else is a chosen StakeDef id. Collapsing auto and none would make "no default set"
    // indistinguishable from "I am deliberately risking nothing".
    stakeId: string;
    // [EVENT] OFF FOR THIS ONE ROLL. The encounter's own wager is ON by default and rides
    // every attempt while one is running -- that is what makes an encounter something an
    // ordinary action happens INSIDE of, rather than something only a specially-authored
    // stake can touch. This is the exception, per roll, and it is deliberately not an entry
    // in the Stakes control: the base wager and the encounter's are two questions, and
    // merging them would put "is this fight advancing" inside a dropdown about what the
    // actor risks.
    //   IT SURVIVES A ROLL, like the rest of the arm and unlike the spend. Turning it off is
    //   usually a run of attempts that do not advance the fight -- talking, looking for a way
    //   out -- and not a single one.
    eventOff?: boolean;
    // [F-19] THE ATTEMPT, IN THE AUTHOR'S OWN WORDS, TYPED HERE RATHER THAN IN NAI'S FIELD.
    // This reverses SECTION 38's "no text field" rule and the reversal is structural, not a
    // preference: the outcome sentence is COMPOSED with this one and written to the document
    // as a single instruction paragraph, so the engine has to hold the attempt before the
    // document does. The old objection -- "a second story input puts the action out of
    // narrative" -- was true of a panel that only primed; it is not true of one that
    // transcribes.
    actionText: string;
    armedAt: number;
}

// [F-15d] A SAVED MOVE, MINUS THE PARTS THAT BELONG TO THE MOMENT. It carries the four
// settings and the attempt; it deliberately does NOT carry `target`, because a target is a
// person standing in front of you THIS TURN and a saved one would silently re-aim a move at
// somebody who left the room three scenes ago.
//
// [F-20] A SPEND IS NOT CARRIED EITHER, AND IT IS THE SAME RULE AS `target`. The field that
// used to sit here was `situational`, which WAS carried -- correctly, because a free number
// is a standing opinion about a kind of attempt. A spend is not an opinion, it is a
// resource leaving the protagonist THIS TURN, and a preset that re-spends two Luck every
// time it loads is the target bug wearing different clothes. Presets stored before F-20
// keep an orphaned `situational` key that nothing reads, exactly as ae_tracked_narrative
// does -- there is nothing to migrate because a dropped read is not a schema change.
//
// IT LIVES ON THE CONFIG, NOT IN GLOBAL STORAGE, and that is the one decision here worth
// stating: everything a preset NAMES -- an approach StateDef, a ResolutionDef, a StakeDef --
// lives on the config, which is storyStorage. A preset in api.v1.storage would outlive the
// catalog it references and load into the next story as three ids pointing at nothing.
// Image presets go in global storage for the opposite reason: they name no story record.
interface CeActionPreset {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    approachDefId: string;
    // [I-04] NO `resolutionId`. It was one of the four settings a preset carried; the composer
    // no longer offers a resolution, so a preset that pinned one would be a second writer for a
    // value the world already answers -- and loading an old preset would silently re-point the
    // roll at whatever table it was saved against. Presets written before this keep an orphaned
    // key nothing reads; a dropped read is not a migration.
    stakeId: string;
    actionText: string;
}

// [F-11] What one item contributes while worn. ITEMS CARRY STATES, NOT THE REVERSE --
// the reference runs from the entity DOWN into the state catalog, never back up, so the
// catalog stays readable without loading a single entity and a def can be deleted without
// walking the world graph. D-27's one-directional rule; downward is constraint, upward is
// evidence only.
interface ItemBonus {
    defId: string;
    amount: number;
}

// [D-23] ONE ADDRESS. ownerId is '' for world, a charId for char, a factionId for
// faction, and IGNORED for protagonist (resolved through ceProtagonistId).
interface StateRef {
    scope: StateScope;
    ownerId: string;
    defId: string;
}

// [POSITION] PrimaryRelationship DELETED and replaced by CharPosition. The old
// record was six generator-owned prose fields plus a stage ladder; this is two
// author-owned prose fields plus a tier that lives outside the record entirely.
//
// This is ONE SIDE'S READING, not a shared fact. npcWeb holds what is true about a
// pair; Position holds what THIS character privately takes to be true about the other,
// and A's Position on B is free to flatly contradict B's Position on A. That asymmetry
// is the point — it is why Position is per-character and npcWeb is per-pair, and why
// the two deliberately never merged.
//
// targetId is the CharacterRecord id ('__player__' for the protagonist) and is the only
// thing the pressure axis key and the scene-presence gate are built from. targetName
// rides alongside it as the display/context string, because the model needs a name and
// the axis needs an id. A record migrated from the old free-text targetName may resolve
// to no id at all; an empty targetId is a WORKING state, not an error — the Position
// still renders anywhere a name alone is enough, it just earns no tier and no AN line.
// Inventing an id to close that gap would be fabricating a link the author never made.
//
// shape and standing are AUTHOR prose and no generator ever rewrites them. That is the
// entire pressure model held structurally: the beat pass only CLASSIFIES, the tier is
// arithmetic, and the tier grades the FRAMING of the author's own words
// (_ceFramePosition) without ever touching the words themselves.
interface CharPosition {
    targetId: string;    // CharacterRecord id, or CE_PROTAGONIST_REF; '' when unresolved
    targetName: string;  // display/context name; resolved through the protagonist name for CE_PROTAGONIST_REF
    shape: string;       // what this relationship IS, structurally — the standing fact
    standing: string;    // where it stands right now — the movable reading
}

// [HOOK-TARGETID]
// targetId joins targetName here for the same reason CharPosition has both, and it closes
// a REAL LEAK rather than merely tidying the shape. resolveHookTarget returned the literal
// string '__player__' AS THE NAME, and two of its readers decoded that ('Player', 'the
// player') while two did NOT: generateHookUpdates' own system prompt said "tension
// regarding __player__" and the breaking-point toast said "tension with __player__". An id
// column is what makes that impossible -- '__player__' is a value targetName can no longer
// hold, because the id slot is where it belongs.
// SAME SEMANTICS AS CharPosition, deliberately: targetId is a CharacterRecord id or
// '__player__' for the protagonist, '' when the target is someone the cast does not
// contain. AN EMPTY targetId IS A WORKING STATE, NOT AN ERROR -- the hook still renders and
// still escalates, it just names someone the world has no record of.
// MIGRATION IS IN loadCharacter and needs no pass over storage: an absent targetId reads as
// '', and the one legacy encoding that must not survive (targetName === '__player__') is
// rewritten there.
interface UnresolvedHook {
    // ONE character's live tension toward ONE other person. See CharacterRecord.hook.
    id: string;
    targetId: string;    // CharacterRecord id, or CE_PROTAGONIST_REF; '' when unresolved
    targetName: string;  // display/context name — NEVER the raw ref
    description: string;
    // 0-4 tier. RECORD MIRROR of historyStorage's ce_pressure_hook_<charId>, which stays
    // the source of truth; the mirror exists only for the synchronous readers (the
    // injection builders, the surfaces) that cannot await. [HOOK-MIRROR] EVERY writer of
    // the tier writes BOTH halves — the escalator and ceProfileSetTier. A writer that
    // moves one and not the other is the desync this note exists to prevent.
    pressureLevel: number;
    developmentNotes: string[];   // escalator evidence phrases, newest first, cap 3
    // Spawn wording, kept the first time an escalation rewrites `description`, so drift
    // off the original subject stays visible. Rendered on the profile hook card.
    genesisDescription?: string;
}

// ── Character Identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

interface CharacterRecord {
    id: string;
    // [ENT-ID1 DEPRECATED] entityId WAS NEVER DECLARED HERE. It is written and read
    // at ~20 sites on this record and every one of them went through `as any` or an
    // untyped local, so none was type-checked -- the same hole isStatic was declared
    // late to close, found the same way (a tsc error-set diff, this time on new code
    // that dared to touch the field on a properly typed record).
    //
    // It is declared on the way OUT. As of ENT-ID1 it is defined to equal `id` and
    // carries no information; it survives this cycle only so the ~60 existing readers
    // and the bridge payloads that ship it keep working. DELETE IT WITH THE
    // DISCRIMINATED-UNION PASS, together with CharacterMeta.entityId -- one type-level
    // churn instead of two. Do not add a new reader.
    entityId?: string;
    name: string;
    isProtagonist?: boolean;
    conceptDump?: string;
    nicknames: string[];

    // Biographical
    species?: string;
    age?: string;
    gender?: string;
    occupationRole?: string;
    briefDescription?: string;

    // [T-10] Where they are when the story is not watching. ABSENT MEANS THEY KEEP NONE,
    // which is most characters -- so this is optional and never backfilled, and ceSchedOf
    // is the one reader that turns absence into an answer.
    schedule?: CharSchedule;

    // Flags
    scenePresent: boolean;
    active: boolean;
    lorebookEntryId?: string;
    dynamicPriorityRank?: number;  // [vNEXT] 1-based manual priority; unset = unranked. Under a dynamic cap, lower rank = processed first. One character per rank (assignment swaps).

    // Static narrative fields (AI-generated or author-written, infrequently updated)
    physicalDescription?: string;
    voiceAndMannerisms?: string;
    personalityCore?: string;
    background?: string;
    customFieldValues: Record<string, string>;
    coreValues: string[];

    // Image generation — visual identity fields (edited via NAI sidebar; read by bridge)
    visuals?: string;       // base character visual tags — applied in ALL generation modes
    uc?: string;            // base unwanted content — applied in ALL generation modes
    sceneVisuals?: string;  // additional tags for scene illustrations only (auto-snap, director's cut)
    avatarVisuals?: string; // additional tags for avatar portraits only (emotion images)
    avatarWidth?: number;   // avatar portrait width in pixels
    avatarHeight?: number;  // avatar portrait height in pixels
    avatarSeed?: number;    // avatar generation seed — 0 = random

    // [CHARCHAT-PERSONA] Per-character texting register — STRUCTURED, not
    // generated. Every axis is a key into one of the CHARCHAT_*_OPTS tables, and the
    // whole thing compiles to ONE short clause that rides the next-message instruction
    // (buildCharChatPersonaClause). That is the reason it is structured rather than
    // free prose: the instruction block is terse and sits at the true bottom of
    // context, so a generated paragraph would both drown it and be re-paid every turn.
    // `notes` is the escape hatch for anything the axes can't say.
    // Still optional and absent by default — an absent/disabled persona means the
    // global CHARCHAT_DEFAULT_RUBRIC in the system prompt is the whole register, so
    // old saves need no migration fill.
    // [LEGACY earlier] register/voice/content are the OLD freeform shape. Nothing
    // writes them anymore; buildCharChatPersonaClause still READS them so a character
    // authored against earlier-earlier keeps working (they fold in as trailing notes).
    // Delete them once no story in the wild has one — flagged, not silently kept.
    chatPersona?: {
        enabled?: boolean;
        style?: string;      // key into CHARCHAT_STYLE_OPTS
        verbosity?: string;  // key into CHARCHAT_VERBOSITY_OPTS — also drives max_tokens
        emoji?: string;      // key into CHARCHAT_EMOJI_OPTS
        notes?: string;      // free-text extra clause, appended verbatim
        register?: string; voice?: string; content?: string;   // [LEGACY] read-only
    };

    // Dynamic state
    // [REL-PAIR] `position` DELETED FROM THE TYPE. Relationships are pair-addressed
    // now (see CeRelationship); a character holds no side of one. The FIELD still exists on
    // records written before v9 and ceBackfillRelationships still reads it -- through an
    // `any` cast, deliberately, because the type is the statement that nothing NEW should
    // read it while the migration is the one thing that still must.
    // [HOOK-ONE] ONE HOOK, OR NONE. It was an array and the array was a lie: every
    // reader in the engine — escalator, spawn gate, both injection bands, the presence
    // gate, the index — read hooks[0] and nothing else, while the Profile surface let an
    // author add and scan without limit. Hooks 1..n were inert data rendered as though
    // live. Resolution is `hook = null` plus clearing the pressure key, which is why
    // `resolved` is gone: a flag that leaves the record in place froze the character out
    // of ever spawning another. Collapsed by schema v4.
    hook: UnresolvedHook | null;
    // [SEC:STATE] Every 'char' and 'protagonist' scoped StateDef's value for this
    // character, keyed by defId. ON THE RECORD RATHER THAN IN A SIDE TABLE keyed by charId:
    // _ceSceneLines already pays for loadCharacter on every present character every
    // generation, so injection reads cost nothing extra, and a charId-keyed side table is
    // exactly the shape ENT-ID1 spent a migration removing. A missing key reads as the
    // def's `start`, so no backfill pass exists or is needed.
    stateValues: Record<string, number>;
    ephemeral: EphemeralSnapshot | null;
    ephemeralHistory: EphemeralSnapshot[];
    lastEphemeralGen: number;  // [E1] was lastEphemeralTokens
    // [POSITION] relationshipHistory / lastArcGen / lastArcTokens deleted with
    // generateRelationshipUpdate. Position has no cadence of its own: it updates on the
    // beat pass that was already running, so there is nothing left to stamp.
    lastHookGen: number;       // was lastHookTokens
    // Token-mode stamp companions to the *Gen fields above. Written alongside
    // the gen stamps on every update (each generator reads the current token counter at
    // persist time), so both units stay fresh and switching dynamicIntervalMode never
    // fires a spurious cycle from a stamp left in the wrong unit.
    lastEphemeralTokens?: number;
    lastHookTokens?: number;

    // Equipment and resources
    equippedItemIds: string[];

    // Engine flags
    autoThoughtsEnabled: boolean;
    // [FOLLOW-CAM] When set, this character's body is moved to the camera
    // location on every commitTravel, so they stay scene-present wherever the story
    // goes. Placement only -- it does not touch any generator toggle, and presence is
    // still computed by recalculateScenePresence the same way for everyone. A character
    // with no entityId has no body to move and is skipped.
    followCamera?: boolean;
    // [F-4] Opt OUT of being summoned when their lorebook entry key-activates. ABSENT MEANS
    // TRUE, per author, so every existing character is summonable without a migration --
    // and the villain who should not walk in every time he is named gets this set false.
    summonOnMention?: boolean;
    // [PROFILE-SHAPE] Which authoring model the profile surface
    // renders for this character: 'studio' (the six-slot/facet model) or 'classic'
    // (STATIC_FIELD_DEFS + coreValues). The two describe the same person by different
    // means, so exactly one is shown -- rendering both would give the model two answers
    // to every question. Empty/undefined means "never chosen", and _ceResolveShape scans
    // the record instead, the same stored-wins-else-scan arrangement _ceResolveCharPattern
    // already uses for `pattern`. Switching does NOT delete the other model's writing.
    uiShape?: string;
    // [PATTERN-STORED] Explicit pattern identity, replacing pure
    // derivation. Empty string / undefined = "not chosen" (or a earlier record,
    // resolved by _ceResolveCharPattern's legacy scan). One of CE_PATTERN_IDS
    // ('contradiction' | 'wantneed' | 'defense' | 'ruleset' | 'appetite' | 'mask'
    //  | 'collateral' | 'obligation') or ''   -- [PATTERN-8]
    // -- validated against that list wherever it is written, never trusted raw.
    //
    // This DOES NOT retire the scan; it changes which one is authoritative. Storing was
    // withheld through earlier specifically to avoid a field that could drift from the
    // customFieldValues pair it describes. Per-user direction: an author may want to
    // *change* which pattern is active for a character whose resist/conduct text already
    // exists, and a derive-only design has no way to express "yes, keep this pattern" vs
    // "actually, run the mask pattern from now on" -- those are different author intents
    // that collapse to the same customFieldValues state under pure derivation. No
    // mechanical issue was found that requires derive-only; this field is the fix.
    pattern?: string;
    hooksEnabled: boolean;
    // [DYN1] Author's manual "stop generating for this one" switch, read by the
    // dynamic engine and mirrored onto CharacterMeta. Declared late: six sites read it
    // through `(char as any)` for want of this line, so none of them were type-checked.
    isStatic?: boolean;

    createdAt: number;
    updatedAt: number;
}

interface CharacterMeta {
    // Lightweight index entry for each character — used for list rendering and fast lookups.
    id: string;
    name: string;
    isProtagonist?: boolean;
    entityId?: string;   // [ENT-ID1 DEPRECATED] always === id. Delete with the union pass.
    scenePresent: boolean;
    active: boolean;
    briefDescription?: string;
    species?: string;
    age?: string;
    occupationRole?: string;
    lastThought?: string;         // mirrors ephemeral.compressedLine
    currentLocationId?: string;
    equippedItemNames?: string[];
    isStatic?: boolean;            // [DYN1] mirrors CharacterRecord.isStatic for card render
    dynamicPriorityRank?: number;  // [vNEXT] mirrors CharacterRecord.dynamicPriorityRank so the cap sorts without a storage read
}

// ── World-Building Support ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// [FIELD-GROUPING] `slot` and `kind` are now DECLARED. migrateConfig's facet
// seeder has written both onto every entry since earlier and several consumers already
// read them (the native sheet's field catalog casts to any to do it), so the interface was
// simply lying about the shape. Optional because an author-defined field from Settings has
// neither, and ceStaticFieldGroup() treats that absence as "ungrouped" rather than as a bug.
interface CustomFieldDef { id: string; label: string; placeholder?: string; instructions: string; slot?: string; kind?: string; }

// [F-32] SpeciesDef DELETED. A species is an EntityRecord now -- id, name, description,
// lorebookEntryId and lorebookTags all exist there and mean the same things, and the record
// carries a catalog, custom fields, per-field generation, image slots and a field order
// besides. Removed rather than kept as documentation, exactly as FactionDef was: a declared
// shape with no storage behind it is what made relationshipStates look alive for five
// versions. GlobalConfig.species goes with it.

// [F-28] FactionDef DELETED. A faction is an EntityRecord now -- id, name, description,
// lorebookEntryId, lorebookTags and standingLadder all exist there and mean the same things.
// The interface is removed rather than kept as documentation: a declared shape with no
// storage behind it is the exact thing that made relationshipStates look alive for five
// versions. GlobalConfig.factions goes with it.

// [CE-STRIP earlier] NpcArchetypeDef / ItemArchetypeDef interfaces removed — Bestiary + Item Templates cut.


// ── Entity World Graph ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// [CE-STRIP earlier] EntityConnection trimmed to awareness/adjacency only —
// requiresResourceTag/resourceCost/isSilent/requiresVehicle/travelActionId were
// all Actions/Travel-cost fields with no meaning once those systems are gone.
interface EntityConnection {
    targetId: string;
    label: string;
    oneWay?: boolean;
}

// [MILESTONE] THE NEXT MOST IMMEDIATE THING, and only ever one at a time. A list of
// pending milestones would be a task manager; the current one is the thread's whole live
// state, and the archive behind it is what the story already did about this thread.
//   `targetId` IS ON THE MILESTONE, NOT THE THREAD. A thread often has no location --
//   "someone must answer for something" is not anywhere -- but the immediate objective
//   almost always does, so a proximity read against this is a true statement about the next
//   step where one against the thread was a guess about the whole arc.
// [EVENT] AN ENCOUNTER IS A MILESTONE WITH A TRACK ON IT. Not a record type, not a roster,
// and deliberately not a set of temporary characters: three raiders are a STRING in the
// milestone's own text plus one bounded number, so concluding the fight costs a delete of
// nothing and the model is never asked to keep four half-dead NPC sheets straight.
//
// THE FIELDS THAT DESCRIBE IT ARE ALREADY ON THE MILESTONE. `text` is the encounter's
// description and `targetId` is where it is happening; both predate this and mean the same
// things. This sub-object adds only what a milestone could not already say: which SHAPE
// grades it, and which stake table charges it.
//
// shapeDefId NAMES A StateDef OF scope 'event'. That def owns the span, the bands and the
// per-band directives -- so the encounter's whole vocabulary is authored once in
// Settings > States and reused, and an author who wants a different fight authors a
// different shape rather than filling in a form per brawl.
//
// stakeId IS APPLIED IN ADDITION TO THE MOVE'S OWN, NOT INSTEAD OF IT. The base wager
// charges what the attempt costs the actor -- their Health, their Stamina, their spend --
// and this one moves the encounter's track. They are two tables because they answer two
// questions, and merging them would make every approach's default wager depend on whether
// a fight happened to be running.
interface MilestoneEvent {
    shapeDefId: string;
    stakeId: string;
    startedAt: number;
    startedStoryT?: number;
}
interface Milestone {
    id: string;
    text: string;
    targetId?: string;
    createdAt: number;
    storyT?: number;
    resolvedAt?: number;
    resolvedStoryT?: number;
    // [EVENT] Absent on every milestone anyone has ever written, which is what makes this
    // additive: no event means the historical milestone, unchanged in every reader.
    event?: MilestoneEvent;
    // [EVENT] Present only while `event` is. Kept as its own field rather than inside the
    // sub-object so a reader that only wants "is one running" never has to unpack it.
    eventArmedAt?: number;
    // [EVENT] THE BAND LABEL AS IT STOOD WHEN THE ENCOUNTER ENDED, stamped once at the
    // archive and never live. The LIVE line is DERIVED on every read (ceEventStateLine), so
    // editing a shape's band labels retitles every running encounter and there is no second
    // copy to go stale -- but an archived encounter has no track left to derive from, and a
    // history that silently re-reads today's bands would rewrite what happened. Same
    // arrangement JRNL-STAMP has, and self-suppressing for the same reason.
    stateLine?: string;
}
interface ThreadMilestones {
    v: number;
    threadId: string;
    current: Milestone | null;
    // NEWEST FIRST. The renderer shows them in this order and the generator reads the
    // first few, so the sort lives at the write and neither has to know about it.
    archive: Milestone[];
}

interface EntityRecord {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    // [F-28] 'faction' JOINED THE UNION and cfg.factions is gone. A faction was a config
    // row with a hand-written name box while a location was a record with custom fields,
    // per-field generation and a synced lorebook entry -- and every attempt to close that
    // gap on the faction screen rebuilt machinery this type already had. It is an entity
    // now, so it inherits all of it by being one.
    // [F-32] 'species' JOINED THE UNION and cfg.species is gone, on F-28's argument
    // repeated word for word one type over: a species was a config row with a name box and
    // a description box while a location was a record with custom fields, per-field
    // generation and a synced lorebook entry. It is an entity now, so it inherits all of
    // that by being one.
    type: 'location' | 'item' | 'narrative' | 'character' | 'faction' | 'species';
    parentId: string | null;
    narrativeParentId?: string | null;
    // [NARR-KIND] Which of NARRATIVE_KINDS this thread is. It steers the five lenses'
    // hints and generator instructions and owns nothing else, which is why it is freely
    // re-selectable: no answer is keyed on it.
    narrativeKind?: string;
    // [MILESTONE] What the thread's own generators are allowed to read: character ids and
    // entity ids, in one array. ONE LIST AND NOT TWO, because the question the author is
    // answering is "what is this thread about" and splitting it by record type would make
    // them answer it twice. Resolved against both indexes at read time.
    contextIds?: string[];
    storyState?: 'locked' | 'active' | 'completed';
    description: string;
    // [LOC-PROFILE] WHAT THIS PLACE IS, in the author's own words -- 'tavern',
    // 'region', 'hollow', 'orbital', 'dream'. FREE TEXT AND NOT AN ENUM, because the one
    // thing that has to survive any genre and any scale is the word for the thing, and no
    // closed list survives both a suburban house and a continent.
    //
    // IT IS NOT mapKind AND MUST NOT BE DERIVED FROM IT. mapKind is a drawing instruction
    // for the world map and carries no narrative claim; this is the noun the story uses.
    // A place can be drawn as a 'structure' and be a shrine, a prison or a wreck.
    //
    // ITS JOB IS THE IDENTITY LINE -- "<name> is a <locationType> inside <parentName>" --
    // which is cheap, true at every scale, and does most of the work of situating a place.
    // Four of those live together as you descend a nest and the whole chain reads.
    locationType?: string;
    // [LOC-PROFILE4] Raw seed material, matching CharacterRecord.conceptDump
    // exactly -- not injected anywhere, read by every generator on the surface. The
    // generation shim already fed the record's written fields as "reference notes"; this
    // is the same slot for material that is not a field answer yet.
    conceptDump?: string;
    // [LOC-PROFILE4] 'classic' | 'builder'. Which authoring model the surface
    // renders, mirroring CharacterRecord.uiShape and for the same reason: two ways of
    // describing one place, exactly one shown, because rendering both gives the model two
    // answers to every question. Classic is the single lorebookText field and its
    // generator -- the shape every existing location was authored in. Absent means
    // builder, and the migration below resolves an existing record by scanning it.
    uiShape?: string;
    // [LOC-PROFILE4] The author's ordering of builder fields, by def id. IT IS
    // ALSO THE ORDER THE LOREBOOK ENTRY COMPILES IN, which is the point: what a place
    // leads with is an authoring decision and the catalog's declaration order cannot know
    // it. Ids absent from this list sort after it in catalog order, so a field added to
    // the catalog later appears rather than vanishing.
    fieldOrder?: string[];
    // [LOC-PROFILE5] WHO OWNS THIS PLACE, as a reference rather than as prose.
    // The Control lens answers who is welcome and what happens to someone who should not
    // be here; that is a paragraph and cannot be followed by anything. This is the one part
    // of the answer the engine can act on: an id it can resolve.
    //
    // NO ownerName IS STORED. SS13's reference rule asks for id-and-label together, and
    // that rule exists because a picker may name something the world does not have a record
    // for -- a character outside the cast, typed free. Both kinds here ARE records, so the
    // name is resolvable at every read and a stored copy could only ever disagree with it.
    // That is the ENT-ID1 lesson applied one field down: a second copy of a name is a
    // staleness chain, and one that renames itself when the owner is renamed is better.
    ownerId?: string;
    ownerKind?: string;   // 'character' | 'faction' — which table resolves ownerId
    // [LOC-PROFILE] The facet bag, matching CharacterRecord.customFieldValues
    // exactly -- same shape, same generator, same storage discipline. Keyed by
    // LOCATION_FACETS id. One implementation: there is no second custom-field mechanism.
    customFieldValues?: Record<string, string>;
    // [SEED-DURABLE] The pipeline's one-line steer per question, keyed by the SAME facet id
    // customFieldValues uses. PROMPTS 19.1: the seed goes ABOVE the field's instruction and
    // THE INSTRUCTION IS UNTOUCHED -- ceCustomFieldSeedInstr's rule is that the author's own
    // text wins wherever there is any, so writing a seed into `instructions` would delete a
    // carefully written facet instruction the first time a record went through the pipeline.
    //
    // IT IS A RECORD FIELD AND NOT THE WIZARD'S HINT BOX, and that distinction cost a
    // question. `_ceWizHints` is a MODULE VAR, explicitly session-scoped -- its own header
    // says persisting it "gave it a lifetime nothing wanted" -- because it describes THIS
    // press rather than a property of the record. A seed is the opposite: it is the shortest
    // true statement about this record, it survives a reload, and it is what every later
    // generation of that field steers from.
    fieldSeeds?: Record<string, string>;
    // [LADDER] Which rung of the charter's ladder this place sits at. Locations only.
    // DEFAULT, NEVER DERIVED (WORLDPIPE 6.3): a record made by expanding a parent takes the
    // parent's next rung down, and one tap changes it. Absent reads through the same
    // normalise-to-default path an absent mapKind does, so nothing migrates.
    rung?: string;
    // [CE-STRIP LOC-PROFILE] `notes` DELETED. Declared, defaulted, migrated, and
    // read by exactly nothing outside the generation shim's note dump. Whatever it was for
    // is what the facets are for now.
    //
    // [ENT-AUDIT] `state` and `stateDescriptions` DELETED. state's ONLY WRITER WAS
    // migrateEntity DEFAULTING IT TO 'default'. No surface could change it, and both
    // readers of stateDescriptions were guarded by state !== 'default' -- a condition that
    // could not be true. The mechanism was not undriven, it was unreachable: the readers
    // survived a cut the writer did not, which is worse than dead code because it reads as
    // live.
    //
    // [ATMO] THE SCHEDULE IS WHAT REPLACED THEM, and it is a reimplementation rather than a
    // restoration -- which is why neither field came back and why the word "state" is not
    // used for it anywhere. That word is spoken for: a state is a bounded integer with
    // bands (SEC:STATE), and a place's mood is prose. `atmosphere` was the OTHER deleted
    // field here and its name is reused deliberately, because what it was always meant to
    // be is what this is.
    //
    // WHAT IT IS: what this place is LIKE at a given hour -- "the common room is loud and
    // the fire is banked", "shutters down, nobody about". It is the ONE thing about a
    // location that reaches the Author's Note (an.place), and `description` no longer does:
    // that is the gazetteer field now and feeds the World lorebook entry alone.
    //
    // WHY A SCHEDULE AND NOT A FIELD: a place is not one thing all day. Rows give it an
    // hour-by-hour reading, plans give it named conditions (a storm, a siege) and
    // activePlanId pins one of those on regardless of the clock. That is a timetable, and
    // the file already has one -- so this is CharSchedule, unchanged, with `doing` carrying
    // the text and `locationId` unread. See ceAtmoFor for the read and DayPlan for the
    // shape note.
    //
    // ONLY LOCATIONS ARE ASKED. Nothing stops the field existing on another type; nothing
    // reads it there, and no surface offers it.
    schedule?: CharSchedule;
    connectedEntityIds: EntityConnection[];
    // [ENT-ID1] presentCharacterIds DELETED. Declared, defaulted, null-filtered,
    // copied, and counted into EntityMeta.presentCharacterCount -- and never written by
    // anything. Presence is computed by recalculateScenePresence off the body tree and
    // has been for as long as that function has existed. It looked alive because both
    // ends touched it; there was no writer in the middle. Same shape of finding as the
    // journal broadcast, and found the same way: ask who WRITES, not who references.
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] actions / actionInstances / rules / skillBonuses / showActionWidget
    // removed — Actions system cut.
    // [ENT-AUDIT] tagSubscriptions DELETED -- defaulted in migrateEntity, read
    // nowhere. Its only other appearances are on npcArchetypes/itemArchetypes objects
    // built by the wizard commit path, whose INTERFACES WERE DELETED IN AN EARLIER
    // CE-STRIP; that branch is still live and still writing into a shape nothing declares.
    // Flagged, not cut here: retiring the Bestiary and Items wizard topics is a product
    // decision, not a field audit.
    isEquipped?: boolean;                  // [WP-5] item equip state
    equippedToCharId?: string;             // [WP-5] which character an equipped item is on
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] isVehicle/gated/entryCostTag/entryCostAmount/entryActionId removed — dead Actions/Travel remnants, no enforcement guard reads them anymore.
    // [ENT-AUDIT] inventoryIds DELETED. Declared, defaulted, and swept by the
    // ENT-ID1 id migration -- which was the only code that ever touched it. Containment is
    // parentId; this was a second containment model that never got a reader.
    // [F-28] THE FACTION'S OWN STANDING LADDER, or absent to use cfg.standingLadder. Moved
    // here verbatim from FactionDef. READ IT THROUGH ceStandingLadderFor, never directly.
    standingLadder?: string[];
    // [F-30] WHICH RUNG IS SILENT, AND WHICH RUNG A STRANGER STARTS ON. Indices into the
    // ladder in force, absent to inherit the world's, and the world's absence is the middle
    // rung -- which is the value ceSyncStandingDefs hard-coded before these existed, so a
    // story that touches neither behaves exactly as it did. READ THEM THROUGH
    // ceStandingRungFor, never directly: the inheritance and the clamp both live there.
    standingRest?: number;
    standingStart?: number;
    lorebookEntryId?: string;   // [WP-1] linked NAI lorebook entry id (entity profile)
    lorebookTags?: string[];    // [WP-1] extra trigger keys beyond the entity name
    lorebookText?: string;      // [WP-1] dedicated extended-lore body (NOT description/state)
    locationVisuals?: string;   // [WP-img] image tags for location-specific renders (gated; not the general scene prompt)
    // [ENT-IMAGE] The render shape for this entity's own image, mirroring
    // CharacterRecord.avatarWidth/avatarHeight exactly -- a place uses the SAME image
    // architecture a character does, it just wants one picture instead of twenty-six.
    // Absent means the landscape default (a place is usually wider than it is tall), which
    // is the one thing that differs from the character default of square.
    imgWidth?: number;
    imgHeight?: number;
    // [ENT-AUDIT] eventDayTarget DELETED. One reference in the whole file: this
    // declaration. No writer, no reader, no factory default, no migration guard. The
    // comment described an AN countdown that does not exist.
    icon?: string;           // emoji or short glyph shown in bridge entity pickers and map nodes
    // [MAP-KIND] 'region' | 'structure' | 'interior' | 'hidden' | 'vehicle'.
    //
    // [MAP-VEHICLE] THIS FIELD IS NO LONGER DRAWING-ONLY, AND THE CLAIM THAT IT WAS HAS
    // BEEN FALSE SINCE 'hidden' SHIPPED. The line here used to read "it gates no travel, no
    // injection, no containment"; 'hidden' already gates injection (the World outline skips
    // it) and geometry (ceMapBuildTree skips it before packing). 'vehicle' gates which end
    // of a journey moves, and 'interior' gates whether a hop is charged at all.
    // The honest statement is the narrower one: MOST VALUES ARE PAINT, AND THE ONES THAT
    // ARE NOT SAY SO AT THEIR ENTRY IN CE_MAP_KINDS. That table is the register.
    //
    // A VALUE RATHER THAN A SECOND FIELD, for the reason MAP-HIDDEN gives at its own entry:
    // ceMapKindOf normalises anything it does not recognise to 'region', so widening the
    // allowed set needs no migration and no backfill, while an `isVehicle` boolean on
    // EntityRecord needs a migrateEntity line that someone will one day forget. There WAS
    // such a boolean once -- CE-STRIP removed it as a dead Travel remnant -- and it is not
    // coming back.
    //
    // IT IS A FIELD RATHER THAN A FUNCTION OF DEPTH, and the reason is the one thing the
    // tree cannot know: SCOPE. Depth 0 is a continent in one story and a suburban house in
    // the next, so "depth 2 means a building" holds only for hierarchies that happen to be
    // six levels deep. Deriving it would have quietly mis-drawn every story that is not
    // epic-scaled, and nothing in the record says which kind of story this is. The author
    // knows; the data does not.
    mapKind?: string;
    // [HEX-LOCCOLOR] THE AUTHORED REGION COLOUR, and it is OPTIONAL IN THE STRONG SENSE:
    // absent means derive, not grey. A literal '#rrggbb' per CE-STYLE SS3.1's carve-out --
    // a colour an author chose is content, so no theme may restate it, where the derived
    // colour it overrides is chrome and stays a slot. Normalised in migrateEntity and
    // DELETED when junk rather than defaulted, on travelTier's rule one field along.
    mapColor?: string;
    // [F-32 SPEC-LIST] WHICH LIST THIS SPECIES IS ON: 'people' | 'bestiary'. THE ONLY
    // THING THAT DIFFERS between a playable kind and a monster, which is why it is one
    // field and not two record types -- two types would fork the catalog row, the group
    // order, the custom-field config key and the noun, to express a difference the records
    // themselves do not have.
    //
    // A VALUE RATHER THAN A BOOLEAN, for the reason MAP-KIND gives at its own entry:
    // ceSpeciesList normalises anything it does not recognise to 'people', so widening the
    // set later (a third list -- spirits, constructs, the dead) needs no migration and no
    // backfill, while an `isBestiary` boolean needs a migrateEntity line someone will one
    // day forget.
    //
    // IT IS NOT locationType. That field is the NOUN the story uses ('beast', 'people',
    // 'undead'); this is which of the two composed lorebook entries the record compiles
    // into. An author can write a 'people' that lives on the bestiary and the record is
    // correct both times, because the two answer different questions.
    speciesList?: string;
    // [SEC:TRAVEL] WHAT SCALE THIS PLACE SITS AT for travel pricing, overriding its
    // literal depth in the location tree. Absent means depth, which is correct for a world
    // whose nesting IS its geography and wrong the moment one branch is drawn finer than
    // another: a city with its rooms mapped sits four levels down while the moor beside it
    // sits at one, and nothing in the tree says that crossing them should cost the same.
    //
    // IT IS NOT mapKind, and the two do not collapse into each other even though mapKind
    // now has a say in pricing. THIS FIELD IS THE SCALE OF THE PLACE; mapKind:'interior'
    // ZEROES THE HOP. A cabin on a ship halfway across the world still sits at whatever
    // tier its author gave it -- that tier is what prices every hop into the hull that
    // holds it -- and stepping through its door is still free. Two different questions,
    // two different fields, and neither is derivable from the other.
    //
    // ABSENT AND 0 ARE DIFFERENT AND MUST STAY DIFFERENT. 0 is a real tier -- the coarsest
    // one -- so migrateEntity normalises this field without defaulting it. Defaulting to 0
    // would price every unedited location in the world at the most expensive rung its
    // author wrote, silently, on upgrade.
    travelTier?: number;
    // [F-11] Item only. What this thing adds to its wearer's rolls, by def id. Read only
    // for entities in the wearer's equippedItemIds -- carrying it is not wearing it, and a
    // sword in a chest modifies nobody.
    bonuses?: ItemBonus[];
    createdAt: number;
    updatedAt: number;
}

interface EntityMeta {
    // Lightweight index entry for each entity node in the world graph.
    id: string;
    name: string;
    type: string;
    parentId: string | null;
    // [LOC-PROFILE] Propagated so the identity line and the locationType picker's
    // vocabulary harvest need no full record read -- the same reason mapKind is here.
    locationType?: string;
    narrativeParentId?: string | null;
    // [TAB-STORY] Propagated so the thread list can print a kind without a record
    // read, the same reason locationType is here. Undefined on every other type.
    narrativeKind?: string;
    storyState?: string;
    childCount: number;
    // [ENT-ID1] presentCharacterCount DELETED with presentCharacterIds -- it was
    // the only consumer of that array and had no consumer of its own.
    // [ENT-AUDIT] state DELETED with the record field. Propagated on every save,
    // read by nothing.
    icon?: string;           // propagated from EntityRecord for bridge display
    mapKind?: string;        // [MAP-KIND] propagated so the map needs no full record read
    // [HEX-LOCCOLOR] Propagated for the reason directly above and no other: the hex map
    // resolves a colour for every region on every repaint, and a world of two hundred places
    // would be two hundred storage reads a frame.
    mapColor?: string;
    // [F-32 SPEC-LIST] Propagated so the Species tab can split its two lists off the index
    // alone, the same reason mapKind and locationType are here. Undefined on every other
    // type.
    speciesList?: string;
    // [SEC:TRAVEL] Propagated for the identical reason one line up: a quote prices every
    // hop of a whole path and is recomputed on every map selection, so it reads the index
    // and never a record. A world of two hundred places would otherwise be two hundred
    // storage reads per tap.
    travelTier?: number;
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] gated/isVehicle removed from EntityMeta — dead Actions/Travel remnants.
}

// ── Cast & World lorebooks  [CASTLORE] ────────────────────────────────
// TWO ALWAYS-ON ENTRIES the engine composes from records the author has already
// written. Same projection shape as JournalState: bubbles/records are the source of
// truth, the lorebook entry is a VIEW of them, and the id lives here so a hand-deleted
// entry is silently remade rather than duplicated.
//
// EXCLUSIONS LIVE ON THIS RECORD, not on the characters and locations they name. They
// are a fact about ONE LIST's contents, not about the record being left out -- the same
// person can be off the roster and still perfectly active -- and putting a
// `showInDramatis` flag on CharacterRecord would have made two unrelated concerns share
// a field, plus a migration on every stored character to add it.
//
// lastCastText / lastLocText ARE THE DIRTY CHECK AND THEY EARN THEIR STORAGE. The sync
// runs off the presence choke point, which fires on every scene move; without a
// comparison against what was last WRITTEN, an unchanged cast would rewrite two lorebook
// entries every time the camera moved a room.
// [F-32 SPEC-LORE] FOUR ENTRIES NOW, NOT TWO, and the two new ones are a THIRD AND FOURTH
// entry rather than one with two headings -- because the record's own speciesList toggle
// exists to say which list a species is on, and folding both lists into one entry would
// reduce that toggle to a choice of heading.
// THEY RIDE THE 'world' HALF, not the 'cast' half. Their input is EntityRecord.description,
// written through saveEntity and nowhere else -- exactly the geography entry's input and
// exactly its choke point. They do NOT depend on presence and must not be rebuilt when the
// camera walks through a room.
interface CastLoreState {
    castOn: boolean;         // roster entry enabled. Default true.
    locOn: boolean;          // geography entry enabled. Default true.
    pplOn?: boolean;         // [F-32] peoples entry enabled. Absent means true.
    bstOn?: boolean;         // [F-32] bestiary entry enabled. Absent means true.
    // 0 = no limit (the whole active cast). N>0 = only characters whose N-th-from-root
    // ancestor is the same as the camera's. 1 is "same top-level region".
    castTier: number;
    // Depth cap for the geography outline, counted from the root. 0 = no limit.
    locDepth: number;
    castExclude: string[];   // character ids never listed
    locExclude: string[];    // entity ids never listed (their children still can be)
    pplExclude?: string[];   // [F-32] species ids never listed on the peoples entry
    bstExclude?: string[];   // [F-32] species ids never listed on the bestiary entry
    castLoreId?: string;
    locLoreId?: string;
    pplLoreId?: string;
    bstLoreId?: string;
    lastCastText?: string;
    lastLocText?: string;
    lastPplText?: string;
    lastBstText?: string;
}

// ── Relationships and Knowledge ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// [REL-PAIR] ONE RECORD PER PAIR, AND CharPosition IS FOLDED INTO IT. This was
// two systems -- the npc web's link record (a shared, flat, bilateral fact) and CharPosition (each
// side's private tiered read, stored on the character record) -- and they described the
// same thing from two storage locations.
//
// THE MERGE DIRECTION IS INTO THE PAIR, NOT ONTO THE CHARACTER, and that is the whole of
// the decision. `shape`, `standing` and `together` are true for both parties, so a copy on
// each character record would be two homes for one truth and they drift on the first edit.
// The target's Relationships screen does not hold a copy; it FINDS this record, because the
// record is addressed by the pair.
//
// THE ASYMMETRY MOVED, IT DID NOT DIE. CharPosition's whole argument was that A's read of B
// may contradict B's read of A. That now lives in exactly two places: `wants` (what each
// side is after, keyed by character id) and the pressure tier (per side, in historyStorage).
// The prose the two of them share is shared; the thing each of them is privately chasing is
// not. That split is sharper than the old one, where two authored paragraphs were free to
// disagree with no mechanism ever noticing.
//
// NO LONGER NPC-ONLY. CE_PROTAGONIST_REF is a legal charA_id/charB_id. The old web filtered
// the protagonist out and the injector gated on presentMetas (which excludes them), so a
// link naming the player could never inject at all -- while the Drama generator's own prompt
// invited exactly that pairing. The commonest relationship in any story was the one this
// record could not express.
//
// `id` IS KEPT AND THE PAIR IS THE ADDRESS. Two facts, not a contradiction: every lookup
// goes through ceRelFind on the unordered pair, so there is one derivation for "which record
// is this", and `id` survives only because mergeArrayById is what world-pack import merges
// on. Minting a second addressing scheme would be the thing to avoid; keeping the one the
// importer already uses is not that.
interface CeRelationship {
    id: string;
    charA_id: string;
    charB_id: string;
    // Shared, and none of these is per-side. `shape` is what the relationship IS,
    // `standing` is where it sits right now, `together` is what happens in the room when
    // both are present. shape/standing fold into ONE of the two characters' lorebook
    // entries; `together` is an Author's Note line. See [REL-INJECT].
    shape: string;
    standing: string;
    together: string;   // was `dynamic` -- renamed in the same patch that merged the records
    // [REL-WANT] PER-CHARACTER, keyed by CharacterRecord id (or CE_PROTAGONIST_REF).
    // This is what the pressure tier is fed from and aimed at: "what does this side want out
    // of this relationship" is a question a beat can be judged against, where "where do they
    // stand" is a temperature nobody can classify from one turn of prose. A missing key reads
    // as '' and earns no tier, exactly as an unresolved targetId used to.
    wants: Record<string, string>;
    // [WEB-FLATTEN] priority DELETED. It was npcWeb's own private pressure
    // ladder (0-100, default 30) and it drove a SECOND injection: links at 70+ were
    // spliced into the Imperative block by onContextBuilt on top of the Author's Note
    // line every link already got, so a high-priority pair reached the model twice per
    // generation in two different formats. It does not come back now that the records are
    // merged: the pair record is flat and the graded part is the per-side tier.
}

// ── Travel  [SEC:TRAVEL] ──────────────────────────────────────────
// WHAT A JOURNEY COSTS, AND NOTHING ABOUT WHETHER IT IS ALLOWED. There is no gate here
// and there is not going to be one. An author playing in a world they wrote themselves is
// subject only to limits they set, so the job is to make a limit VISIBLE and let them
// decide against it -- travel with the price unpaid stays one tap away at all times. This
// is the whole reason the AE travel system is not being restored: almost all of it was
// enforcement, and enforcement is the half that had no audience.
//
// PRICED PER TIER, NOT PER STEP. A flat per-step price makes the hierarchy the unit of
// distance and the hierarchy is not a unit of anything -- two continents are siblings and
// so are two bedrooms, and a branch somebody bothered to map finely would cost more to
// cross than a branch they sketched. A tier table lets the author say what each LEVEL of
// their own world costs, which is the only party that knows.
//
// NO SEEDED ROWS AND NO DEFAULT NUMBERS. Same rule the calendar's invented months were
// deleted under: a default is not neutral, it is what every author who never opens the
// screen actually ships with, and there is no honest guess at the scale of a world nobody
// has written yet. An empty table means travel is free. That is a legible state and the
// right one to start from.
interface TravelTierCost {
    tier: number;    // the tier this row prices
    // MINUTES, ALWAYS, however it was typed. Settings offers days/hours/minutes because
    // asking for 1440 is asking the author to do arithmetic the engine can do -- but the
    // SPLIT IS A DOOR, NOT A SHAPE. Storing three numbers would mean three ways to say
    // ninety minutes and a normalisation question at every read. See ceTravelDHM.
    mins: number;
    cost: number;    // units of TravelDef.stateId spent per hop priced at this tier
}

interface TravelDef {
    // WHICH STATE PAYS, as a StateDef id, or absent for time-only travel. Read through
    // ceTravelState and never off this field: a def deleted out of the catalog leaves the
    // id dangling, and that is one check in one place rather than four.
    stateId?: string;
    tiers?: TravelTierCost[];
}

// ── Global Configuration ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

interface ContextTemplates {
    // [CE-STRIP earlier CTX-CUT] trackedNarrative/location/player/npcWeb/activeNarrative
    // removed — those injections (and the Drama tab editor UI for all of them) are
    // gone. Only the two AN survivors remain, still macro-parsed via
    // parseMacroTemplate() but with no UI exposing them for editing anymore.
    npc: string;           // [LOREFOLD] per-character thoughts line — now ONLY the "Thinking:" line (relationship/mid-range moved to the character lorebook)
    criticalHook: string;  // [HOOK-BAND] tier 3+ hook line, merged into the same AN block.
                           // |hookPressure| is the tier-graded clause — empty at 3, the
                           // surface-it directive at 4. Key name kept for authors' saved
                           // customisations; it is the hook line, not only the critical one.
    npcWeb: string;        // [REL-PAIR] RETIRED; nothing reads it. Kept in the type only
                           // so migrateConfig's backfill loop has a key to recognise, and
                           // deleted once no stored config carries a customised copy.
    // [REL-PAIR] `position` IS DELETED FROM THE TYPE, not left as a retired string
    // beside npcWeb. The two are not the same case: npcWeb's stored value is an author's
    // customised sentence that a later pass may want to migrate, while `position` templated
    // a record that no longer exists in that shape at all. A declared field nothing writes
    // and nothing reads is the parked field [F-20] forbids.
    // [REL-INJECT] The two Author's Note halves of a pair record. `together` is
    // shared and renders once; `want` is per side and renders once per side that wrote one.
    relTogether: string;
    relWant: string;
    place: string;         // [D-08] where the protagonist is, plus the location's own description
    present: string;       // [D-08] who else is in the scene
    // [T-06] The clock line. ONE MACRO, |band|, AND THERE WILL NOT BE A SECOND CARRYING A
    // NUMBER. An author is free to rewrite the sentence; they are not offered the hour.
    time: string;
}

// [LOREFOLD] Narrative-prose templates for the per-character dynamic block
// folded into that character's lorebook entry (buildCharDynamicBlock /
// buildSceneLorebookActivation): equipment, conditions, relationship, and mid-range.
// The NPC web (bilateral, two-character shared data) is deliberately NOT here — it
// stays in the AN (cfg.contextTemplates.npcWeb), injected once when both parties are
// present, to avoid the per-character double injection folding it here would cause.
// Deliberately a SEPARATE bucket from ContextTemplates: this feeds the lorebook, not
// the AN, and is authored in World Settings. Still macro-parsed via parseMacroTemplate().
interface LorebookTemplates {
    // [LOREFOLD] Narrative-prose lines for the per-character dynamic block
    // folded into that character's lorebook entry (buildCharDynamicBlock). Each is
    // macro-parsed via parseMacroTemplate(); empty [ ] blocks self-suppress.
    // [F-09] `condition` REMOVED FROM THE TYPE but deliberately still read once, by
    // migrateConfig, which copies whatever the author had onto each migrated def's own
    // `line` before deleting cfg.conditions. A state's line is per-def now (see StateDef.line):
    // one config-level template could not serve both "Marek is dying" and "Marek is Hostile
    // toward the Iron Circle", and scope does not predict which a def wants.
    equipment: string;      // worn/carried line; macros |name| |equipList|
    // [POSITION] relationship / relTrajectory / relStatus DELETED. Position
    // does not fold into the lorebook: it is a two-party line and only means anything
    // when BOTH parties are in the scene, which is an Author's-Note gate, not a
    // per-character one. Folding it here would have injected one side's private
    // reading every time its owner was present — including when the target is nowhere
    // near the scene — which is the same shape of bug WEB-FLATTEN removed from npcWeb.
    // Its template therefore lives in ContextTemplates (the AN bucket) as `position`.
    // [REL-INJECT] REVERSED, AND THE NOTE ABOVE IS KEPT BECAUSE THE OBJECTION STILL
    // HOLDS -- what changed is where the gate sits. The pair's shared prose folds here now,
    // under a BOTH-PARTIES-PRESENT test, and is emitted by exactly one of the two entries
    // (see ceRelFoldOwner) so a mutual pair says it once rather than twice.
    relationship: string;   // shared pair prose; macros |nameA| |nameB| |shape| |standing|
    journal: string;        // [CHARJOURNAL] this character's own journal entries; macro |journalBody|
}

// [LOREFOLD] Per-field include switches for buildCharDynamicBlock. Each
// gates ONE narrative line; unchecking drops that field from the lorebook entry
// without touching the underlying character data. Authored in World Settings
// alongside the templates above.
interface LorebookFieldToggles {
    equipment: boolean;
    conditions: boolean;
    journal: boolean;   // [CHARJOURNAL]
    activity: boolean;  // [T-10] the schedule's `doing` line
    position: boolean;  // [F-5] how this character reads another present character
}

// ── World-Build Wizard: Memory Sections ──────────────────────────────────────
// [vNEXT] The wizard builds the static Memory block from an ordered list of named
// sections (Setting Name, Style, Themes, Conflict, ...). Each section is generated
// in its own scoped pass (so they never bleed into each other), reviewed/edited by
// the user, then assembled and committed ONCE to NAI memory (or, for Style, to the
// Author's Note). Memory is treated as static world canon — committed at build time,
// not rewritten during play (cache stability). The user may re-commit at will; the
// engine does not block it.
//   target 'memory' -> assembled into the Memory block as "[ Label ]\n<content>"
//   target 'an'     -> committed to the Author's Note instead (e.g. Style)
interface WizardMemorySection {
    id: string;
    label: string;          // section heading, e.g. "Themes"
    enabled: boolean;       // unchecked sections are omitted from the assembled block
    content: string;        // the editable generated body (blank -> omitted even if enabled)
    instructions: string;   // hard scope guidance for the per-section generator
    target: 'memory' | 'an';
    // [WORLD-HELPER] The AUTHOR-facing line. `instructions` is aimed at a model and one of
    // the seeds literally opens "Output ONLY a single ATTG line", so rendering it at a person
    // was showing them the wrong half of the record. Optional, because every story saved
    // before this existed has sections without it -- ceWorldHelper falls back to the default
    // table by id rather than migrating them.
    helper?: string;
}

// ── World-Build Wizard: Chats + Topics ───────────────────────────────────────
// [vNEXT] The wizard is a set of topic-scoped chats (modeled on Story Engine's
// multi-chat records). Each chat is a transcript on one WizardTopic. The user can
// keep several chats, bounce between them, choose which OTHER chats / lorebook
// entries / the story each chat pulls in as context, and "commit" a chat to its
// topic (which routes by the topic's commitKind — e.g. memory-section fan-out,
// the Author's Note, or the projectConcept field).
//
// This is the DATA layer only: records, the topic registry, storage, and CRUD.
// Generation (chat turns + the summarize/commit passes) is layered on separately.
interface WizardChatMessage {
    role: 'user' | 'assistant';
    content: string;
}

// commitKind routes what "commit this chat" does:
//   'concept'      -> writes/refreshes cfg.projectConcept (the general context chat)
//   'memoryFanout' -> runs per-section scoped generation over cfg.wizardSections,
//                     then assembles + commits Memory (and AN for Style sections)
//   'note'         -> reserved: a single free section committed straight to memory/AN
// Entity-producing topics (characters, factions, locations, ...) get their own
// commitKinds in later phases — kept open as a string so the registry is extensible.
type WizardCommitKind = 'concept' | 'memoryFanout' | 'note' | string;

interface WizardTopic {
    id: string;
    label: string;
    instructions: string;     // the chat persona / scope — parallels field-gen instructions
    commitKind: WizardCommitKind;
    seedMessage?: string;     // [vNEXT] optional first assistant turn, seeded into new chats on this topic
}

// [DISTILL] Cached distillation of a chat's transcript into declarative world
// facts. Regenerated only when the chat has grown past sourceMsgCount, so a linked chat
// costs one extra generation per growth spurt rather than one per batch generation.
interface WizardNotesCache {
    text: string;
    sourceMsgCount: number;   // chat.messages.length when this was distilled
    updatedAt: number;
}
interface WizardChat {
    id: string;
    topicId: string;
    title: string;
    messages: WizardChatMessage[];
    contextChatIds: string[];      // other wizard chats fed in as read-only context
    includeStory: boolean;         // pull recent story text as context
    lorebookEntryIds: string[];    // lorebook entries fed in as context
    characterIds?: string[];       // characters fed in as context, chat-owned
    notesCache?: WizardNotesCache; // see WizardNotesCache
    createdAt: number;
    updatedAt: number;
}

// [CE-STRIP earlier] TierTravelDefault removed — travel-tier cost/narration cut with Travel.

interface GlobalConfig {
    // Engine timing intervals (in tokens)
    contextTemplates: ContextTemplates;
    lorebookTemplates: LorebookTemplates;
    lorebookFields: LorebookFieldToggles;  // [LOREFOLD] per-field include switches
    interfaceMode?: string;        // [earlier] 'bridge' | 'native' | 'both' — which HUD renderer(s) mount
    thoughtIntervalGens: number;   // [E1] generations between ephemeral updates
    // [POSITION] arcIntervalGens / arcIntervalTokens deleted with the
    // relationship generator. Position rides the ephemeral beat pass and has no cadence.
    hookIntervalGens: number;      // generations between hook updates
    // Optional token-based cadence. When dynamicIntervalMode === 'tokens', the
    // dynamic engine measures the *IntervalTokens fields against a persistent story token
    // counter instead of the *IntervalGens fields against the generation counter. Default
    // 'gens' — the token fields are only consulted in token mode.
    dynamicIntervalMode?: 'gens' | 'tokens';
    thoughtIntervalTokens?: number; // context tokens between ephemeral updates (token mode)
    hookIntervalTokens?: number;    // tokens between hook updates
    totalGenerations: number;      // persistent story-level generation counter
    ephemeralCap: number;
    maxGenerationsPerTurn?: number; // [TURN-CAP] hard cap on background generations dispatched in one runDynamicEngine pass (0 = unlimited, default 1). NAI permits ONE generation at a time and rejects the rest outright, so this is closer to a hardware limit than a preference -- see runDynamicEngine.
    maxDynamicCharacters?: number; // [vNEXT] cap on present characters processed as dynamic per turn (0/undefined = no cap). Ranked chars fill slots first (by rank), then unranked present-dynamic chars.
    // [SCENE-STAGE-TUNE] The Scene stage's geometry. FOUR OPTIONAL NUMBERS, no migration
    // and no schema bump: every read goes through ceStageSettings, which supplies the
    // default for an absent or out-of-range field, so a config written before this existed
    // resolves to exactly the constants that shipped. Same treatment defaultBackgroundUrl
    // already gets. CE_STAGE_SETTINGS (29) is the table that names and bounds them.
    stageScale?: number;           // percent of the derived standee size, 40-200
    stageSpread?: number;          // percent of the frame's width the row occupies, 20-100
    stageFloor?: number;           // percent of the frame's height the feet sit above the base, 0-40
    stageMaxChars?: number;        // most standees drawn at once; 0 = everyone present
    stageFacing?: string;          // 'right' | 'left' | 'none' -- which way the source art looks
    graveyardEntityId?: string;    // [KILL] id of the location killCharacter files bodies into. '' until the first death \u2014 the place is created lazily. STORED AS AN ID, not found by name: the old killCharacter matched on name === 'Graveyard', which broke the moment the author renamed it or wrote a story with two, and silently made a second one.

    // Character sheet
    sheetCategories: SheetCategory[];

    // Defaults for new characters
    autoThoughtsDefault: boolean;
    showFieldHeaders: boolean;

    // Style
    generalStyle: string;

    // Field customisation
    fieldLabels: Record<string, string>;
    fieldInstructions: Record<string, string>;
    fieldBudgets: Record<string, number>;
    customStaticFields: CustomFieldDef[];
    // [LOC-PROFILE4] The same library, for places. A SECOND ARRAY RATHER THAN A
    // slot ON THE FIRST: a character field and a location field are offered in different
    // pickers, generated against different context and compiled into different entries,
    // so one list would need filtering at every read and would put a character's Presence
    // slot one tap from a location's field catalog.
    customLocationFields: CustomFieldDef[];
    // [ITEM-CAT] The same library again, for things. A THIRD ARRAY FOR THE SAME
    // REASON THE SECOND EXISTS, and the reason is stronger here: an item has NO built-in
    // catalog at all -- there is no ITEM_FACETS to sit beside LOCATION_FACETS, because a
    // sword, a debt, a passport and a severed hand share no question worth asking of all
    // four. Whatever an item is in this story, the author says so here.
    customItemFields: CustomFieldDef[];
    customFactionFields?: CustomFieldDef[];   // [F-28]
    customNarrativeFields?: CustomFieldDef[]; // [NARR-LENS]
    customSpeciesFields?: CustomFieldDef[];   // [F-32]

    // Presets
    relationshipArchetypes: RelationshipArchetypeDef[];
    // [SEC:STATE] The def catalog. Here rather than in its own record for the same
    // reason species/factions/conditions are here -- a def is world vocabulary, it is
    // edited from Settings, and it inherits ceSettingsOp's write chain for free. The
    // VALUES are elsewhere (CharacterRecord.stateValues, STORAGE_STATE_WORLD,
    // STORAGE_STATE_FACTION): D-06 sorts by rate of change and a catalog and a gauge do
    // not change at the same rate.
    stateDefs: StateDef[];
    // [G-03] Category display order for the States editor, the profile drill and the faction
    // resources panel. OPTIONAL AND SELF-HEALING: absent means first-appearance order (the
    // pre-G-03 behaviour exactly), an unknown name in it is inert, and a category missing
    // from it sorts after the ones that are named. Read through ceStateGrouped, never direct.
    stateCategoryOrder?: string[];
    // [REC-ORDER] AUTHORED ORDER FOR RECORDS, one id list PER TYPE. Same shape and same
    // self-healing contract as stateCategoryOrder directly above, and for the same reason:
    // an order list is not a record. A deleted entity leaves a stale id that matches nothing
    // and costs nothing, a new entity appends itself by being absent, and there is no
    // reconciler -- which would be a second source of truth about what exists.
    // KEYED BY TYPE, NOT ONE GLOBAL LIST, per the author. A character's place among characters
    // is a different question from a faction's place among factions, and one list would make
    // reordering a cast reshuffle the faction menu.
    // ONE LIST SERVES FLAT AND NESTED LISTS BOTH. Diplomacy sorts each SIBLING SET by this
    // array; sorting a subset by its index in a global sequence preserves the authored
    // relative order, so a tree needs no per-parent storage.
    recordOrder?: any;          // { [entityType: string]: string[] }
    // [F-12] The resolution catalog. Beside stateDefs for the same reason and under the
    // same write chain; a resolution is world vocabulary, not a fact about anybody.
    resolutionDefs: ResolutionDef[];
    // [I-04] WHICH TABLE EVERY ACTION IS JUDGED BY. The Actions panel used to ask on every
    // attempt; per author that is a fact about the WORLD (\u201cthis is a 2d6 fail-forward
    // game\u201d), not a per-turn decision, so it is answered once in Settings > Rolls.
    // OPTIONAL, AND DANGLING IS THE SAME AS ABSENT. A config written before this key and one
    // naming a resolution the author has since deleted both want the first def rather than a
    // refusal to roll -- read through ceDefaultResolutionId and never directly, because a
    // direct read is how one call site comes to roll a table another one displays.
    defaultResolutionId?: string;
    // [ACT-MODE] WHICH SHAPE THE ACTIONS PANEL TAKES. Optional and read through
    // ceActionsMode, which normalises anything unrecognised -- including absence -- to
    // 'simple'. Schema v11 stamps 'full' onto stories that already had a config.
    actionsMode?: string;
    // [ACT-MODE] THE WAGER A SIMPLE ROLL CHARGES, and it is SEEDED EMPTY on purpose: per
    // author a simple action is narrative and risks nothing unless that is asked for. Read
    // through ceStakeDef, which returns null for a deleted id rather than falling to the
    // first def -- so removing the table an author pointed at goes back to charging nothing,
    // never to charging something else.
    simpleWagerId?: string;
    stakeDefs: StakeDef[];             // [F-14]
    // [F-15d] OPTIONAL, because a config written before presets existed has no key and an
    // empty list means the same thing. migrateConfig is the only place that closes that
    // gap, so every reader below it can index without a shape check.
    actionPresets?: CeActionPreset[];
    // [JRNL-STAMP] WHETHER ENTRIES SHOW THEIR IN-WORLD DATE. Per author this is a property of
    // the AUTHOR rather than of a journal -- somebody who does not want dates does not want
    // them on the character's either -- so it is one value here and not a field on each of
    // the three journal records, which is the opposite call from scanPerson one door over.
    // OPTIONAL AND ABSENT MEANS ON, which is what every story written before the switch
    // existed has been doing. Read through ceJrnlStampsOn, never directly.
    journalStamps?: boolean;

    // [F-28] factions and [F-32] species both moved to entity storage. Neither key exists
    // here any more; the migrations delete them from stored configs on load.
    // [F-26] OUR FACTION, OR ABSENT. Optional exactly as protagonistCharId is, and for the
    // same reason: a story about one drifter has no side, and forcing one would put an empty
    // heading on every screen that mentions factions. Its ONE job is framing -- a Move
    // between the protagonist's faction and another can be described as such -- and it must
    // never be assumed present.
    protagonistFactionId?: string;
    // [T-07] The calendar. Optional, and ABSENT IS NOT BACKFILLED -- ceCalendar fills every
    // default on read, so a story written before this key behaves identically to one written
    // after it and nothing has to run a migration to make that true. The first edit in
    // Settings > Time materialises it; nothing else writes it.
    // [F-4] The world's default. Per-character summonOnMention overrides it; absent is ON.
    summonOnMention?: boolean;
    calendar?: CalendarDef;
    // [SEC:TRAVEL] What a journey costs. Optional and NEVER BACKFILLED, exactly as
    // `calendar` is and for the same reason: absent means free, which is what every story
    // written before this key already does, so no migration has to run to keep it true.
    travel?: TravelDef;
    // [T-19] Whether the story is TOLD the time-of-day band. Opt-in: absent reads as OFF,
    // so no existing story starts injecting the hour because it upgraded. Gates the
    // an.time injection only -- the clock, schedules and dated entries are unaffected.
    timeInject?: boolean;
    // [T-18] What is ON the calendar, as opposed to what the calendar IS. Content rather
    // than vocabulary, which is why it is authored on the Map's Time tab and not in
    // Settings -- the same split that sent factions to Diplomacy.
    datedEntries?: DatedEntry[];
    // [F-24] THE WORLD'S DEFAULT STANDING LADDER, coldest first. Every faction uses this
    // unless it overrides with its own -- per author: shared default, per-faction override.
    // SHARING THE LADDER DOES NOT SHARE THE VALUE. A character holds a separate standing
    // toward each faction regardless, so this reduces AUTHORING, never the number of things
    // a character carries. Saying otherwise is the mistake a shared list invites.
    standingLadder?: string[];
    // [F-30] The world defaults for the two rungs, read through ceStandingRungFor with the
    // same absent-means-middle rule the faction's own fields get.
    standingRest?: number;
    standingStart?: number;
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] factionResourceDefs / factionResourceSections removed — Faction
    // Resources cut. tierTravelDefaults removed — Travel cut. stakeDefs / actionTemplates
    // removed — Actions cut.
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] npcArchetypes/itemArchetypes removed from GlobalConfig — Bestiary + Item Templates cut.
    defaultBackgroundUrl?: string;  // module-specific scene default background URL

    // [vNEXT] World-Build Wizard — concept fields the user seeds up front (title/genre/
    // initial idea) plus the ordered Memory-block section list the wizard assembles.
    projectName?: string;
    projectGenre?: string;

    projectConcept?: string;        // the user's initial idea; chat starts from this
    wizardSections?: WizardMemorySection[];
    // [CE-STRIP v2.0.x] holdingDef removed — Holding cut.

    // [earlier CHARCHAT-FRESH-THOUGHTS, per user, REPLACES earlier CHARCHAT-THINK
    // outright — see the CE-INDEX entry for why] Character Chat: refresh the contact's
    // ephemeral snapshot via generateEphemeral() — the SAME generator the story's
    // dynamic engine uses, not a second bespoke implementation — right after each
    // reply, so buildCharChatInstruction's existing "right now" line is never more
    // than one turn stale for the FOLLOWING reply. Originally fired before
    // the reply instead; moved to onGenerationEnd after testing showed the pre-reply
    // timing fails outright (NAI rejects a nested api.v1.generate() call while the
    // reply's own generation is still in progress) — see onGenerationEnd's CHARCHAT
    // branch for the full writeup. Unlike the old inline "[Thinking: ...]" line this
    // IS persisted (char.ephemeral / char.ephemeralHistory) and viewable, and it costs
    // a separate GLM call rather than sharing the reply's own token budget. Optional;
    // undefined reads as off (an extra call per turn is opt-in), so no migration fill
    // needed. old charChatThinking is a dead orphan field on any earlier save.
    charChatFreshThoughts?: boolean;

    // [CHARCHAT-IMG] Chat image attachments. When on, an
    // "[Image: <description>]" tag written by EITHER side of a chat is caught after the
    // turn lands, run through a chat-scoped architect pass, and dispatched to the bridge
    // for generation. Optional; undefined reads as off — per user, auto-with-a-toggle,
    // and off by default because each image costs a REAL architect generation
    // (api.v1.generate, unlike the chat turn itself which is free — empirically
    // confirmed via the probe: script output budget does not move across a native
    // editor.generate() reply) plus an image generation in the bridge.
    charChatImages?: boolean;

    // [POV-REMEMBERED] The chat POV is a REMEMBERED SELECTION, not a
    // per-launch pick. Persisting it is what makes the Profile launcher's Chat cell a
    // one-tap start: there is nothing left to ask, so it never has to route through the
    // Chat surface. Story-scoped, because a povId is a charId and charIds are story data
    // \u2014 GlobalConfig is storyStorage-backed despite the name (see loadConfig).
    // '' = No POV; CC_POV_INTERVIEW = the interview sentinel; anything else is a charId.
    // A povId naming a character who has since been deleted or deactivated is CLEARED
    // BACK TO CONFIG by cePublishChat, not merely hidden from one render, so it can never
    // sit here as a filter nobody can see or reach.
    charChatPovId?: string;
    // [CC-XIALONG] Which model the chat pins for the duration of a session. Default
    // 'xialong-v1', chosen when the chat still emitted an AetherRoom identity card as its
    // system prompt and the model was picked to match that format. [CC-PREFIX] THE CARD IS
    // GONE -- the chat no longer writes the system prompt at all -- so the original reason for
    // this default no longer holds and the pin has not been re-measured against the current
    // context shape. Left in place rather than changed on a guess; it is a SETTING, so the
    // A/B that would answer it needs no code change.
    charChatModel?: string;
    // [CC-MODES] The kind of conversation the NEXT chat starts as. Remembered for the
    // same reason charChatPovId is: it is a setting about how you use this story, not a
    // property of any one thread, and re-picking it every time is the friction the POV
    // row already learned not to impose.
    charChatMode?: string;

    // [CCAV-ENGINE] Chat avatar SIZE (px, the WIDTH axis \u2014 shape
    // supplies the height multiplier) and SHAPE. Moved engine-side from the bridge's
    // localStorage ('ae_ccav_size' / 'ae_ccav_radius'). an earlier JSXCHAT note called
    // these "unportable by construction"; that is WITHDRAWN as wrong, and the reasoning it
    // missed is worth stating: the engine authors the avatar WIDGET, so it chooses that
    // widget's own content, so it can write the display settings INTO the sentinel and let
    // the bridge paint what it is told. That is not the CE-IMG-OPTS mirror trap (nothing
    // here tracks the bridge's live state) \u2014 it is the ordinary engine-is-source /
    // bridge-is-renderer split every other cross-artifact value in this file already uses.
    // Undefined reads as 40 / 'round'; see ceChatAvatarDisplay for the one resolver.
    charChatAvatarSize?: number;
    charChatAvatarShape?: string;

    // [CHAT-BUBBLE-STYLE] The chat bubble's max width as a percentage of
    // the editor column. A pure decoration-rule input: compiled to CSS by
    // _charchatBubbleRules and pushed through decorations.updateRules, so a change is live
    // on the open chat with no document write and no bridge round trip.
    charChatBubbleWidth?: number;

    // [earlier, SUPERSEDED BY earlier CHAT-BUBBLE-AXES] The old five-preset id. NO LONGER
    // WRITTEN by anything; kept in the shape because it is persisted record and because
    // ceChatBubbleDisplay still READS it once, as the source of the per-axis defaults for a
    // config that predates the axes. Deleting the field would silently reset every existing
    // chat to the 'chat' seed. See CE_CB_LEGACY_SEED.
    charChatBubbleStyle?: string;

    // [CHAT-BUBBLE-AXES] The bubble's look as SEVEN INDEPENDENT AXES
    // plus colour, replacing the single preset id above. Every one is a key into its own
    // CE_CB_* table and every one is validated by ceChatBubbleDisplay, so an unknown value
    // degrades to that axis's default rather than reaching the rule builder.
    charChatBubbleFrame?: string;    // bubble | card | rule | underline | none
    charChatBubbleCorner?: string;   // sharp | soft | round | pill  (also sets padX)
    charChatBubbleFill?: string;     // solid | tint | wash | frosted | none
    charChatBubbleEdge?: string;     // none | hairline | bold | bright
    charChatBubbleDepth?: string;    // flat | lift | bloom | inset
    charChatBubbleType?: string;     // sans | serif | mono
    charChatBubbleVoice?: string;    // normal | italic | wide | tight
    charChatBubblePalette?: string;  // key into CE_CB_PALETTE -- a POV/contact hue PAIR
    charChatBubbleAccent?: string;   // key into CE_CB_ACCENT -- marker band + Emotion tail
    // [CHAT-LANE] inset | full. The only geometry axis, and safe only because it
    // moves the avatar lane with the bubble -- see CE_CB_LANE.
    charChatBubbleLane?: string;

    // Free hex overrides. EMPTY MEANS \u0022use the palette\u0022, so clearing the field is the undo
    // and there is no separate custom-on flag. A malformed value resolves to the default
    // ink rather than failing -- see _ceCbRgb.
    charChatPovColor?: string;
    charChatContactColor?: string;
    charChatAccentColor?: string;
    // '' = auto-contrast off the resolved fill. See _ceCbInk for the one assumption it makes.
    charChatBubbleInk?: string;

    // [CHAT-STAGE] The visual-novel layer behind the story editor while a chat is open:
    // the camera's location as a backdrop, the POV standing left, the contact right.
    // OFF BY DEFAULT AND PERSISTED, unlike Scene's stage, which is a session flag because
    // it was a test. This one has to survive a reload in the middle of a conversation.
    // The two numbers are OPTIONAL WITH A RESOLVED DEFAULT (ceChatStageSettings), which is
    // defaultBackgroundUrl's precedent and CE_STAGE_SETTINGS' after it: no migration, no
    // schema bump, and a config written before they existed resolves to the shipped values.
    // DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED WITH stageScale / stageFloor -- see CHAT-STAGE.
    charChatStage?: boolean;
    charChatStageScale?: number;
    charChatStageFloor?: number;

    // ── [D-01] THE PROTAGONIST POINTER ──────────────────────────────────────
    // THE SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR WHO THE PROTAGONIST IS. '' means the story has none,
    // which is a supported state and the default. A POINTER RATHER THAN A PER-RECORD FLAG
    // because a boolean on 40 records makes "two protagonists" representable and leaves
    // every one of those 40 read sites deciding for itself what that would mean; one id
    // makes "none" and "exactly one" the only states there are.
    // CharacterRecord.isProtagonist SURVIVES as a maintained PROJECTION of this field, not
    // as a second opinion -- see setProtagonist, which is its only writer. That is a
    // deliberate deviation from "delete the stored flag": loadConfig hits storyStorage on
    // every call with no cache, so deriving the flag inside loadCharacter would put a
    // storage read inside every roster loop in the file. The invariant that matters
    // (one writer, exclusivity enforced) is kept; the read sites are untouched.
    protagonistCharId?: string;
    // Display mirror of that character's name, so injection paths can resolve
    // CE_PROTAGONIST_REF without a roster load. Written by the same two places that write
    // the id. Was previously an undeclared field reached through `as any`.
    protagonistName?: string;
}

// ── Image Settings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// [IMGFIELDS] Replaces the old 5-slot ImagePreset system entirely. Click-
// to-add fields: each has its own base-prompt/UC fragment and an optional weight,
// toggled active/inactive independently (any number can be active at once — not
// a single-select "active preset" anymore). Active fields compile into the final
// prompt/UC at generation time (see compileActiveImageFields). Global storage
// (user's call — reusable across every story, matching where presets already
// lived). Starts EMPTY on a fresh install — no migration from old preset tags.

interface ImagePromptField {
    id: string;
    name: string;        // display name, e.g. "Cinematic Quality"
    basePrompt: string;  // positive prompt fragment
    uc: string;           // negative/UC fragment
    weight: number;      // 0.1-10; 1 = unweighted (compiled plain, no wrapping)
    active: boolean;     // click-to-activate/deactivate; compiled in when true
}

interface GlobalImageSettings {
    autoDownload: boolean;
    // [STORYIMG] Opt-in, default false: does a studio generation also pin itself
    // into the story editor? Off means nothing is written into the document at all.
    studioPlaceInStory?: boolean;
    // [STORYIMG-DEFAULT-ON] One-shot migration guard, never read as a setting.
    studioPlaceReseeded?: boolean;
    contextSize: number;         // number of story paragraphs fed to the architect prompt
    architectPrompt: string;     // GLM system prompt for image prompt generation
    architectAutoSnap: boolean;  // whether to run architect pass on auto-snap
    defaultWidth: number;        // ratio now lives on the Image Studio widget; this is
    defaultHeight: number;       //           the persisted last-used/default value it reads from
    defaultScale: number;        // prompt guidance scale; widget field, default 5 if blank
    defaultSteps: number;        // no per-generation field (user: "Steps are set to 28") —
                                  //           this is the editable default, kept in the field-manager
                                  //           settings area rather than removed outright
    // [IMG-MODEL] The NAI model id every generation is sent with. OPTIONAL AND BACKFILLED
    // rather than required, for the reason every field above it is: a config written before
    // this existed is still a valid config, and loadImageSettings seeds it.
    imageModel?: string;
}

// ── Bridge Payload Shapes ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// [ATTENTION] Derived-only. NOT a record field, NOT a storage key, and never
// written anywhere — _ceDramaAttention recomputes it from the pressure tiers at payload
// build time. That is deliberate and is what makes it cheap: there is no migration, no
// undo semantics to get wrong, and no way for it to disagree with the tiers it describes.
// Undo already reverts the tiers (historyStorage), so the attention state reverts with
// them for free.
//
// Rides TWO payload shapes because the author meets characters on two surfaces: the scene
// roster (SceneCharacterInfo, below) and location inspection (buildEntityDetailPayload's
// children[], which is a different code path over the same characters). Both must carry it
// or the dot appears on one surface and not the other, which is worse than neither.
interface CharAttention {
    level: 'calm' | 'building' | 'ask';
    reason: string;        // author-facing sentence, rendered as the dot's tooltip
    axis?: string;         // 'pursuit' | 'hook' | 'position' — which one raised it
}

interface SceneCharacterInfo {
    // Compact character snapshot included in SCENE_STATE_UPDATE.
    id: string;
    name: string;
    entityId: string;
    briefDescription: string;
    compressedLine: string;   // ephemeral.compressedLine — immediate state line
    isProtagonist: boolean;
    isFollowing?: boolean;         // [FOLLOW1] entity body is under protagonist body
    attention?: CharAttention;     // [ATTENTION] derived, see CharAttention above
}

// [CE-STRIP earlier] SceneActionInfo / SceneInstanceInfo removed — Actions system cut.
interface SceneStatePayload {
    // Full payload sent as EVT_SCENE_STATE after any state-changing operation.
    // Bridge renders this into the Scene HUD.
    locationId: string;
    locationName: string;
    locationDescription: string;
    presentCharacters: SceneCharacterInfo[];
    // [CE-STRIP v2.0.x] quickInstances field removed — QuickInstance type no longer exists.
    locationGraph?: any;          // graph of current location: parent, children, connections — awareness only, no travel-cost annotations
    factionRelationships?: { id: string; name: string; states: string[]; stateIdx: number; stateLabel: string; }[];   // DEAD: nothing assigns or reads this. Belongs to the receiverless-broadcast sweep.
    presentItems?: { id: string; name: string; icon?: string; }[];  // item entities at current location
    // [CE-STRIP v2.1.x] presentMinorNpcs removed — minor-NPC tier retired, everyone is a normal character now.
    // NOTE: bridge still reads state.presentMinorNpcs at three sites (Section 6b "Present NPCs"
    // render, two target-selector populators) — degrades gracefully to empty array via its own
    // `|| []` guards, but won't show these characters until the bridge pass picks this up.
    // [CE-STRIP earlier] npcArchetypes/itemArchetypes removed from SceneStatePayload — Bestiary + Item Templates cut.
    defaultBgUrl?: string;          // cfg.defaultBackgroundUrl forwarded to bridge for the location banner
    // [CE-STRIP v2.0.x] clock field removed — Clock/Calendar cut.
}

interface CharacterVisualRecord {
    // Per-character visual data payload sent as EVT_CHAR_DATA_SYNC.
    // Bridge uses this as the source of truth for image generation character matching.
    id: string;
    name: string;
    nicknames: string[];
    // Visual identity fields
    visuals: string;
    uc: string;
    sceneVisuals: string;
    avatarVisuals: string;
    avatarWidth: number;
    avatarHeight: number;
    avatarSeed: number;
    // Narrative identity fields (used to build chat persona — replaces separate roster persona)
    species: string;
    age: string;
    gender: string;
    briefDescription: string;
    physicalDescription: string;
    personalityCore: string;
    voiceAndMannerisms: string;
    background: string;
}

// ── Helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

interface ImportMergeCounts { added: number; updated: number; }

interface ImportResult {
    charCount: number;
    entityCount: number;
    configImported: boolean;
    configCategoryCounts?: { [category: string]: ImportMergeCounts };  // [IMPORT-MERGE] per-array upsert tallies, e.g. actionTemplates: {added:1,updated:2}
    npcWebImported: boolean;
    npcWebCounts?: ImportMergeCounts;   // [IMPORT-MERGE]
    partyStateImported: boolean;
}


// ============================================================================
// SECTION 2 — CONSTANTS, SEED CATALOGS, AND THE STORES THAT READ THEM
// Storage keys, the EVT_* bridge protocol, the seeded StateDef/Resolution/Stake
// catalogs, context and lorebook templates, and the entity facet catalogs.
// Also the wizard chat and draft-staging CRUD, which live here because they are
// nothing but reads and writes of two keys declared above.
// Never write a raw storage key or event name — reference the constant.
// ============================================================================

// ── Storage Keys — Character & Config (storyStorage) ────────────────────────

var STORAGE_INDEX         = 'ce_index';           // CharacterMeta[]
var STORAGE_CONFIG        = 'ce_config';           // GlobalConfig
// [ENT-ID1] The storage-wide schema stamp. A per-record migration cannot rewrite ids
// ACROSS records -- it never knows whether the others are converted yet -- so anything
// that does gates on this number, runs once and stamps. Unstamped reads as 1.
var STORAGE_SCHEMA_VER    = 'ce_schema_version';  // number — see ceRunSchemaMigrations
var CE_SCHEMA_VERSION     = 12;                    // 2 = char.id === body.id (ENT-ID1)
                                                  // 8 = [SPEC-BODY] species prose moved
                                                  //     description -> lorebookText
                                                  // 3 = hook pressure is 0-4 [HOOK-TIER]
                                                  // 4 = hooks[] -> hook  [HOOK-ONE]
var STORAGE_CHAR_PFX      = 'ce_char_';            // + charId → CharacterRecord
var STORAGE_LORE_IDS      = 'ce_active_lore';      // JSON string of active lorebook entry IDs
var STORAGE_SUMMON_ANCHOR = 'ce_summon_anchor';    // [F-4] tempStorage — sectionId the turn started at
var STORAGE_IMG_TAGS      = 'ce_img_slot_tags';    // [IMG-TAG-SHARED] storyStorage — slot -> tag, shared by every CHARACTER (species keep their own; see CE_IMG_SLOT_OWNERS)
var STORAGE_GEN_COUNT     = 'ae_gen_count';           // [E1] number — persistent story generation count
var STORAGE_TOKEN_COUNT   = 'ae_token_count';         // number — persistent story token count (token-mode dynamic cadence; NEW key, not the ce_last_tokens tombstone above)
var STORAGE_PARTY         = 'ce_party_state';       // PartyState
var STORAGE_WIZ_CHATS     = 'ae_wizard_chats';      // [vNEXT] WizardChat[] (World-Build Wizard transcripts)
var STORAGE_JOURNAL       = 'ae_journal_v1';        // [JOURNAL] JournalState — player journal bubbles + projected lore entry ids
var STORAGE_CAST_LORE     = 'ae_cast_lore_v1';      // [CASTLORE] CastLoreState — the two always-on roster/geography entries: options, per-record exclusions, projected lore ids, and the last-built text each was written from
// [SEC:STATE] The two scopes with no record to hang values on. Characters keep theirs on
// CharacterRecord.stateValues; config is the CATALOG and must not hold live gauges (D-06).
// storyStorage, not history: an undone turn does not refund a spend.
var STORAGE_STATE_WORLD   = 'ce_state_world_v1';    // [SEC:STATE] Record<defId, number>
// [T-03] historyStorage, the only key in this block that is: the clock moves every turn and
// must rewind. The opposite call from the state values two lines up.
var STORAGE_CLOCK         = 'ce_clock_v1';          // [SEC:CLOCK] ClockState in HISTORY storage
// [F-3] Who rolls. historyStorage so the choice rewinds with the turn it acted on, same tier
// as the armed action and the resolution ledger. '' means the protagonist and is never
// written -- absence is the default, not a stored value.
var STORAGE_ACTOR         = 'ce_actor_v1';          // [F-3] selected actor id, or absent
var STORAGE_STATE_FACTION = 'ce_state_faction_v1';  // [SEC:STATE] Record<factionId, Record<defId, number>>
// [EVENT] Keyed by MILESTONE id, the third instance of the faction shape and stored for the
// same reason: an encounter has no record of its own to hang a gauge on. storyStorage, not
// history -- an undone turn does not refund a hit, exactly as it does not refund a spend,
// and ceReverseStake is the door back for both.
var STORAGE_STATE_EVENT   = 'ce_state_event_v1';    // [EVENT] Record<milestoneId, Record<defId, number>>
var STORAGE_CHAR_JOURNAL_PFX = 'ae_char_journal_v1_'; // [CHARJOURNAL] prefix — per-character CharJournalState, keyed by charId

// [CHAT-THREAD] THE LIVE THREAD, AS A RECORD RATHER THAN AS PROSE. Until now a conversation
// WAS its rendering: sections in the document, re-parsed with _charchatRowIsPov every time
// anything needed to know who said what. Every property the chat wanted -- who spoke, what they
// felt, whether a photo was attached -- had to survive a round trip through prose and be
// recovered by regex, and the regexes are the reason so much of that subsystem is defensive.
// A MESSAGE IS NOW A RECORD AND ITS RENDERING IS A VIEW.
// storyStorage, because a thread belongs to ONE story and should travel with it; historyStorage
// was never the right home and its undo semantics stopped being relevant the moment the
// document stopped being the record.
// KEYED ON charChatEntryId(contactId, povId), THE SAME FUNCTION THE LOREBOOK ARCHIVE USES, so
// thread identity is one concept with one definition. A thread and its archive entry answer to
// the same name, which is what makes CC-CTX able to keep pushing chat into story context with
// no change at all: it reads a transcript, and where that transcript comes from is not its
// business.
var STORAGE_CHAT_THREAD_PFX = 'ce_chat_thread_v1_';

interface ChatMessage {
    id: string;
    // 'pov' is the author's side, 'contact' is the character's. NOT 'user'/'assistant': those
    // are transport words, and this record outlives any particular transport.
    role: 'pov' | 'contact';
    text: string;              // the body ALONE -- no name header, no Emotion line
    at: number;
    // [CC-EMO-SLOT] The resolved canonical slot, contact turns only. Stored as a field for the
    // painter AND replayed inside the turn text for the model, which are two different jobs:
    // strip it from the replay and the model stops seeing that every prior turn carried one,
    // which is the only thing keeping it producing them.
    emotion?: string;
    // The FULL gallery uuid, not the 8-hex document prefix. That truncation existed solely
    // because a document sentinel had to be short; off the document there is nothing to
    // shorten for, and _ccimgBuildIndex's prefix scan has nothing left to do.
    attachmentId?: string;
    attachmentPrompt?: string;
    // The pre-reply snapshot that produced this turn. On the record because it is evidence
    // about a message, not a separate timeline.
    thought?: { eventAnchor?: string; emotionalResponse?: string; immediateDesire?: string };
}
interface ChatThread {
    contactId: string;
    povId: string;
    povName: string;
    mode: string;
    messages: ChatMessage[];
    startedAt: number;
    updatedAt: number;
}
// [F-26] Third instance of one shape, keyed by factionId. AUTHORED ONLY -- no generator
// path and no createdAt formatting beyond ordering.
var STORAGE_FAC_JOURNAL_PFX = 'ae_fac_journal_v1_';
var STORAGE_CHAR_IMG_PFX = 'ae_char_imgslots_v1_'; // [IMG-SLOTS] prefix — per-character CharImageSlotState (slot list + tag overrides), keyed by charId
const STORAGE_NPC_WEB     = 'ae_npc_web';           // JSON string of CeRelationship[]

// ── Storage Keys — Entity World Graph (storyStorage) ─────────────────────────

var STORAGE_ENTITY_INDEX  = 'ae_entity_index';          // EntityMeta[]
var STORAGE_ENTITY_PFX    = 'ae_entity_data_';          // + entityId → EntityRecord
// [MILESTONE] + threadId → ThreadMilestones. ITS OWN KEY AND NOT A FIELD ON THE RECORD,
// for three reasons and the third is the one that decides it: the archive grows without
// bound; EntityRecord is read by the map, the index and every publish, none of which want
// it; and archived milestones are GENERATION INPUT read by one caller at one moment, which
// is a different lifetime from everything on the record.
var STORAGE_MS_PFX        = 'ce_thread_ms_';            // + threadId → ThreadMilestones
// [MILESTONE] THE ONE FOCUS THREAD. A story-level singleton, so a key rather than a flag
// on N records -- a flag would let two threads both claim it and there would be no single
// place to ask. storyStorage, not historyStorage: choosing what the story is about is an
// authoring act, not play state that Undo should walk back.
var STORAGE_ACTIVE_THREAD = 'ce_active_thread';         // string — entity id, '' for none
// [EVENT] THE THREADLESS MILESTONE STORE. A reserved threadId passed to loadMilestones, so
// a situational encounter -- a patrol, an ambush, a bar fight belonging to no arc -- gets a
// milestone with no entity behind it and costs the author no thread to file it under.
// NOTHING REAPS THIS. Every other milestone document dies with deleteEntity; this one has no
// entity, so its archive is CAPPED at the write instead. That cap is the price of
// threadlessness and not a tuning knob.
var CE_MS_LOOSE           = '__loose__';                // [EVENT] reserved threadId
var CE_LOOSE_ARCHIVE_MAX  = 20;                         // [EVENT] oldest dropped on conclude

// ── Storage Keys — Engine State (mostly tempStorage; several say otherwise) ──
// tempStorage clears at session end. Keys marked storyStorage below are there because a
// draft or a chat the user is asked to review must survive a reload and must not rewind.

// [D-03] Name says protagonist, value stays 'ce_player_location': renaming an opaque
// storage token would cost every story a migration to buy nothing.
var STORAGE_PROTAGONIST_LOC        = 'ce_player_location';   // string — current location entity ID
var STORAGE_ENTITY_HISTORY    = 'ce_loc_history';       // string[] — recent location IDs
var STORAGE_WIZ_ACTIVE_CHAT   = 'ae_wiz_active_chat';   // [vNEXT] string — active wizard chat id
var STORAGE_WIZ_TAB_SRC       = 'ae_wiz_tab_src_';      // prefix — per-tab (species/faction) chosen context-source chat id
var STORAGE_WIZ_LOC_PARENT    = 'ae_wiz_loc_parent';    // string — current parent entity id in the Locations wizard tree
// [CHARTER] storyStorage — CharterState. A NEW KEY, not an ae_wiz_* one: the wizard prefix
// belongs to the surface this supersedes, and reusing it would make the kill list ambiguous
// about which records survive the cleanup.
var STORAGE_CHARTER           = 'ce_charter_v1';
// [BUILD-CHAT] storyStorage — { [scopeId]: BuildChat }. ONE RECORD FOR EVERY SCOPE'S CHAT
// rather than one key each: a scope is a short string, the transcripts are small, and a key
// per scope would make "delete this story's build chats" a list-and-loop instead of a
// delete. The shape is the wizard chat's -- id, title, messages, createdAt -- because that
// shape was fine; what is NOT reused is its generation path.
var STORAGE_BUILD_CHATS       = 'ce_build_chats_v1';
var STORAGE_WIZ_INTRO_LOC     = 'ae_wiz_intro_loc';     // string — chosen intro location entity id
// [SCOPE-DEPTH] tempStorage — which location's children the Build route is working on.
// '' is the top rung. Session-only by WORLDPIPE 7.5's rule.
var STORAGE_BUILD_SCOPE       = 'ce_build_scope';
var STORAGE_WIZ_INTRO_DRAFT   = 'ae_wiz_intro_draft';  // [STAGING] storyStorage — staged intro draft { locationId, prose }
var STORAGE_WIZLIVE           = 'ae_wizlive_state';     // [WIZLIVE] storyStorage — LiveWizState. Must not rewind on Undo: the marker paragraph id stays authoritative even if chat turns are undone.
var STORAGE_WIZLIVE_CAT       = 'ae_wizlive_cat_id';   // [CE-CHATS] storyStorage — cached id of the "CE Chats" lorebook category (find-or-create; falls back to a categories() scan by name if the cache misses).
var STORAGE_CHARCHAT          = 'ae_charchat_state';    // [CHARCHAT] storyStorage — CharChatState. Same Undo reasoning as STORAGE_WIZLIVE.
var STORAGE_CC_CTX_CAT        = 'ae_cc_ctx_cat_id';     // [CC-CTX] storyStorage — cached CE Chat Context category id
var STORAGE_CC_CTX_MODE       = 'ae_cc_ctx_mode';       // [CC-CTX] Record<threadEntryId, 'off'|'summary'|'transcript'> — which threads reach story context
var STORAGE_CC_CTX_SUMMARY    = 'ae_cc_ctx_summary';    // [CC-CTX] Record<threadEntryId, string> — the recap 'summary' injects. Distinct from the journal save: that is a character's history, this is story context.
var STORAGE_CHARCHAT_CAT      = 'ae_charchat_cat_id';   // [CHARCHAT-SAVE] storyStorage — cached id of the CE Character Chats lorebook category (find-or-create; mirrors STORAGE_WIZLIVE_CAT).

// [STAGING] Batch generation parks parsed results here as WizardDraft records; only
// commitStagedDrafts() performs the irreversible writes.
var STORAGE_WIZ_DRAFTS        = 'ae_wiz_drafts';        // storyStorage — WizardDraft[]
// [STORYIMG] storyStorage — [{uuid, sectionId, createdAt}]. sectionIds are identifiers,
// not indexes, so this survives edits elsewhere. See SECTION 16b.
var STORAGE_STORY_IMGS        = 'ae_story_imgs';        // storyStorage — story image anchors
// displayName of the hidden disabled entry holding the serialised world config.
const AE_LOREBOOK_PACK_NAME = '[AE World]';
var STORAGE_CHAR_SNAP_PFX     = 'ae_snap_';             // + charId — character snapshot strings
var STORAGE_LAST_TRAVEL       = 'ae_last_travel';       // number — timestamp of last travel
// DEAD KEYS, NO MIGRATION NEEDED: 'ae_tracked_narrative' [CE-SWEEP] and
// 'ae_pending_knowledge' [F-12a] are orphaned strings no reader looks at. Do not re-add
// a reader for either; the features are gone, only the leftovers are not.
var STORAGE_PENDING_INJECT    = 'ae_pending_inject';    // [F-12a] string — one directive, spliced beside the last user message on the next story turn, then consumed
var STORAGE_ARMED_ACTION      = 'ae_armed_action';      // [F-15a] CeArmedAction — the primed move, consumed by the first real story generation
var STORAGE_LAST_RESOLUTION   = 'ae_last_resolution';   // [F-15a] the last fired roll, kept ONLY so the Actions panel can show its arithmetic back
var STORAGE_POS_TERMINUS      = 'ce_pos_terminus';      // [F-17] a Position that hit a bound and is waiting for the author to rewrite Standing
var STORAGE_PENDING_FLAVOR    = 'ae_pending_flavor';    // [E5] string — player flavor to compose into next > prompt
// [X1] Bridge-roundtrip promise resolution: keyed by promptId.
let _pendingBridgeResolves: Record<string, (value: any) => void> = {};
let _bridgeReqSeq = 0;   // monotonic counter for unique promptIds
var STORAGE_ENGINE_SKIP       = 'ae_engine_skip';       // 'true' — suppresses dynamic engine on retry/undo
var STORAGE_RETRY_CHARS       = 'ae_retry_chars';       // [FIX] was referenced (runDynamicEngine/hook budget) but never declared — assembly omission

// ── Storage Keys — Image Settings (api.v1.storage — global, not story-bound) ─

var STORAGE_KEY_PROMPT_FIELDS    = 'ae_img_prompt_fields_v1'; // ImagePromptField[]
var STORAGE_KEY_GLOBAL           = 'auto_img_global_settings'; // GlobalImageSettings

// ── Bridge Event Name Constants ────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE COMPLETE PROTOCOL. Every name below has a verified partner in bridge 3.0.8. A
// broadcast with no case on the other side lands in the bridge's `default:` logger and
// does nothing. BEFORE ADDING A NAME, ADD ITS CASE ON THE OTHER SIDE IN THE SAME PATCH --
// and prefer a new `op` on an existing message: AE_CHARCHAT_ACTION is one message, six
// verbs, one dispatcher.
//
// Engine → bridge (bridge has a case for each):
//   GENERATE_IMAGE  STORY_ID_SYNC  HANDSHAKE_REQUEST  AE_DIAG_REPLY
//   AE_CHARCHAT_STATE  AE_ENGINE_THINKING  AE_CHAT_BUBBLE_STYLE
//   AE_DELETE_PORTRAIT_CASCADE  AE_CHAR_IMAGE_ACTION  AE_GALLERY_INDEX_REQ
//   AE_IMG_META_REQ  AE_IMG_REGEN  AE_GALLERY_ACTION  CLICK_GENERATE_NOW
// Bridge → engine (handleBridgeMessage has a case for each):
//   HANDSHAKE_ACK  AE_CHARCHAT_ACTION  AE_CHAR_IMAGE_UPLOADED  AE_GALLERY_INDEX_RESP
//   AE_IMG_EDIT_OPEN  AE_IMG_META_RESP  AE_IMG_REGEN_DONE  AE_SET_PLAYER_FLAVOR
//   AE_STORY_IMG_DROP  AE_CHAR_IMAGE_META
// AE_CHAR_IMAGE_ACTION ops: delete / download / upload / meta ('meta' answers on
// AE_CHAR_IMAGE_META). Console-only, no bridge sender by design:
//   AE_DIAG_CMD → aeHandleDiagCommand, replies on AE_DIAG_REPLY.

const EVT_GENERATE_IMAGE = 'GENERATE_IMAGE';           // image payload — bridge generates and displays. SEND VIA ceBroadcastImage.
const EVT_STORY_ID_SYNC  = 'STORY_ID_SYNC';            // current story ID after handshake; scopes the bridge gallery
const EVT_HANDSHAKE_REQ  = 'HANDSHAKE_REQUEST';        // engine announces its script ID to bridge
const EVT_HANDSHAKE_ACK  = 'HANDSHAKE_ACK';            // bridge confirms connection
const AE_DIAG_CMD        = 'AE_DIAG_CMD';              // bridge→engine: run a /log, /diag or /ce command
const EVT_DIAG_REPLY     = 'AE_DIAG_REPLY';            // engine→bridge: command output (always shown, not log-gated)
const EVT_CHARCHAT_STATE = 'AE_CHARCHAT_STATE';        // engine→bridge: { active, contactId, contactName, ... }
const EVT_ENGINE_THINKING = 'AE_ENGINE_THINKING';      // engine→bridge: { on } — drives the generation indicator


